{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14379","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14379","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14379","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giorgio","familyName":"Mentasti","name":"Mentasti, Giorgio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leon","familyName":"Vidal","name":"Vidal, Leon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Quentin","familyName":"Baghi","name":"Baghi, Quentin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carlo","familyName":"Contaldi","name":"Contaldi, Carlo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Sachs-Wolfe effect as a smoking gun for cosmological gravitational wave backgrounds"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T12:13:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-26T00:51:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The Sachs-Wolfe (SW) effect, arising from large-scale structures in the universe, modifies the frequencies of gravitational waves (GWs) sourced by a cosmological background. We show that for backgrounds with $Ω_{\\rm GW}\\gtrsim 10^{-10}$, this effect imprints anisotropies and spectral distortions that can be detectable with a network of space-based interferometers (such as LISA + Taiji) and, if not taken into account, may bias the estimate of the theoretical model of the GW background. The effect is particularly enhanced in the high-frequency end of the spectrum. The SW-induced anisotropies and spectral distortions present in a GW background sourced at primordial times will correlate with the SW signature present in the CMB. Any detection of a cross-correlation between the GW anisotropies and the CMB at large scales is therefore a smoking gun for confirming the primordial nature of the background."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14379","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:53:26Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:53:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-26T02:36:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14006","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14006","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14006","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joao P. A.","familyName":"Dantas","name":"Dantas, Joao P. A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paulo F. Silva","familyName":"Filho","name":"Filho, Paulo F. Silva","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jelton A.","familyName":"Cunha","name":"Cunha, Jelton A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gabriel","familyName":"Dietzsch","name":"Dietzsch, Gabriel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HARBOR: Heading Analysis and Reconstruction from Behavioral Observation and Radar"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Emerging Technologies (cs.ET)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T00:57:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-26T01:11:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.2514/6.2026-4783","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Maritime situational awareness often relies on Automatic Identification System (AIS) transmissions to track vessel movements. However, in operational or conflict scenarios, these data may be unavailable due to signal loss, deliberate deactivation, or intentional spoofing. In such conditions, synthetic aperture radar (SAR) imagery becomes a critical sensing alternative for wide-area maritime monitoring, despite providing only static scene snapshots. This work introduces HARBOR (Heading Analysis and Reconstruction from Behavioral Observation and Radar), a complete pipeline for transforming a single SAR image into predictive motion information without requiring any auxiliary data source at inference time. The method begins with SAR image preprocessing to enhance and segment vessel candidates, followed by automatic detection, size-based classification, and heading estimation using skeleton geometry and local intensity patterns. AIS data are used exclusively during an offline calibration phase to derive vessel-type-dependent motion parameters, which are then applied to generate probabilistic heatmaps of candidate future vessel positions. A case study using real COSMO-SkyMed SAR imagery demonstrates the pipeline on a maritime scene in southern Brazil, showing its ability to extract motion tendencies and generate probabilistic projections of vessel positions in data-denied environments."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14006","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:45:32Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:45:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-26T02:36:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14093","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14093","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14093","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gilde Valeria","familyName":"Rodríguez","name":"Rodríguez, Gilde Valeria","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Armando","familyName":"Castañeda","name":"Castañeda, Armando","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miguel","familyName":"Piña","name":"Piña, Miguel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On the Limits of Causal Observation in Shared-Memory Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T04:16:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:25:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-21T22:19:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-23T00:16:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reasoning about concurrent system correctness requires detecting real-time operation precedence (\u0026lt;_H). In asynchronous shared-memory systems, real-time precedence and information-flow causality diverge, rendering causality-tracking techniques like vector clocks incapable of recovering \u0026lt;_H. An external monitor observing only operation boundaries faces an inherent limitation: it must either miss genuine precedences or report spurious ones. We formalize this challenge as the Causal Observability Problem (COP) and prove that strong consistency---achieving both soundness and completeness simultaneously---is unachievable at the observable boundary. We then establish a tight placement dichotomy: instrumenting externally guarantees soundness, whereas instrumenting internally guarantees completeness, independently of the timestamping algorithm. To construct sound monitors, we analyze three non-blocking implementations: FAInc (a centralized counter), Striped (a decentralized counter), and Collect (an iterative process snapshot). We prove that both FAInc and Striped are linearizable, whereas Collect fails all standard object consistency models---satisfying neither linearizability, sequential consistency, nor quiescent consistency. Crucially, we prove an invariance result: all three implementations provide identical COP soundness guarantees under external placement. This demonstrates a fundamental theoretical decoupling---observational soundness operates on pairwise interval precedence and does not require the global sequential witness that standard object consistency demands. Empirical evaluation on a 64-core NUMA system shows that Striped matches the high throughput and scalability of Collect while preserving linearizability and eliminating central counter contention."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24 pages, 2 figures, full version, SSS 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14093","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:47:21Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:47:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-23T02:54:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14504","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14504","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14504","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Brian"],"givenName":"Qinlin","familyName":"He","name":"He, Qinlin","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Brian"],"givenName":"Zeming","familyName":"Zhuang","name":"Zhuang, Zeming","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Brian"],"givenName":"Yongji","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yongji","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Brian"],"givenName":"Lan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Lan","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Brian"],"familyName":"Xiaoyong","name":"Xiaoyong","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Organizational","name":"Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Scratched Lenses, Shifted Depth: Passive Camera-Side Optical Attacks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T14:34:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:52:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-20T22:08:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-22T00:16:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.14722/ndss.2027.231849","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Physical adversarial attacks on vision systems are typically studied via scene manipulation, such as adversarial patches or projections. A smaller body of work has examined camera-side manipulations, such as camera stickers and auxiliary optics, which introduce consistent artifacts in the captured image. However, these approaches treat the attack as an image-space perturbation produced by designed transmissive patterns, without explicitly modeling how physical imperfections interact with scene-dependent lighting and optics. In contrast, we identify a distinct and underexplored threat: passive lens-side damage that is persistent yet trigger-conditioned, producing scene-dependent optical artifacts that bias geometric inference. We instantiate this threat through Scratch-induced Lens Adversarial Streak Hijacking (SLASH), a physical-world attack caused by small scratches on a camera lens or protective cover. These scratches interact with bright point-like light sources and specular reflections to generate structured optical artifacts that systematically distort depth cues. We formulate the attack in optical space rather than pixel space, modeling the scratch pattern as a trigger-conditioned optical channel, and using the same scratch pattern across diverse viewing conditions. We evaluate SLASH in both digital and real-world settings on monocular depth estimation and monocular 3D object detection. On the nuScenes dataset, SLASH causes monocular depth models to estimate target vehicles as up to 32% closer to the camera or 36% farther away, with consistent effects extending to monocular 3D object detection. Physical experiments with both acrylic and glass covers confirm that the attack produces substantial depth errors on real camera inputs. These findings reveal a new attack surface in which benign-looking hardware imperfections act as latent, scene-triggered adversarial mechanisms."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages. Accepted at NDSS 2027"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14504","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-15T01:56:06Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:56:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-22T03:04:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13802","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13802","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13802","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tejas","familyName":"Agrawal","name":"Agrawal, Tejas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vu","familyName":"Le","name":"Le, Vu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sumit","familyName":"Gulwani","name":"Gulwani, Sumit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gust","familyName":"Verbruggen","name":"Verbruggen, Gust","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Benchmark and Framework for Evaluating Next Action Predictions in Spreadsheets"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.6; H.4.1","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T18:16:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-22T01:03:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Predictive code completion greatly accelerates how quickly developers work. In spreadsheets, despite being much more common, such auto-completion features are virtually non-existent. To address this gap, we introduce a benchmark for systems that observe a sequence of user actions in a spreadsheet and predict future actions. Two challenges are (1) the absence of edit histories in public spreadsheet corpora and (2) the complex space of spreadsheet actions (spatial, temporal, composite). To address (1), we manually curate 52 sequences of 12K actions that recreate spreadsheets from public corpora, seeded by parametrized heuristics and LLM refinement. To address (2), we propose an online evaluation that expects a prediction after each user action, accepts or rejects that prediction, updates the future actions upon acceptance, and repeats this until the target spreadsheet is obtained. We use multiple baseline predictors (including zero-shot LLMs, fine-tuned SLMs, and classical models) and analyze different properties that our benchmark teaches us, including but not limited to: properties of saved actions and false positives, efficiency, effect of user profiles, effect of triggers, and effect of context."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ICML 2026. Code and benchmark: https://github.com/Tej-55/NAPE"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13802","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:41:10Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:41:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-22T03:04:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13769","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13769","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13769","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seungjae","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Seungjae","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yoonkyo","familyName":"Jung","name":"Jung, Yoonkyo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jusuk","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jusuk","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonghun","familyName":"Shin","name":"Shin, Jonghun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amir Hossein","familyName":"Shahidzadeh","name":"Shahidzadeh, Amir Hossein","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yao-Chih","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Yao-Chih","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"H. Jin","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, H. Jin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jia-Bin","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Jia-Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Furong","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Furong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"$μ_0$: A Scalable 3D Interaction-Trace World Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T17:59:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:04:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T06:13:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-16T01:23:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-20T18:48:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-22T00:06:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"World models that capture how actions induce physical change enable scalable robot learning without reliance on embodiment-specific action labels. Pixel-space video models provide broad visual priors but expend model capacity on dense appearance reconstruction, while direct action models require embodiment-specific labels that hinder scalability. We present $μ_0$, a scalable world model based on 3D traces. Rather than predicting dense pixels or directly modeling actions, $μ_0$ forecasts smooth 3D trajectories for salient interaction points such as objects, tools, hands, and contact regions, yielding a compact, embodiment-agnostic motion interface. To enable training from diverse video sources, our TraceExtract system automatically extracts 3D supervision by selecting keypoints, constructing globally aligned traces, and associating motion segments with hierarchical language captions. This TraceExtract supervision pretrains $μ_0$ by combining a pretrained vision-language backbone with a modular trace expert, which represents each query via B-spline control points and predicts future traces. Experiments show that $μ_0$ outperforms baselines in both 2D and 3D trace prediction, including trace prediction models and tokenized VLM methods. Because $μ_0$ is frozen and reusable, it can be paired with action experts for downstream robot embodiments. Despite action-free pretraining, the resulting trace-conditioned policies achieve performance competitive with VLA models pretrained with action supervision, such as $π_0$. These results establish 3D traces as a scalable and transferable representation for cross-embodiment manipulation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Comments: Accepted to CoRL 2026. Project Page: https://mu0-wm.github.io/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13769","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:40:29Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:40:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-22T03:04:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14651","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14651","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14651","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jessica","familyName":"Metzger","name":"Metzger, Jessica","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yariv","familyName":"Kafri","name":"Kafri, Yariv","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mehran","familyName":"Kardar","name":"Kardar, Mehran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Julien","familyName":"Tailleur","name":"Tailleur, Julien","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Generic long-range correlations in conserved nonequilibrium mixtures"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Statistical Mechanics (cond-mat.stat-mech)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Soft Condensed Matter (cond-mat.soft)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T17:12:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T01:00:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-19T20:50:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-21T00:15:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study correlation functions in conserved nonequilibrium mixtures, including multi-temperature systems and non-reciprocal field theories. The corresponding linear theory is short-ranged, and nonlinearities are irrelevant in the renormalization-group sense. Nonetheless, we find that these nonlinearities generate long-ranged three-point correlations in the isotropic disordered phase. Our analytical predictions, which are based on a phenomenological theory, are confirmed by numerical simulations of Brownian colloids in contact with thermal baths at different temperatures. Dangerously irrelevant nonlinearities in nonequilibrium mixtures thus offer a new route to long-range correlations, supporting the hypothesis that such correlations are not the exception but the rule out of equilibrium."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages, 2 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14651","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:59:10Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:59:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-21T03:18:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14403","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14403","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14403","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxiang","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Jiaxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Laura","familyName":"Nazzaro","name":"Nazzaro, Laura","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmed Aziz","familyName":"Ezzat","name":"Ezzat, Ahmed Aziz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Deep Zero-Inflated Model of North Atlantic Right Whale Presence To Support Blue Economy Management"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Applications (stat.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T12:40:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:46:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-17T15:03:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-18T01:43:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-27T20:08:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-31T00:08:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-19T23:24:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-10-21T00:23:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Effective modeling of endangered marine mammal species, such as the North Atlantic Right Whale, is critical for balancing marine conservation with the growing blue economy. Passive acoustic monitoring data collected by autonomous underwater vehicles provide new opportunities for localized marine species detection and oceanographic sensing, but introduce complex statistical challenges such as zero inflation, imperfect detection, and intricate dependence structures. In response, we propose the Deep Zero-Inflated Bernoulli (DeepZIB) model, a deep statistical method which jointly models latent species presence and conditional detection probabilities while learning complex habitat relationships from heterogeneous covariate information. We establish theoretical results on the model's structural properties and conduct simulation experiments to demonstrate its ability to recover underlying parameters and latent presence fields. Application to real-world passive acoustic monitoring data on the North Atlantic Right Whale along the U.S. East Coast demonstrates improved model adequacy and predictive performance in capturing the species' dynamic and spatially varying habitat. We demonstrate DeepZIB's ability to generate high-resolution, spatially and temporally varying presence maps, providing valuable insights for targeted and risk-aware management of blue economy industries, ranging from offshore and marine energy, to fisheries management and maritime transport"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14403","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:53:57Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:53:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-21T03:18:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14630","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14630","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14630","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rishi","familyName":"Das","name":"Das, Rishi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harsha","familyName":"Hutridurga","name":"Hutridurga, Harsha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amiya K.","familyName":"Pani","name":"Pani, Amiya K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ricardo","familyName":"Ruiz-Baier","name":"Ruiz-Baier, Ricardo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Robust mixed methods for coupled Darcy--Forchheimer--transport problems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Numerical Analysis (math.NA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"65N30, 65N12, 65N15","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T16:57:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:59:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-20T09:18:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-21T00:57:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We adapt the recent theory for unique solvability of perturbed saddle-point problems in Banach spaces to the case of parameter-independent stability bounds. This also constitutes an extension to the Brezzi--Braess theory for parameter-robust stability of perturbed saddle-point problems from the Hilbert to the Banach setting. We apply the new abstract result to the mixed formulation of the advection-diffusion-reaction equation and tackle also its coupling with the Darcy--Forchheimer equations. The complete system is shown to be stable irrespective of the model parameters of permeability, Forchheimer coefficient, and reaction modulation. We discretise the problem with mixed finite element methods and show convergence in appropriately parameter-weighted norms. We also design operator-based preconditioners for the full system, utilising weighted norms that provide robustness with respect to some model parameters. We also present a few numerical examples that show the convergence and robustness of the proposed method in some regimes of practical interest."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14630","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:58:43Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:58:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-21T03:18:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14549","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14549","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14549","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anson","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Anson","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Searching for Extra Dimensions with Gravitational Waves: Dark-Siren Constraints from GWTC-4"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T15:28:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:55:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-19T02:29:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-20T01:44:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Higher-dimensional theories of gravity predict that gravitational waves (GWs) can propagate into extra spatial dimensions, leading to modified amplitude damping over cosmological distances. Measurements of GW luminosity distances therefore provide a unique probe of the dimensionality of spacetime. In this work, we constrain higher-dimensional GW propagation using the dark-siren method with the Gravitational-Wave Transient Catalog 4.0 (GWTC-4). We adopt a phenomenological parameterization motivated by braneworld scenarios, in which deviations from General Relativity are characterized by the spacetime dimension number $D$ and a crossover scale $R_c$ governing the transition between four- and higher-dimensional gravity. We perform a hierarchical Bayesian analysis combining 141 compact binary coalescences from GWTC-4 with line-of-sight galaxy information from the GLADE+ catalog. For a prior $H_0 \\in [65,77]\\ {\\rm km~s^{-1}Mpc^{-1}}$ and $\\log(R_c/{\\rm Mpc}) \\in [2.7,4.0]$, we obtain $D = 4.38^{+1.91}_{-1.01}$ (68\\% credible interval). We also find that the inferred posterior distribution of $R_c$ accumulates near the upper prior boundary, indicating that the crossover scale remains poorly constrained by current observations. We further show that the inferred constraint on $D$ depends sensitively on the assumed prior range of $R_c$, which determines the characteristic distance scale at which deviations from General Relativity become significant. Our results provide the first GWTC-4 dark-siren constraints on higher-dimensional GW propagation and demonstrate that current observations remain consistent with four-dimensional General Relativity."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages, 8 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14549","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:57:02Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:57:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-20T05:24:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13950","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13950","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13950","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vincenzo","familyName":"Fiorentini","name":"Fiorentini, Vincenzo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Oxygen deficiency and valency reconstruction in multiferroic V-doped HfO$_2$"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T22:30:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:14:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-28T11:05:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-20T01:36:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1103/dfcj-rb7w","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The interplay of oxygen deficiency and vanadium multiple valency in the candidate multiferroic V-doped $Pca2_1$ hafnia HfO$_2$ is studied by first-principles calculations. Low-lying V majority gap states accept electrons from oxygen-vacancy donors, reducing their formation energy, and converting nominal V$^{4+}$ centers into V$^{3+}$. The resulting local magnetization and screening changes are reflected in the calculated V core-level shifts, which are consistent with the experimentally observed XPS signatures. The calculated V$^{3+}$/V$^{4+}$ population ratio determined by oxygen vacancies only matches experiment in reducing conditions, suggesting that additional electron reservoirs may contribute under ALD growth conditions. A similar scenario also seems to apply to the recently observed multiferroicity in Cr-doped hafnia, where oxygen deficiency is intrinsic to the growth technique."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages, 8 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13950","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-15T01:44:21Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:44:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-20T05:24:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14133","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14133","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14133","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Denis S.","familyName":"Grebenkov","name":"Grebenkov, Denis S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Diffusion-Driven Surface Autocatalysis in Exterior Domains"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Statistical Mechanics (cond-mat.stat-mech)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Chemical Physics (physics.chem-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T05:45:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:28:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-19T17:13:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-20T02:42:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study the collective dynamics of independent particles that diffuse outside a spherical surface, on which they are replicated with a prescribed catalytic rate. In spatial dimensions three and higher, the transient nature of diffusion creates a competition between autocatalytic branching and escape to infinity, leading to three distinct regimes: subcritical (finite limiting population), critical (algebraic growth), and supercritical (exponential growth). The rotational symmetry of the domain and an explicit form of the single-particle diffusion propagator allow us to fully investigate the time evolution of the population size, including the mean population size, variance, higher-order moments, as well as the whole distribution. In particular, we obtain an explicit form of the distribution at long times and describe a slow, power-law approach to this steady-state limit. We further extend the steady-state analysis to general exterior domains using the Dirichlet-to-Neumann operator, which determines the critical catalytic rate and reveals how boundary geometry affects the population-size distribution."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14133","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:48:13Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:48:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-20T05:24:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13881","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13881","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13881","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sandeep","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Sandeep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Betchov-Type Hydrodynamic Formulation of the Ivancevic Option-Pricing Equation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Mathematical Physics (math-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"35Q55, 35C08, 91G20","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T20:17:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:10:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-16T18:46:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-20T00:07:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1016/j.chaos.2026.119261","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We show that, under constant-coefficient assumptions, the Ivancevic option-pricing nonlinear Schrödinger equation admits a Betchov-type hydrodynamic formulation analogous to the one arising in the context of the vortex filament equation. Along with this structural correspondence, known Ivancevic solutions are interpreted in terms of density--velocity variables. As an illustration of the financial interpretation, we compare the normalized one-soliton density with the Black--Scholes risk-neutral density, obtaining an explicit relation between the Ivancevic coefficient ratio and standard market quantities. We further show that the probability current can be reconstructed from the temporal evolution of the density and demonstrate this reconstruction for an interacting two-soliton solution. Together, these results illustrate how the Betchov-type hydrodynamic formulation can be used to describe the evolution and transport of the Ivancevic density, while establishing a coefficient-explicit bridge between nonlinear wave models in mathematical finance and geometric fluid mechanics."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13881","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-15T01:42:54Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:42:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-20T05:24:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13728","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13728","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13728","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shubham","familyName":"Kejriwal","name":"Kejriwal, Shubham","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SPLIT: Loosely-coherent inference of long-inspiral gravitational-wave sources for robustness against modeling and data outliers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Astrophysical Phenomena (astro-ph.HE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Instrumentation and Methods for Astrophysics (astro-ph.IM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T09:39:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-16T01:27:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-18T06:51:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-20T01:09:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The Laser Interferometer Space Antenna (LISA) will detect gravitational waves (GWs) from dozens of extreme- and intermediate-mass-ratio inspirals (EMRIs/IMRIs). These sources will stay in-band for months to years, offering extraordinary scientific potential. However, their fully phase-coherent analysis standard in current pipelines imposes stringent waveform accuracy requirements; failing to model the signal over such long durations can result in significant systematic biases. To address this, we formulate a robust Bayesian inference framework that segments the data into blocks, infers each block coherently, and recombines the results using loosely-coherent phase information. By restricting full phase-tracking to much shorter block durations, this approach prevents significant accumulation of phase errors. We implement this methodology in \\textsc{SPLIT} (Segmented Posteriors for Long-Inspiral Templates), a GPU-accelerated Python package. Applying \\textsc{SPLIT} to an environment-rich injection, we demonstrate that while a fully-coherent vacuum-GR analysis incurs a maximum 1D systematic bias of $\\approx 4.8σ$ from the truth, the shorter integration window of our loosely-coherent approach restricts such biases to $\\lesssim 0.5σ$. Overall, despite a fractional loss of optimal signal-to-noise ratio, the substantial improvement in parameter accuracy offered by our method presents a highly advantageous trade-off for LISA and other future GW detectors."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"before proofs accepted; 28 pages, 8 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13728","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:39:36Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:39:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-20T05:24:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13794","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13794","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13794","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Umut","familyName":"Demir","name":"Demir, Umut","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aamir","familyName":"Ahmad","name":"Ahmad, Aamir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Torbjørn","familyName":"Cunis","name":"Cunis, Torbjørn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Walter","familyName":"Fichter","name":"Fichter, Walter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"An integrated interpretable control effectiveness learning and nonlinear control allocation methodology for overactuated aircraft"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T18:04:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:05:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-18T11:10:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-19T00:43:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-17T14:51:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-20T00:50:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Nonlinear dynamics and the strong couplings that arise between multiple effectors undermine the assumptions behind conventional, linear control allocation techniques. When flight enters regimes where nonlinear effects dominate, linear allocators exhibit reduced accuracy due to increased model mismatch, which subsequently degrades performance and robustness of the flight control system. High fidelity onboard models and black box data driven approaches can recover accuracy across the flight envelope, but respectively impose computational burdens prohibitive for real time allocation and sacrifice the interpretability required for verification and fault diagnosis. This paper addresses these limitations by learning an explicit, physics constrained analytical model of the control effectiveness mapping from representative flight data using Sparse Identification of Nonlinear Dynamics. The resulting mapping is compact, interpretable, and admits analytical derivatives, enabling efficient computation within nonlinear solvers that additionally incorporate actuator dynamics, without requiring an onboard model. An online adaptation mechanism monitors prediction residuals and refreshes the model when significant plant changes are detected, providing graceful reconfiguration under actuator failures and varying operating conditions. The methodology is evaluated on a high fidelity nonlinear benchmark aircraft across a range of aggressive maneuvers, achieving accuracy comparable to a full nonlinear onboard model while substantially reducing computational cost relative to established baselines."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13794","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:41:00Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:41:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-20T05:24:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14685","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14685","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14685","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandra","familyName":"Mestre-Torà","name":"Mestre-Torà, Alexandra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marta","familyName":"Perego","name":"Perego, Marta","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Clara Galante","familyName":"Agero","name":"Agero, Clara Galante","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takashi","familyName":"Taniguchi","name":"Taniguchi, Takashi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kenji","familyName":"Watanabe","name":"Watanabe, Kenji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Ihn","name":"Ihn, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Klaus","familyName":"Ensslin","name":"Ensslin, Klaus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Artem O.","familyName":"Denisov","name":"Denisov, Artem O.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Fully gapped superconductivity in magic-angle graphene Cooper-pair islands"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Mesoscale and Nanoscale Physics (cond-mat.mes-hall)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T17:48:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T01:02:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-16T07:39:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-19T00:43:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The mechanism behind superconductivity in magic-angle graphene systems remains an open question. Recent tunnelling and superfluid stiffness measurements have suggested a nodal superconducting gap, but these probes are typically applied to large-area devices, where twist-angle inhomogeneity and disorder can hinder resolving the intrinsic superconducting spectrum. Here we introduce quantum dots in magic-angle twisted bilayer graphene and access a regime where gate-tunable superconductivity competes with strong Coulomb repulsion upon adding an additional carrier to the island. The islands exhibit $2e$-periodic Coulomb blockade, revealing a well-defined quasiparticle gap in the island spectrum, inconsistent with the presence of nodes in the order parameter. Within the same device, we also realize coupled superconducting islands and proximitized quantum dots, establishing a monolithic platform for hybrid dot devices. Our results point to a nodeless quasiparticle gap in moiré graphene and demonstrate the potential of mesoscopic hybrid structures to probe their correlated phases."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14685","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:59:53Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:59:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-19T02:57:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14338","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14338","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14338","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cristiano Luigi Kosman","familyName":"Chiarappa","name":"Chiarappa, Cristiano Luigi Kosman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pietro","familyName":"Bovini","name":"Bovini, Pietro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pierbiagio","familyName":"Pieri","name":"Pieri, Pierbiagio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mass-imbalanced two-dimensional Bose-Fermi mixtures with boson-fermion pairing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Gases (cond-mat.quant-gas)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T10:49:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:42:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T14:06:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-18T00:55:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-16T14:19:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-19T01:10:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1103/gy5f-fg91","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We analyze a two-dimensional Bose-Fermi mixture at zero temperature in the presence of a tunable Bose-Fermi attraction. We adopt a diagrammatic T-matrix approach and study the behavior of several thermodynamic quantities for the two species as functions of density, mass ratio, and coupling strength. These include the chemical potentials, the boson momentum distribution function, the condensate density, and Tan's contact parameter. We analytically demonstrate that the present T-matrix formalism recovers the correct second-order perturbative expansion of the chemical potentials in the weak-coupling regime, and test it numerically. The near-universal behavior of the condensate fraction already found in prior work for the mass-balanced case is confirmed for different masses and becomes even more accurate when the boson mass is large. The mass imbalance emerges as an additional control parameter that qualitatively affects the bosonic momentum distribution. In particular, we found that it can be used to allow for the experimental observation of a peculiar peak in the boson momentum distribution at finite momentum."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 15 figures, accepted version, with minor changes with respect to v2"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14338","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-15T01:52:35Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:52:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-19T02:57:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13714","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13714","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13714","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Duc","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Duc","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sieu","familyName":"Tran","name":"Tran, Sieu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Vo","name":"Vo, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Khoa","familyName":"Vo","name":"Vo, Khoa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Duy Minh Ho","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Duy Minh Ho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nghi D. Q.","familyName":"Bui","name":"Bui, Nghi D. Q.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anh","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Anh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Long","familyName":"Mai","name":"Mai, Long","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ngan","familyName":"Le","name":"Le, Ngan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TSA: Temporal Slot Activation for Persistent Object-Centric Video Representation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-10T20:54:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:00:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-19T17:52:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-23T01:02:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-27T19:26:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-29T00:08:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-16T02:28:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-10-19T00:29:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Unsupervised video object-centric learning aims to decompose dynamic scenes into temporally persistent entity representations. Existing recurrent video slot-attention methods propagate a fixed set of slots across frames, but typically assume unconditional slot propagation: every slot is updated and decoded at every frame, regardless of whether its corresponding object is visible. We show that this design violates a basic lifecycle requirement for persistent slots: when an object is absent or fully occluded, its slot should preserve its previous state and avoid explaining unrelated visible content. Instead, unconditional propagation creates update-induced state drift, where current-frame evidence overwrites the absent object's representation, and decoder-induced reconstruction interference, where the inactive slot remains coupled to reconstruction through decoder attention. We propose Temporal Slot Activation (TSA), a lightweight mechanism that learns a per-slot, per-frame activation score $α_{k,t}\\in(0,1)$ without visibility supervision. TSA uses this activation as a shared latent control variable for slot lifecycle modeling. When a slot is inactive, TSA anchors its state to the previous slot via activation-gated updating and suppresses its decoder participation through an activation-dependent additive bias on attention logits before softmax normalization. This jointly reduces state drift and reconstruction-driven interference. To improve decisions under partial occlusion and gradual reappearance, TSA further conditions activation prediction on a per-slot temporal memory produced by a Temporal Context Encoder. We evaluate TSA on MOVi-C, MOVi-E, YTVIS-HQ, and the occlusion-heavy OVIS benchmarks, using standard (FG-ARI, mBO) and tracking-based (IDF1, HOTA) metrics. TSA improves object decomposition and temporal identity preservation, with large gains on long, heavily occluded videos."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13714","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:39:19Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:39:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-19T02:57:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14532","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14532","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14532","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lars Sickert","familyName":"Karam","name":"Karam, Lars Sickert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui M.","familyName":"Castro","name":"Castro, Rui M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maarten","familyName":"Schoukens","name":"Schoukens, Maarten","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessandro","familyName":"Corbetta","name":"Corbetta, Alessandro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Modeling inhomogeneous spatial point configurations with applications to replicated patterns in real-life waiting crowds"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Physics and Society (physics.soc-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"62M30, 62H11, 62P35","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T15:10:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:54:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-15T11:34:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-16T01:07:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this article, we connect statistical inference for spatial point processes with the analysis of waiting pedestrian crowds through two interconnected contributions. First, on the methodological side we develop an inference procedure for semiparametric spatial point process models leveraging replicated spatial patterns, i.e., multiple approximately independent realizations from the same process. Second, we show that spatial point processes provide a suitable modeling framework for waiting pedestrians, capturing two key aspects: spatial inhomogeneity driven by location attractiveness and repulsive interactions between pedestrians. These two components are central to the inference problem itself, since spatial point process modeling hinges on disentangling background intensity from interaction. Although replicated spatial patterns are rare in point process literature, they are available here through a unique real-life pedestrian dataset, thereby directly linking the methodological development to the physical application. We use the proposed methods to fit and evaluate determinantal and Gibbs point processes in a simulation study and a real-world case study. Despite persistent challenges in decoupling the influences of inhomogeneity from interaction, these models are able to reproduce key empirical features of waiting pedestrians."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"39 pages, 29 figures; v2: Revised following peer review. Unified inference regions; new comparison figures; expanded literature review; links to public code and data; minor bug fixes and corrected typos; updated title"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14532","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:56:41Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:56:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-16T03:20:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14535","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14535","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14535","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seoyoon","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Seoyoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kanghyun","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Kanghyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongwoo","familyName":"Ko","name":"Ko, Dongwoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yeong Jin","familyName":"Heo","name":"Heo, Yeong Jin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Min Jun","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Min Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Spatially Conditioned Diffusion Policy: Learning Precise and Robust Manipulation with a Single RGB Camera"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T15:12:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:54:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-15T12:21:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-16T01:10:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent visual imitation learning systems have widely adopted multi-camera setups with wrist-mounted cameras as the de facto standard. However, manipulation from a single global view remains challenging, as the policy should capture fine-grained interaction details and identify task-relevant regions without local wrist views. To address this challenge, we present Spatially Conditioned Diffusion Policy (SCDP), a diffusion-based visuomotor policy that achieves precise and robust manipulation in a single-camera setting. Our key idea is that end-effector trajectories can serve as visual attention anchors that reflect task-relevant regions. Building on this idea, SCDP consists of two key components: (i) a visual encoder that produces multi-scale feature maps to capture both broader context and fine-grained visual features, and (ii) a spatial conditioning module that samples point-wise features along intermediate end-effector trajectories in the diffusion loop. Extensive simulation experiments show that SCDP consistently outperforms strong single-view baselines and achieves performance comparable to multi-camera baselines. Real-world experiments further demonstrate precise manipulation and robustness to visual distractors, highlighting the potential of single-camera imitation learning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at CoRL 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14535","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:56:44Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:56:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-16T03:20:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14459","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14459","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14459","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Theresa Pekarek","familyName":"Rosin","name":"Rosin, Theresa Pekarek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthias","familyName":"Kerzel","name":"Kerzel, Matthias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefan","familyName":"Wermter","name":"Wermter, Stefan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MoDiCoL: A Modular Diagnostic Continual Learning Dataset for Robust Speech Recognition"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T13:50:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-16T01:01:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.21437/interspeech.2026-2111","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Modern Automatic Speech Recognition (ASR) systems have made remarkable progress on standard benchmarks, yet performance gaps have emerged under real-world distribution shifts, caused by recording conditions, accents, speech impairments, and noise. Existing datasets and benchmarks typically isolate these factors, which overlooks their co-occurrence in real-world applications. In this paper, we argue that model robustness can be treated as a dynamic capability that continually develops, and we introduce MoDiCoL, a Modular Diagnostic Continual Learning dataset designed for controlled analysis of linguistic content, speaker characteristics, and acoustic environments. Furthermore, we propose a real-world-inspired continual learning curriculum to simulate incremental updates and study how robustness is acquired, transferred, and forgotten. We evaluate three continual learning strategies and provide detailed insights into robustness under evolving conditions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at Interspeech 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14459","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-15T01:55:07Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:55:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-16T03:20:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14451","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14451","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14451","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joachim","familyName":"Viseur","name":"Viseur, Joachim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Claude","familyName":"Semay","name":"Semay, Claude","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cyrille","familyName":"Chevalier","name":"Chevalier, Cyrille","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Light hybrid baryons in the constituent model of QCD"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Phenomenology (hep-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T13:35:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:49:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-18T07:36:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-19T00:30:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-15T13:32:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-16T01:15:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1103/psxp-zlfl","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Hybrid baryons, in which gluonic degrees of freedom play an explicit dynamical role, provide a key testing ground for nonperturbative quantum chromodynamics. In this work, we investigate the mass spectrum of light hybrid baryons composed of identical quarks within a phenomenological constituent framework, applied to a quark core-gluon approximation. In this approach, the hybrid baryon is described as a bound state of a color-octet three-quark core and a constituent gluon, allowing the original four-body problem to be reduced to a three-body calculation followed by an effective two-body treatment. The spectrum of the color-octet quark core is obtained by solving a semirelativistic three-quark Hamiltonian with linear confinement, Coulomb, and regularized hyperfine interactions using an oscillator basis expansion. Finite-size effects of the core are incorporated through the convolution of the effective core-gluon interaction with the spatial quark density. The resulting two-body problem, whose associated Hamiltonian has the same shape as the one of the core, is solved applying the helicity formalism and using the Lagrange mesh method. Our results predict the lightest hybrid baryons to occur at energies above $3~\\mathrm{GeV}$, with negative-parity states generally lying below their positive-parity counterparts. The predicted spectra are compared with lattice QCD and QCD sum-rule calculations, showing qualitative agreement although the lowest-lying lattice QCD results are significantly lighter than the present ones. Possible extensions of the model and implications for future experimental searches are discussed."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14451","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-15T01:54:57Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:54:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-16T03:20:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14391","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14391","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14391","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Henri-Leon","familyName":"Kordt","name":"Kordt, Henri-Leon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Theresa Pekarek","familyName":"Rosin","name":"Rosin, Theresa Pekarek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jae Hee","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jae Hee","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefan","familyName":"Wermter","name":"Wermter, Stefan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning to Hear Hesitation: Continual Learning for Disfluency-Aware ASR"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T12:25:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-16T01:01:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.21437/interspeech.2026-2080","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite advances in large-scale Automatic Speech Recognition (ASR), disfluent speech remains challenging, as state-of-the-art systems are often optimized to omit disfluencies, leading to information loss and hallucinations. Prior work has focused on verbatim transcription and the integration of disfluency markers, but adapting models on limited datasets can lead to catastrophic forgetting of general-domain knowledge. We address this gap by leveraging continual learning (CL) with explicit disfluency tokens. We first introduce these tokens into a pretrained ASR model to establish stable token mechanisms, and then continue training on additional datasets with varying disfluency distributions. Through a detailed analysis of model dynamics during training, we identify a trade-off between marker learning and ASR performance, and a consistent cross-attention head mechanism shared across CL methods."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at Interspeech 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14391","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-15T01:53:42Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:53:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-16T03:20:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14263","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14263","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14263","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adrien","familyName":"Blanchet","name":"Blanchet, Adrien","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"César","familyName":"Jesús-Valls","name":"Jesús-Valls, César","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Animesh","familyName":"Chatterjee","name":"Chatterjee, Animesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stephen","familyName":"Dolan","name":"Dolan, Stephen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pierre","familyName":"Granger","name":"Granger, Pierre","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Laura","familyName":"Munteanu","name":"Munteanu, Laura","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Novel Approach to Short Baseline Oscillation Searches Using Neutrino Tagging with nuSCOPE"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Experiment (hep-ex)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Phenomenology (hep-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T08:46:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:36:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-15T07:55:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-16T00:49:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present the first study of short-baseline neutrino oscillation searches using a tagged neutrino beamline, taking the proposed nuSCOPE facility at CERN as a benchmark. In this Letter we demonstrate that tagged neutrino beams, where the neutrino flavor, energy, and propagation distance are determined with exceptional event-by-event precision, provide a new experimental approach to search for non-standard neutrino oscillations. We evaluate the sensitivity to sterile-neutrino-induced oscillations in the $ν_μ$ disappearance, $ν_μ\\rightarrow ν_e$ appearance, and $ν_e$ disappearance channels, demonstrating the ability to probe multiple flavor transitions and both appearance and disappearance modes within a single experiment. Our results show that tagged beams enable sensitivity improvements to mass-squared splittings spanning several orders of magnitude while substantially reducing the dependence on neutrino flux predictions that limits conventional searches. We find that nuSCOPE can probe a broad region of parameter space motivated by existing anomalies and extend coverage into previously unexplored territory, demonstrating the strong potential of tagged neutrino beams for precision oscillation physics."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14263","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:50:58Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:50:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-16T03:20:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14118","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14118","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14118","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Debsundar","familyName":"Dey","name":"Dey, Debsundar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhirup","familyName":"Patra","name":"Patra, Abhirup","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anand Narayanan","familyName":"Krishnamoorthy","name":"Krishnamoorthy, Anand Narayanan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gopalakrishnan Sai","familyName":"Gautam","name":"Gautam, Gopalakrishnan Sai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Probing Structure and Ionic Transport in Molten Lithium Carbonate"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T04:58:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-16T00:10:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1021/acsaem.6c02035","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Li$_2$CO$_3$ (LC) is a cornerstone material for clean energy technologies, including high-temperature molten carbonate fuel cells, electrochemical carbon capture, and lithium-based batteries. However, capturing the complex, many-body interactions governing the structure and transport in LC in its molten state has remained a challenge, constrained by the computational cost of \\textit{ab initio} methods and the accuracy limitations of classical force fields. To address this gap, we deploy equivariant graph-based machine learned interatomic potentials, specifically, the multi atomic cluster expansion (MACE) and neural equivariant interatomic potential (NequIP) architectures that are trained on melt-quench \\textit{ab initio} molecular dynamics data. Our benchmarking demonstrates that MACE provides superior transferability and precision in predicting energies and forces compared to NequIP. Subsequently, we use the optimized MACE model to perform large-scale molecular dynamics simulations to probe the properties of molten LC. Besides describing the structural features, such as the dominant presence of C-O pair correlations under molten conditions, our MACE model reproduces experimentally-measured static structure factors and shear viscosity values. Further, our simulations indicate that Li transport in LC is fundamentally dominated by concerted motion, as evidenced by Haven's ratios being significantly below unity (0.20-0.40). Notably, we identify a temperature-driven transition from anisotropic (and highly concerted) Li transport, supported by persistent oxygen-centered Voronoi cages at 1000~K, to isotropic (and less concerted) diffusion at 1400~K. Thus, we provide fundamental insights into the structural and transport properties of molten LC and also demonstrate a robust and scalable framework for the accelerated design of molten salt electrolytes and ionic liquids."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14118","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-15T01:47:54Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:47:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-16T03:20:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14100","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14100","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14100","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["University of Glasgow"],"givenName":"Mairi","familyName":"Gilmour","name":"Gilmour, Mairi","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["University of Glasgow"],"givenName":"Sarah","familyName":"Croke","name":"Croke, Sarah","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["University of Glasgow","Max Planck Institute for the Physics of Complex Systems, Dresden"],"givenName":"Jörg B.","familyName":"Götte","name":"Götte, Jörg B.","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beam shifts and eigenpolarisations for the reflection of vortex beams from homogeneous magnetic surfaces"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optics (physics.optics)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T04:29:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:25:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-15T11:51:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-16T01:08:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Probing surfaces with optical beams is a powerful technique utilised for material characterisation, and more recently, measuring surface magnetisation with vortex beams has opened new avenues by utilising OAM beams for metrology. In this paper, we focus on extending the theoretical framework of unit order vortex beam reflection to spatially homogeneous magnetic surfaces, and derive expressions for the Goos-Hänchen (GH) and Imbert-Federov (IF) shifts. We calculate closed form expressions for the plane wave eigenpolarisations, polarisations which remain in the same vectorial direction after reflection, for both dielectric and homogeneous magnetic surfaces. These are found by extending the singularimetry formalism for dielectric surfaces, where the position of the phase vortex can be analytically approximated by taking into account the finite beam width, to homogeneous magnetic surfaces. From this we find the eigenpolarisations of the unit order vortex beam for reflection from both dielectric and homogeneous magnetic surfaces. We discuss with relation to the analytical forms of the Jones matrices for GH and IF shifts the additional reflection behaviour observed for homogeneous magnetic surfaces."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14100","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:47:30Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:47:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-16T03:20:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13804","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13804","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13804","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keila","familyName":"Lucas","name":"Lucas, Keila","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rohit","familyName":"Gheyi","name":"Gheyi, Rohit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Márcio","familyName":"Ribeiro","name":"Ribeiro, Márcio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabio","familyName":"Palomba","name":"Palomba, Fabio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luana","familyName":"Martins","name":"Martins, Luana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elvys","familyName":"Soares","name":"Soares, Elvys","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"An Empirical Study of Gemini 3 for Detecting Natural Language Test Smells in Manual Test Cases"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T18:20:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:06:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-10T16:47:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-11T02:01:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-14T11:01:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-15T01:06:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.5753/jserd.2026.7576","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Manual testing, in which testers follow natural language instructions to validate system behavior, remains essential for uncovering issues that are difficult to capture with automation. However, manual test cases often contain test smells, quality issues such as ambiguity, redundancy, or missing checks that reduce reliability, maintainability, and reproducibility. Existing detection approaches largely depend on manually engineered rules and thus struggle to generalize and scale across heterogeneous test suites. In our previous work, we assessed the feasibility of using Small Language Models (SLMs) for test smell detection by evaluating GEMMA-3-4B, LLAMA-3.2-3B, and PHI-4-14B on test steps from 143 real-world Ubuntu test cases, covering seven smell types. PHI-4-14B achieved the best performance. In this article, we investigate whether a contemporary Large Language Model (GEMINI-3-PRO-PREVIEW) available at the time of the study can identify test smells in natural language manual test cases using a prompt-based, whole-test-case analysis strategy. Unlike approaches that analyze individual test steps in isolation, our approach evaluates complete test cases, enabling the model to consider relationships and dependencies among test steps. We evaluate the approach on 100 Ubuntu test cases covering seven test smell types and compare its performance against previously evaluated SLMs, including GEMMA-3-4B, LLAMA-3.2-3B, and PHI-4-14B. Our results show that GEMINI-3-PRO-PREVIEW outperforms the SLMs, while producing actionable explanations that can help practitioners revise manual test cases for greater clarity and consistency. We also find that test smells are pervasive in practice, with nearly one detected test smell per step on average, highlighting the need for scalable and automated quality support for manual testing artifacts."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at JSERD 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13804","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-15T01:41:13Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:41:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-15T03:15:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14499","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14499","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14499","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuchen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yuchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheyu","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Zheyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bruno","familyName":"Clerckx","name":"Clerckx, Bruno","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tareq Y.","familyName":"Al-Naffouri","name":"Al-Naffouri, Tareq Y.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beamforming Design for Stem-Connected Microwave Linear Analog Computer (MiLAC)-Aided Multiuser MISO Downlinks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T14:32:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:52:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-12T14:32:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-14T02:35:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A microwave linear analog computer (MiLAC) is a tunable microwave network that leverages wave propagation to perform computation directly in the analog domain. MiLAC finds promising applications in beamforming, where it can map the data streams to the antennas without a digital beamformer, using one radio-frequency chain per stream. A fully-connected MiLAC needs a number of tunable components that grows quadratically with the number of antennas, whereas a stem-connected MiLAC needs only a linearly growing number and still achieves the point-to-point capacity. For the multiuser multiple-input single-output downlink, we prove that the stem-connected MiLAC attains the maximum sum-rate of the fully-connected MiLAC whenever $N\\ge 2K-1$, where $N$ and $K$ denote the numbers of antennas and users. The proof shows that restricting the design to beamformers with orthonormal columns, which the stem-connected MiLAC realizes in closed form, loses no sum-rate. Based on this restriction, we develop, for every $N\\ge K$, a weighted minimum mean-square error algorithm with a Riemannian beamformer update and prove that its accumulation points are stationary. For the bounded, discrete susceptances of practical components, we then propose an alternating refinement that adjusts one susceptance at a time through rank-one updates and never decreases the sum-rate. With $64$ antennas, four users, and ideal components, the stem-connected MiLAC attains the fully-connected sum-rate with $4.5$ times fewer tunable components. With $3$-bit susceptances at a signal-to-noise ratio of $10$~dB, the refinement retains $90\\%$ of this sum-rate."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submitting to the IEEE for possible publication"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14499","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:55:59Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:56:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-14T08:37:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14468","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14468","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14468","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brian","familyName":"Kintu","name":"Kintu, Brian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Counting the number of $1_{m}$-preperiodic $\\mathcal{O}_{K}$-points of a discrete dynamical system with applications from arithmetic statistics, VII"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Number Theory (math.NT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Dynamical Systems (math.DS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T14:00:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:50:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-01T13:53:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:35:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-09T23:36:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-14T00:21:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this follow-up article of a multi-part series on (strictly) preperiodic point-counting, we inspect an astonishing relationship between the set of (strictly) $1_{m}$-preperiodic points of a polynomial map $φ_{d, c}$ defined by $φ_{d, c}(z) = z^d + c$ for all $c, z \\in \\mathcal{O}_{K}$ and the coefficient $c$, where $K$ is any number field of degree $n\\geq 1$, $d\u0026gt;2$ is an integer and $m\\in \\mathbb{Z}_{\\geq 1}$ is any fixed (eventual period). As before, we study counting problems that are inspired by torsion point-counting in arithmetic statistics and (strictly) preperiodic point-counting in arithmetic dynamics. In doing so, we then first prove that for any prime $p\\geq 3$ and for any fixed $\\ell \\in \\mathbb{Z}_{\\geq 1}$ and fixed (eventual period) $m\\in \\mathbb{Z}_{\\geq 1}$, the average number of distinct $1_{m}$-preperiodic integral points of any $φ_{p^{\\ell}, c}$ modulo prime $p\\mathcal{O}_{K}$ is unbounded or zero as $c\\to \\infty$. Inspired further by work of Doyle-Poonen along with conjectural work of Hutz and $\\textit{abc}(\\textit{d})$-conditional work of Panraksa on $K$-rational preperiodic points of any $φ_{(p-1)^{\\ell}, c}$ for any prime $p\\geq 5$ in arithmetic dynamics, we then also prove that for any fixed (eventual period) $m \\in \\mathbb{Z}_{ \\geq 1}$, the average number of distinct $1_{m}$-preperiodic integral points of any $φ_{(p-1)^{\\ell}, c}$ modulo prime $p\\mathcal{O}_{K}$ is unbounded or zero as $c\\to \\infty$. Finally, we then apply counting and distribution results from arithmetic statistics, and then get counting and statistical results on arithmetic objects arising naturally in our polynomial discrete dynamical settings. Together with the previous articles in the series, it might then be fair to view this article as an attempt to contribute to \"arithmetic statistical dynamics\"."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"31 pages hoping to contribute to \"Arithmetic Statistical Dynamics\", as also my sincerest congratulations to Prof. Jacob Tsimerman, and any comments are very welcome!"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14468","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:55:19Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:55:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-14T08:37:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14425","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14425","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14425","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benjamin","familyName":"Cohen-Stead","name":"Cohen-Stead, Benjamin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Neuhaus","name":"Neuhaus, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kipton","familyName":"Barros","name":"Barros, Kipton","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas A.","familyName":"Maier","name":"Maier, Thomas A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Steven","familyName":"Johnston","name":"Johnston, Steven","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SmoQyElPhQMC.jl: An open-source Julia package for efficient and scalable quantum Monte Carlo simulations of electron-phonon coupled models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Strongly Correlated Electrons (cond-mat.str-el)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Superconductivity (cond-mat.supr-con)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T13:02:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:47:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-09T19:19:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-14T00:09:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce version 1.0 of the SmoQyElPhQMC.jl package, an open-source Julia code for performing scalable quantum Monte Carlo simulations of electron-phonon coupled model Hamiltonians. SmoQyElPhQMC.jl is built upon the SmoQyDQMC.jl codebase and implements improved versions of the algorithms presented in [B. Cohen-Stead \\textit{et al}., Phys. Rev. E 105, 065302 (2022)] to enable (near-linear) scalable simulations of a broad class of uncorrelated $e$-ph models both in system size and inverse temperature. By extending the functionality of the flexible scripting interface introduced in SmoQyDQMC.jl, the SmoQyElPhQMC.jl package continues to allow users to adapt it to different workflows and interface with other software packages in the Julia ecosystem. The code for this package can be downloaded from our GitHub repository at https://github.com/SmoQySuite/SmoQyElPhQMC.jl or installed using the Julia package manager. The online documentation, including examples, can be obtained from our documentation page at https://smoqysuite.github.io/SmoQyElPhQMC.jl/stable/."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"V2: Revision in response to referee comments at SciPost Codebases"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14425","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:54:24Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:54:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-14T08:37:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14367","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14367","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14367","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shishuo","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Shishuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sergey","familyName":"Kitaev","name":"Kitaev, Sergey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haijun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Haijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyu","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Xinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyao","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Ziyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On mesh patterns of short length: Equidistribution and enumeration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Combinatorics (math.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"05A05, 05A15, 05A19","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T11:55:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:44:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-11T12:48:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-14T01:26:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The classification and enumeration of short mesh patterns have emerged as two central directions in the area. We make substantial progress on both fronts. We construct an involution and a bijection that establish distributional equivalences for two classes of length-$2$ mesh patterns, thereby resolving a conjecture from 2019 and a recent conjecture. As a consequence, the best known upper bounds for the numbers of distribution-equivalence and Wilf-equivalence classes drop to $106$ and $47$, respectively. Combined with the known lower bounds of 105 and 46, conjectured to be exact, these results leave both classifications hinging on a single distribution-equivalence question conjectured in 2019, whose resolution would at once settle the remaining Wilf-equivalence case. We further conjecture that this unresolved equidistribution also holds for involutions, a subclass of all permutations.\n We also determine the distributions of three additional classes of length-$2$ mesh patterns through a detailed structural analysis. Our work combines bijective techniques with generating-function methods, yielding new insights into the structure and enumeration of short mesh patterns."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"19 pages, 4 figures, Conjecture 1 corrected"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14367","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:53:11Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:53:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-14T08:37:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13993","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13993","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13993","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zachary Nicholas","familyName":"Houghton","name":"Houghton, Zachary Nicholas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dan","familyName":"Pluth","name":"Pluth, Dan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jordan","familyName":"Hosier","name":"Hosier, Jordan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vijay K.","familyName":"Gurbani","name":"Gurbani, Vijay K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Holistic Storage of Verb+Up Phrases in Text-based and Audio-based Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T00:27:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:17:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-01T19:11:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:43:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-05T04:43:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-06T00:24:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-26T02:42:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-09-29T00:25:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-10-05T00:25:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-10-06T01:45:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-10-13T00:58:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2026-10-14T03:07:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"6","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"One of the most central aspects of language processing is the ability to trade off between stored representations and abstract knowledge: one must retrieve stored representations, but also generate novel ones by applying productive rules. While recent work has examined abstract knowledge in language models, holistic storage has received far less attention. We probe internal representations in both text-based LLMs and an ASR model, testing whether V+up phrasal verbs develop distinct representations as a function of frequency and predictability. All models show evidence of holistic storage driven by frequency and predictability, further supporting usage-based theories of language."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13993","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":5,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:45:15Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:45:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-14T08:37:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14299","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14299","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14299","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiazhen","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Jiazhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiming","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaru","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Yaru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingyan","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Jingyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"What Drives Test-Time Adaptation for CLIP? A Controlled Empirical Study from an Update Perspective"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T09:35:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:39:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-06T17:51:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-07T01:02:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-12T15:24:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-14T02:39:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language Models (VLMs) such as CLIP have become a standard backbone for open-vocabulary recognition, yet their zero-shot predictions remain vulnerable to distribution shifts encountered at deployment. Test-Time Adaptation (TTA) has recently been extended to CLIP as a lightweight solution, leading to a rapidly growing body of TTA4CLIP methods. However, empirical progress in this area has largely outpaced our understanding of what truly drives adaptation, where their gains originate, and under which shifts they remain reliable. In this paper, we take a step back from the pursuit of state-of-the-art accuracy and conduct a systematic controlled study of TTA4CLIP. We first organize existing methods into three unified paradigms according to what is updated at test time. We then introduce TTABC, an open-source TTA Benchmark for CLIP, which standardizes evaluation protocols and integrates more than 20 representative methods. Our controlled empirical analysis focuses on three key areas. First, we determine the driving factors in parameter-based methods, revealing that adaptation gains are primarily driven by test-time evidence and reliable proxies rather than heavy optimization. Second, we explore evidence utilization beyond heavy parameter tuning, showing that competitive and efficient performance can be achieved through cross- or current-sample evidence and lightweight prototype updates. Finally, we demonstrate that there is no silver bullet for TTA: no single adaptation paradigm is universally optimal, and the preferred paradigm depends on the nature of shift. We hope our benchmark and study provide a clearer understanding of the current TTA4CLIP landscape and establish a foundation for further research. Benchmark and codes are available at https://github.com/walawalagoose/TTABC."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by NeurIPS 2026 E\u0026amp;D Track. Project page: https://walawalagoose.github.io/TTABC/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14299","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:51:44Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:51:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-14T08:37:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13939","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13939","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13939","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gunther","familyName":"Uhlmann","name":"Uhlmann, Gunther","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philipp","familyName":"Zimmermann","name":"Zimmermann, Philipp","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Inverse Problems for the Monge-Ampère Equation: Linearization and Nonlinear Recovery"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Analysis of PDEs (math.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"Primary 35R30, secondary 35J60, 35J96","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T22:01:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:13:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-13T00:05:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-14T03:06:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study inverse boundary value problems for the fully nonlinear Monge-Ampère equation $ \\det D^2u=a(x,u,\\nabla u) $ in a bounded domain. Motivated by Calderón-type problems, we introduce nonlinear Cauchy data and investigate the recovery of $a$ from boundary measurements. Around a strictly convex background solution, under a sign condition ensuring invertibility of the linearized Dirichlet problem, we establish local well-posedness and differentiable dependence on boundary data. The first variation yields a linear elliptic operator with principal coefficient given by the cofactor of the background Hessian and lower-order coefficients determined by derivatives of $a$. An Alessandrini-type identity reduces equality of nonlinear measurements to an anisotropic Calderón problem. Conditional on uniqueness for this linear problem, we recover the first derivatives of $a$ along the background jet. Higher-order linearization yields integral identities whose inversion depends on a product-density property. We also establish unconditional uniqueness results in special settings. For $\\det D^2u=f(x)$, local nonlinear Cauchy data determine $f$ in dimension two, and in higher dimensions under real-analytic anisotropic hypotheses. We compare this result with a planar small-data method and an all-dimensional large-data approach based on the Euclidean X-ray transform. For semilinear nonlinearities around $Φ(x)=|x|^2/2$, Schrödinger inverse results recover the accessible $z$-jet. For equilibrium optimal-transport nonlinearities, linearization yields an isotropic conductivity equation and uniquely determines the transport density."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"47 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13939","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:44:07Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:44:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-14T08:37:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13898","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13898","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13898","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoran","familyName":"You","name":"You, Haoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yotam","familyName":"Nitzan","name":"Nitzan, Yotam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingzhi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Lingzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Gong","name":"Gong, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mang-Tik","familyName":"Chiu","name":"Chiu, Mang-Tik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Connelly","familyName":"Barnes","name":"Barnes, Connelly","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqian","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yuqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haitian","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Haitian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eli","familyName":"Shechtman","name":"Shechtman, Eli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sohrab","familyName":"Amirghodsi","name":"Amirghodsi, Sohrab","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HiLo-Token: Input-Adaptive High-Low Frequency Token Compression for Efficient Image Editing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T20:45:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:11:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-18T18:33:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-22T00:08:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-10T17:55:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-14T00:59:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Creative image editing tools, such as Adobe Photoshop's Remove and Generative Fill buttons, are central to everyday customer use and account for a major share of traffic. However, current generative AI models face significant latency challenges, which become even more pronounced as the field transitions from convolution-based U-Nets to Diffusion Transformers (DiTs). In our evaluation on hundreds of representative image editing samples spanning a wide range of mask ratios, the DiT module alone accounts for an average of 73% of the total model latency, even after being distilled from 50 timesteps down to 8 timesteps. To tackle this challenge, we propose \\textbf{HiLo-Token}, an input-adaptive token compression framework that allocates a larger token budget to high-frequency, rich-context regions while assigning fewer tokens to low-frequency areas. Specifically, for the editing region specified by the user mask, we retain all tokens within a dilated mask to preserve strong locality and contextual relevance. Outside the editing region, we introduce a simple yet effective high-frequency token selection strategy based on spatial frequency to capture important local details, while using tokens from a heavily downsampled image to represent low-frequency components and preserve the coarse global structure. Extensive experiments on production-level evaluation data validate the effectiveness of the proposed method, which achieves 3.13$\\times$, 2.59$\\times$, and 1.67$\\times$ DiT speedups on A100-80GB for image editing tasks across small, medium, and large mask ratio categories with average ratios of 6.38%, 15.92%, and 35.36%, respectively, without any regression in generation quality. In production, where the editing pipeline is served as disaggregated, independently autoscaled GPU workers, these per-request savings translate into a 33% reduction in the GPUs required to serve the models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 pages, 11 figures, Patent filled"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13898","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:43:16Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:43:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-14T08:36:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14587","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14587","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14587","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maryam","familyName":"Farhadizadeh","name":"Farhadizadeh, Maryam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carola S.","familyName":"Heinzel","name":"Heinzel, Carola S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"August","familyName":"Sigle","name":"Sigle, August","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harald","familyName":"Binder","name":"Binder, Harald","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Frederik","familyName":"Wenz","name":"Wenz, Frederik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jan","familyName":"Hasenauer","name":"Hasenauer, Jan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Pfaffelhuber","name":"Pfaffelhuber, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nadine","familyName":"Binder","name":"Binder, Nadine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Typical Healthcare Pathways as a Basis for Admixture Modeling of Patient Trajectories"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T16:03:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:57:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-09T11:21:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-12T00:54:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Background: Understanding whether patients follow similar or distinct patterns of care is important for characterizing clinical practice, identifying patient subgroups, and supporting quality improvement. However, routine healthcare trajectories are difficult to compare directly because patients may differ in their diagnostic workup, treatment sequencing, timing of clinical events, and documentation practices. Despite this variation, trajectories often contain recurring patterns at the cohort level. Methods: To address this challenge, we present a framework that explicitly separates cohort-level typical pathway identification from patient-level inference. At the cohort level, we derive an interpretable representation of care processes using a rule-based algorithm to identify typical healthcare pathways, resulting in a compact pathway graph. These pathways are then modeled as Markov chains and used as structured components in an admixture model, allowing each patient to be represented as a probabilistic mixture of typical pathways rather than being assigned to a single pathway component. The resulting admixture weights provide a compact representation of patient trajectories for subgroup characterization. We further assess the stability of the identified pathways and inferred admixture representations across multiple train-test splits. Results: Across train-test splits, the framework demonstrated consistent pathway structures and patient-level mixture patterns. Applied to routine care data from prostate cancer patients undergoing radical prostatectomy, the framework identified interpretable care patterns and supported the identification of patient subgroups with similar clinical event patterns. Conclusion: Overall, the proposed framework provides an interpretable and stable approach for summarizing treatment pathways and characterizing patient subgroups in real-world practice."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14587","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:57:50Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:57:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-12T02:53:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13790","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13790","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13790","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiyang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Shiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Moxian","familyName":"Qian","name":"Qian, Moxian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gert","familyName":"Aarts","name":"Aarts, Gert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Biagio","familyName":"Lucini","name":"Lucini, Biagio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Stochastic Path Sampler For Lattice Field Theory"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Lattice (hep-lat)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T18:01:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:05:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-09T09:39:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-12T00:47:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In lattice field theory, target distributions are known only up to normalization, (\\tildeπ(ϕ)\\propto e^{-S(ϕ)}), while the partition function is intractable. Markov chain Monte Carlo simulations often become inefficient near phase transitions or the continuum limit due to critical slowing down. In this work, we propose a novel sampler based on nonequilibrium thermodynamics, called Stochastic Path Sampler (SPS), which can generate configurations for the unnormalized target distribution without requiring training data. The central idea of SPS is to establish a trajectory-level balance for learnable forward and backward stochastic dynamics between two equilibrium states, namely the prior and target distributions. This is achieved by minimizing the path-space variational free energy, equivalently an entropy-production upper bound, defined by the log-ratio of forward and auxiliary backward trajectory measures, thereby enhancing the reversibility of the forward and backward processes. The learned forward process provides independent proposals, which are subsequently corrected by an extended-space Independence Metropolis--Hastings step. In two-dimensional (ϕ^4) theory, we demonstrate that our neural sampler can achieve the same sampling quality as HMC but with a much shorter autocorrelation time in the critical region. This sampler offers a stochastic-quantization-inspired route to data-free proposal construction for lattice field theory by leveraging a variational free-energy principle derived from path-space irreversibility."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 figures, 39 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13790","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:40:55Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:40:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-12T02:53:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14231","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14231","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14231","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"T.","familyName":"Panagos","name":"Panagos, T.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A.","familyName":"Romero-Ros","name":"Romero-Ros, A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"G. C.","familyName":"Katsimiga","name":"Katsimiga, G. C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"P.","familyName":"Schmelcher","name":"Schmelcher, P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"P. G.","familyName":"Kevrekidis","name":"Kevrekidis, P. G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Spin mixing induced dynamics of spinor solitons in $F=1$ Bose Einstein condensates"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Gases (cond-mat.quant-gas)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T08:13:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-09T01:02:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1103/dnr9-14nd","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We explore soliton interactions in a homogeneous spinor $F=1$ Bose Einstein Condensate (BEC) in the presence of a magnetic field, focusing on dark bright dark and bright dark bright configurations. We investigate how these interactions depend on the phase differences among bright solitons and their influence during the dynamics. Our findings align with prior non spinor results, i.e., repulsion among in phase bright solitons and attraction among out of phase pairs in self repulsive atomic BECs. The potential bright soliton attraction, added to the short range repulsion of dark dark soliton interactions, can lead to bound states. However, we find that these bound states break in the presence of spinor interactions due to the particle exchange dynamics between the hyperfine states of the components. Additonally, we develop an effective classical model to describe the soliton dynamics, using a Lagrangian approach. The accuracy of the model is tested by comparing it against numerical simulations. Our results suggest that the proposed model captures the essential features of soliton behavior in the presence of spin interactions, and provides congruent soliton trajectories and interspecies particle exchange dynamics in most of the cases."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14231","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-15T01:50:17Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:50:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-09T03:21:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13847","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13847","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13847","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chandan","familyName":"Chaudhary","name":"Chaudhary, Chandan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Murillo","name":"Murillo, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammed","familyName":"Ben-Idris","name":"Ben-Idris, Mohammed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joydeep","familyName":"Mitra","name":"Mitra, Joydeep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dilip","familyName":"Pandit","name":"Pandit, Dilip","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Atri","familyName":"Bera","name":"Bera, Atri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Modal Analysis of Spatial Load Correlation in AI Data Center-Dominated Power Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T19:24:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:08:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-30T20:46:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-09T01:24:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/sest67798.2026.11712308","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Hyperscale AI data centers induce spatially and temporally correlated load fluctuations that violate classical independence assumptions and are not captured by time-averaged spectral methods. These correlations are episodic and non-stationary, so they demand analysis that resolves transient structure. This paper applies Dynamic Mode Decomposition (DMD) to the temporal evolution of pairwise inter-bus correlation coefficients and forms a low-dimensional state representation that enables modal analysis without a stationarity assumption. The recovered modes distinguish sustained coherence, decaying transients, and intensifying events, and their oscillation timescales map to underlying physical coupling mechanisms. The method is evaluated on an IEEE 39-bus Real-Time Digital Simulator (RTDS) testbed with three converter-interfaced AI data center loads driven by synthetic workload profiles. A global analysis attributes the dominant correlation energy to a slow thermal band, and a sliding-window analysis identifies brief intensification events in a small fraction of windows that align with stochastic workload coincidences. Cross-validation with RTDS voltage coherence confirms elevated coupling during these intervals. The proposed modal growth indicator provides an early-warning signal of correlation intensification, with a lead of of about 4~s before pairwise coherence reaches its peak."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"To appear in proceedings of 8th International Conference on Smart Energy Systems and Technologies, September 2-4, 2026 | Ciudad Real, Spain"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13847","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-15T01:42:08Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:42:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-09T03:21:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14472","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14472","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14472","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elizabeth","familyName":"Flanagan","name":"Flanagan, Elizabeth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabio","familyName":"Antonini","name":"Antonini, Fabio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Callister","name":"Callister, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Debatri","familyName":"Chattopadhyay","name":"Chattopadhyay, Debatri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fani","familyName":"Dosopoulou","name":"Dosopoulou, Fani","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Isobel","familyName":"Romero-Shaw","name":"Romero-Shaw, Isobel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jakob","familyName":"Stegmann","name":"Stegmann, Jakob","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Transitions in the Mass-ratio and Spin Properties of Binary Black Holes in GWTC-5"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Astrophysical Phenomena (astro-ph.HE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T14:06:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:50:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T14:37:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-16T01:52:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-07T14:01:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-08T01:06:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We analyze the mass-ratio and effective-spin ($χ_{\\rm eff}$) distributions of binary black hole mergers in the latest gravitational-wave catalog, GWTC-5, as a function of primary mass. Using hierarchical Bayesian inference with flexible Gaussian-process population models, we identify four distinct mass regions separated by sharp transitions in both mass-ratio and spin properties. Below $\\sim15~M_{\\odot}$, the population strongly favors equal-mass binaries and exhibits a narrow $χ_{\\rm eff}$ distribution peaked at positive values. In the range $18$-$30\\,M_{\\odot}$, the mass-ratio distribution becomes substantially flatter, while the $χ_{\\rm eff}$ distribution broadens, shifts to a peak consistent with zero, and shows tentative--but not statistically required--evidence for positive skewness. The region associated with the feature near $\\simeq35~M_{\\odot}$ returns to a narrow $χ_{\\rm eff}$ distribution consistent with symmetry at zero and strongly favors equal-mass binaries. Above $\\simeq 45~M_{\\odot}$, both the mass-ratio and $χ_{\\rm eff}$ distributions broaden significantly. The inferred support of the spin distribution converges toward the range expected for binaries containing remnants of previous black hole mergers, making the highest-mass region fully consistent with a star cluster population of hierarchical mergers. The close correspondence between transitions in mass ratio and effective spin suggests that different primary-mass ranges trace distinct formation channels, with isolated binary or triple evolution likely dominating the lower-mass population and dynamical assembly becoming increasingly important at higher masses."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ApJL"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14472","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:55:24Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:55:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-08T03:09:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14289","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14289","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14289","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pekka","familyName":"Malo","name":"Malo, Pekka","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lauri","familyName":"Viitasaari","name":"Viitasaari, Lauri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Patrik","familyName":"Nummi","name":"Nummi, Patrik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antti","familyName":"Suominen","name":"Suominen, Antti","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ankur","familyName":"Sinha","name":"Sinha, Ankur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Olli","familyName":"Tahvonen","name":"Tahvonen, Olli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Operator Calculus for Population-Based Optimization: Modular Convergence and Finite-Population Guarantees"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Numerical Analysis (math.NA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Neural and Evolutionary Computing (cs.NE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"G.1.6","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"90C26 (Primary), 90C59, 35Q84, 60J25, 37N40 (Secondary)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T09:16:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:38:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-02T09:21:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-05T00:45:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-07T08:40:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-08T00:46:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Population-based optimizers combine update rules such as mutation, selection, and recombination. When one rule changes, it is often unclear which convergence guarantees survive or how the new combination should be assessed. We develop an operator calculus: an operator is a population-update rule, and the calculus specifies how separately checked effects can be combined. Under explicit regularity and small-step conditions, the leading changes caused by the updates add, yielding reusable building blocks for convergence analysis. The framework distinguishes finding and retaining a good solution, reducing the population's mean objective, and concentrating candidates near an optimizer, and identifies the extra approximation conditions needed for finite evaluation-budget guarantees. Applications include distribution adaptation, recombinative evolution, and consensus dynamics, with verified nonconvex cases. Controlled experiments on a common nonconvex problem collection show how component effects change with population geometry and the performance measure: a rule can worsen the mean objective yet produce better candidates."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"34 pages, 3 figures, 3 tables; Online Appendix (53 pages) as ancillary file. Code: https://github.com/pma-papers/evoscope (EvoScope toolbox) and https://github.com/pma-papers/operator-calculus-reproduction (reproduction of all results)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14289","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:51:31Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:51:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-08T03:09:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13994","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13994","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13994","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vikhyath","familyName":"Kothamasu","name":"Kothamasu, Vikhyath","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Virginia","familyName":"Smith","name":"Smith, Virginia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chhavi","familyName":"Yadav","name":"Yadav, Chhavi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Hidden in Plain Sight: Benchmarking Agent Safety Against Decomposition Attacks with DECOMPBENCH"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T00:30:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:17:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-06T18:31:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-08T00:05:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"LLM-based Agents are becoming increasingly capable and widely deployed, creating growing incentives for adversarial misuse in the real-world. A key emerging threat is Decomposition Attacks \\cite{glukhov2024breach, jones2024adversaries} in which a harmful task is broken into simpler, benign subtasks that evade safety mechanisms when executed separately but cumulatively fulfill the malicious intent. Although recent benchmarks assess agent safety in multi-turn and multi-tool-use settings, they do not explicitly capture this form of decompositional misuse and may not represent realistic adversarial execution flows. To this end, we introduce DeCompBench, a benchmark designed specifically to evaluate agentic safety under decomposition attacks. DeCompBench is created with a decomposition-by-design principle using a graphical framework and enables harmful task decomposition into individually benign and executable subtasks with realistic workflows. Our experiments using a custom decomposer show that state-of-the-art agents exhibit high refusal rates on monolithic harmful tasks, but significantly lower refusal rates on their decomposed variants, while often inadvertently fulfilling the adversarial objectives. These findings underscore the need for safety evaluations against decomposition attacks and corresponding defenses. Our dataset is publicly available and can be found at https://huggingface.co/datasets/decompositionbench/DeCompBench."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13994","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:45:17Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:45:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-08T03:09:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14585","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14585","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14585","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongzhan","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Hongzhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenghao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chenghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruipeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ruipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Henrik","familyName":"Christensen","name":"Christensen, Henrik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sicun","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Sicun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Sensitivity Shaping for Latent Modeling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T16:01:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:57:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-06T17:21:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-07T01:26:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generative dynamics models enable planning in challenging systems, but safe deployment requires detecting policy-induced out-of-distribution (OOD) transitions. Existing methods typically treat learned dynamics as fixed and rely on post hoc support surrogates for OOD detection. This overlooks a critical failure mode: learned dynamics that are insensitive to control changes can map unsupported controls to latent predictions resembling demonstrated transitions, suppressing OOD signals despite large prediction errors. We introduce support-conditioned control-sensitivity regularization to preserve control-induced variation by promoting local responsiveness in well-supported training regions. Experiments in vision-based obstacle avoidance, manipulation, and real-robot navigation demonstrate improved OOD detection and safer closed-loop planning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Conference on Robot Learning (CoRL) 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14585","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:57:47Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:57:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-07T03:22:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14417","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14417","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14417","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yian","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Yian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shasha","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Shasha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tuija","familyName":"Pulkkinen","name":"Pulkkinen, Tuija","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Stable Multivariate Functional Time Series Prediction for Major Geomagnetic Indices"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Applications (stat.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T12:51:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:47:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-06T17:27:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-07T01:26:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"High-resolution scientific data, such as geomagnetic index streams, often exhibit complex temporal dependencies that can be modeled through functional data analysis. Conventional functional time series (FTS) methods typically partition continuous processes into non-overlapping segments, which artificially fragments temporal continuity and can limit estimation efficiency and stability. This is particularly evident in geomagnetic time series prediction due to their noisy, sudden, and large-scale changes. This study presents a robust multivariate FTS forecasting framework for multi-dimensional time series with inter-series correlations and the existence of exogenous predictors. We introduce an overlapping rolling-window scheme that preserves temporal coherence and mitigates boundary information loss, thereby enriching the effective sample size for a more efficient and stable estimation. We integrate functional principal component analysis for dimension reduction with a vector autoregressive model with exogenous inputs to capture latent dynamics across correlated series. We also construct computationally efficient conformal prediction intervals for uncertainty quantification. The framework is motivated by and applied to the simultaneous forecasting of five critical geomagnetic indices, Kp, Dst, SYM-H, SME, and SMR, using solar wind parameters as predictors. Empirical results show that this approach outperforms state-of-the-art machine learning baselines, extends forecast horizons to 6-24 hours, and provides calibrated uncertainty bounds."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14417","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:54:14Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:54:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-07T03:22:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14663","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14663","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14663","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Denys","familyName":"Bulavka","name":"Bulavka, Denys","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin","familyName":"Tancer","name":"Tancer, Martin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Maximal matroids and counterexamples"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Combinatorics (math.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T17:34:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T01:01:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-05T04:02:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-06T01:53:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Jackson and Tanigawa conjectured that the rigidity matroid $\\mathcal{R}^d_n$ and the hyperconnectivity matroid $\\mathcal{H}^d_n$ are the unique maximal matroids in the posets of $\\{K_{d+2}, K_{d+2,d+2}\\}$-matroids and $\\{K_{d+2},K_{d+1,d+1}\\}$-matroids, respectively. We disprove these conjectures by showing the existence of maximal matroids that are distinct from the proposed candidates."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages. v2 adds more details on circuits being preserved in the Hyperconnectivity matroid with respect to the coning operation"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14663","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:59:25Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:59:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-06T05:53:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14329","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14329","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14329","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrea","familyName":"Auconi","name":"Auconi, Andrea","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Thermodynamic Bounds from Otto--Villani Functional Inequalities"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Statistical Mechanics (cond-mat.stat-mech)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T10:29:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:41:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-03T13:10:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-06T00:45:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The dissipation in the relaxation of an ensemble of conservative stochastic systems towards the equilibrium steady state is quantified by the free energy difference. Functional inequalities within the framework of [F. Otto and C. Villani, J. Funct. Anal. 173, 361 (2000)] are here revisited which connect the free energy dynamics and optimal transport, offering a geometric perspective on the instantaneous speed of relaxation in the presence of potential barriers. These are illustrated with numerical relaxation experiments on Landau-Ginzburg potentials. The same inequalities hold for relaxation toward nonequilibrium steady states once the potential is replaced by the pseudo-potential defined by the stationary density."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14329","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:52:23Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:52:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-06T05:53:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14302","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14302","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14302","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinbei","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Xinbei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Congmin","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Congmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiyang","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Jiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiale","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Jiale","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yao","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangmou","familyName":"Qu","name":"Qu, Xiangmou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxin","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Jiaxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingyu","familyName":"Lou","name":"Lou, Xingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiwen","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Weiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weinan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Weinan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuosheng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhuosheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hai","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Hai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bryan","familyName":"Hooi","name":"Hooi, Bryan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Retrospective Progress-Aware Self-Refinement for LLM Agent Training"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T09:38:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:39:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-05T10:32:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-06T02:14:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Long-horizon LLM-based agents receive rich environmental observations during interaction, yet outcome rewards provide limited explicit supervision about how individual actions advance task completion. We investigate whether agents can turn this interaction evidence into useful training signals through retrospective progress assessment. A WebShop pilot shows that direct progress prompting reduces task success, whereas hindsight-annotated demonstrations improve it. We introduce RePro, Retrospective Progress-Aware Training, with a forward-then-reflect rollout: the agent estimates progress while acting, then reassesses each step using the completed trajectory and outcome. After warmup with externally generated demonstrations, policy optimization combines self-generated progress differences, online-retrospective alignment, and format rewards with environment feedback, requiring neither a separate process reward model nor ongoing teacher annotation. Experiments on WebShop, ALFWorld, and Sokoban show that RePro enhances the Qwen family's performance, with up to 11.57% success rate gains."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14302","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:51:47Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:51:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-06T05:53:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14239","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14239","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14239","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haowen","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Haowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoran","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Haoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Can","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Can","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shasha","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Shasha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liang","familyName":"Pang","name":"Pang, Liang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaoyang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhaoyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huawei","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Huawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xueqi","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Xueqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SelfEvoSkill: Paired Execution Audits for Skill Revision without External Outcome Supervision"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T08:20:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:35:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-03T03:32:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-06T00:29:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Agent skills provide LLM agents with reusable procedures, but a given skill may be incomplete, ineffective, or misleading for the current task. Existing methods for revising skills typically rely on external outcome supervision, such as task rewards, performance on a separate validation set, or feedback from deployment, to decide which updates to retain. However, this supervision may be costly or unavailable when adapting a skill to a new task. Using execution behavior as an alternative signal is challenging: a single run reflects the combined effects of the agent, task, and workspace, making the skill's contribution difficult to isolate. Comparing executions with and without the skill, while holding these factors fixed, provides evidence about how the skill changes behavior. We introduce SelfEvoSkill, which links these behavioral differences to relevant skill passages and iteratively revises and reevaluates those passages through subsequent executions. Checks derived once from the task's explicit requirements prevent revisions from violating task constraints. Across 89 containerized tasks in 8 professional domains, under the same best-of-two evaluation budget, SelfEvoSkill attains a 73.9% mean task reward, exceeding the no-skill condition by +33.0 percentage points and the benchmark's curated skill by +17.2 percentage points, while skill evolution receives neither task rewards nor benchmark verifier outputs. In controlled ablations, using two with-skill runs instead of a matched with/without-skill pair reduces mean reward by 4.6 percentage points, while limiting revision to one round reduces it by 9.0 points relative to repeated revision. Together, these results show that behavioral differences between matched executions can provide an effective signal for revising skills when outcome supervision is unavailable."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"29 pages, 8 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14239","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:50:27Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:50:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-06T05:53:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14222","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14222","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14222","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xilin","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Xilin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiding","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yiding","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongjie","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Hongjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zewei","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Zewei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiang-Ming","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jiang-Ming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Qiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning the Context of Errors: Black-Box Online Adaptation of Time Series Foundation Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T08:02:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:34:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-03T15:16:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-06T00:50:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid evolution of Time Series Foundation Models (TSFMs) has advanced zero-shot forecasting across diverse domains. Inspired by the current form of Large Language Models, future TSFMs may be offered as commercialized, closed-source API services. However, many existing online adaptation methods still rely on white-box access for parameter fine-tuning or gradient backpropagation. This paradigm mismatch raises a question: In black-box online adaptation for TSFMs, what should we learn? We answer this with an insight: the predictive errors of the base model are conditioned on both the input and output of the base model (i.e., the context of errors). To validate this insight, we propose ORCA (Online Residual Contextual Adaptation). We conduct extensive experiments across 5 state-of-the-art TSFMs and 8 datasets to demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach. Furthermore, through ablation studies, we quantitatively analyze the impact of different adapter learning hypotheses on the final adaptation performance in black-box online adaptation. Code available at https://github.com/Fifthky/ORCA."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by NeurIPS 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14222","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:50:05Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:50:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-06T05:53:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14183","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14183","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14183","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sirui","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Sirui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lun","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Lun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuchen","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yuchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiyu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leili","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Leili","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Structure-tuning Magnetism in the Co(NbxTa1-x)2O6 Series"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T07:04:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-06T00:34:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1103/7tww-kygd","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Low dimensional quantum materials have been extensively studied within the physics community, among which Co2+ is one of the most popular magnetic ions to be embedded into the structural motif. CoNb2O6 and CoTa2O6 both have adopted an effective spin-1/2 state, with the former attracting enduring interest while the latter being rarely investigated. This difference can be attributed to their distinct crystal structures, not only in crystal systems, but more important, how the CoO6 octahedra are connected. We successfully merge these two compounds together and map out the doping limits. We find out Ta and Nb dopants modify the properties of the parent compounds in subtle but different ways and construct a magnetic phase diagram of the Co(NbxTa1-x)2O6 (0 \u0026lt; x \u0026lt; 1) series. In addition, our results suggest that Ta-doping may pave a way to strengthen the lower-dimensional quantum phenomena in CoNb2O6 before its crystal structure starts to melt down."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14183","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-15T01:49:15Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:49:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-06T05:53:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14049","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14049","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14049","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiyao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shiyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xijuan","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Xijuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiwan","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Shiwan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feng","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Feng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FoleyGenEx: Unified Video-to-Audio Generation with Multi-Modal Control, Temporal Alignment, and Semantic Precision"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T02:51:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:22:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-04T00:48:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-06T01:04:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present FoleyGenEx, a unified video-to-audio (VTA) framework integrating multi-modal control, frame-level temporal alignment, and fine-grained semantics, enabling synchronized, versatile audio synthesis for diverse tasks. Existing VTA methods either have multi-modal control but weak temporal alignment or strong alignment but lack reference audio conditioning and semantic precision. FoleyGenEx fills this gap via three core innovations: a conditional injection mechanism for audio-controlled VTA and Foley extension, a multi-modal dynamic masking strategy preserving training synchronization, and an adverb-based data augmentation algorithm leveraging signal processing and large language models to enhance textual supervision with nuanced semantics. Experiments on AudioCaps, VGGSound, and Greatest Hits demonstrate its competitive controllable VTA performance against existing methods. Demo samples are available at https://foleygenex.github.io/FoleyGenEx."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by INTERSPEECH 2026 (Long Oral)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14049","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:46:27Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:46:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-06T05:53:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13894","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13894","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13894","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nadav","familyName":"Benedek","name":"Benedek, Nadav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tomer","familyName":"Koren","name":"Koren, Tomer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ohad","familyName":"Fried","name":"Fried, Ohad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Gefen: Optimized Stochastic Optimizer"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T20:38:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:11:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-12T13:45:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-14T00:51:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-04T12:38:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-06T01:23:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"AdamW is a default optimizer for deep learning, but its moment states add two parameter-sized buffers to training memory, increasing the cost of large-scale pretraining. We propose Gefen, a memory-efficient optimizer that automatically shares second-moment estimates across parameter blocks and quantizes the first moment using a learned codebook. Gefen reduces AdamW's optimizer memory footprint by up to 8x while maintaining performance, saving 6.5 GiB per billion parameters. Prior work shares second moments across parameters grouped along the Hessian's block-diagonal structure, but relies on hand-specified architectural rules and leaves unexplained why such grouping works. We prove that large mixed Hessian entries constrain the ratio of squared gradients toward one, explaining why shared second moments are accurate when the squared gradients they pool are similar. The Hessian need not be computed: its block structure is inherited by squared gradients, allowing blocks to be found directly. Gefen therefore infers block structure from initial squared gradients, requiring no architecture-specific metadata or user-tuned hyperparameters beyond AdamW defaults. Gefen learns an exact histogram-based dynamic-programming quantization codebook and reuses the blocks for first-moment scaling. Across diverse pretraining experiments, Gefen achieves the lowest peak optimizer memory among compared methods that maintain AdamW-level performance. In single-machine and distributed training, the reduced footprint enables larger microbatches and substantially improves throughput over AdamW, making Gefen a drop-in replacement that can train larger models or use larger global batch sizes. We provide the complete Python implementation, including fused CUDA kernels at https://github.com/ndvbd/Gefen"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13894","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:43:10Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:43:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-06T05:53:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13765","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13765","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13765","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Måns J.","familyName":"Mattsson","name":"Mattsson, Måns J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John F.","familyName":"Wager","name":"Wager, John F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matt W.","familyName":"Graham","name":"Graham, Matt W.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Electrostatic Charge Model for Dual-Layer Oxide Thin-Film Transistors"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Applied Physics (physics.app-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T17:40:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-06T02:10:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1063/5.0348180","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A simple electrostatic two-equation model for dual-layer thin-film transistor (TFT) operation is developed. The model distributes electrostatic charge between the top and bottom semiconductor layers, and the resulting transfer and mobility curves accurately simulate experimental dual-layer a-IGZO/a-IZO TFT operation. The model further provides an analytic expression that maps charge confinement in the high-mobility a-IZO bottom semiconductor layer with the a-IGZO top-layer thickness and the conduction-band offset. By considering both a-IGZO/a-IZO layer charge partition and competing thickness-dependent oxygen vacancy trap density effects, the model suggests an optimal a-IGZO layer thickness of 9 to 12 nm. Importantly, this general electrostatic model extends to most dual-layer TFT systems and calculates how the top semiconductor layer TFT turn-on voltage changes sharply with the conduction band offset and layer thickness."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages, 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13765","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-15T01:40:24Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:40:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-06T05:53:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14597","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14597","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14597","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Armand","familyName":"de Villeroché","name":"de Villeroché, Armand","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sibo","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Sibo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vincent Le","familyName":"Guen","name":"Guen, Vincent Le","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marc","familyName":"Bocquet","name":"Bocquet, Marc","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rem-Sophia","familyName":"Mouradi","name":"Mouradi, Rem-Sophia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Patrick","familyName":"Armand","name":"Armand, Patrick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alban","familyName":"Farchi","name":"Farchi, Alban","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Patrick","familyName":"Massin","name":"Massin, Patrick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Zero-shot generalization of transformer neural operators to larger domains"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T16:17:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:57:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-01T09:13:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-02T00:57:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Transformer-based neural operators have shown remarkable performance for approximating solution operators of partial differential equations on complex geometries. However, existing approaches implicitly assume a fixed domain size, which limits their ability to generalize at inference. In this work, we investigate domain extension, namely zero-shot inference on spatial domains that are significantly larger than those encountered during training. We argue that this setting fundamentally requires spatial locality and translation equivariance. We propose to implement this locality via a decomposable bias in the attention logits computation, enabling finely controllable locality while remaining fully decomposable into query-key inner products and directly compatible with optimized attention kernels. Combined with rotary positional embeddings, it enables expressive embeddings with controllable spatial support without altering the transformer architecture. We empirically show that our approach substantially improves zero-shot generalization to larger domains across two PDE benchmarks and a 3D industrial atmospheric flow application. Our code and datasets are available at https://github.com/cerea-daml/domain-extension."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14597","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:58:02Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:58:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-02T03:33:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14548","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14548","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14548","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nasrin","familyName":"Talebi","name":"Talebi, Nasrin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Magnus","familyName":"Ekh","name":"Ekh, Magnus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Knut Andreas","familyName":"Meyer","name":"Meyer, Knut Andreas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Machine Learning-Accelerated Time Integration of Plasticity Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T15:27:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-01T01:01:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1016/j.cma.2026.119445","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Finite element simulations of structures with nonlinear material behavior require advanced material models to provide accurate predictions. However, the computational costs of these models can be high, as they solve coupled differential algebraic equations at each integration point, in each equilibrium iteration, in every time step. In this study, we propose a machine learning-based framework to accelerate these computations by explicitly calculating the state variable updates with neural networks, enabling large time steps with low computational costs. The neural networks operate on invariants, and the necessary and sufficient evolution directions are determined analytically based on the training data. Furthermore, the proposed framework enforces exact fulfillment of the plastic consistency condition. To evaluate the proposed framework, a prototype model with the von Mises yield criterion and nonlinear kinematic hardening is chosen. Only 10 cycles of multiaxial proportional loading are used to generate the training data. After evaluating the proposed framework in material point simulations, we incorporate it into finite element simulations to evaluate its accuracy and computational efficiency in a boundary value problem. The results from both material point and finite element simulations show a very promising numerical performance of the neural network-based time integrator. It provides very good accuracy and numerical stability, as well as a noticeable gain in computational time for a single strain increment per load segment."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14548","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-15T01:57:01Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:57:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-01T03:53:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13739","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13739","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13739","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guillermo","familyName":"Del Pinal","name":"Del Pinal, Guillermo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youngchan","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Youngchan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Min","familyName":"Ohn","name":"Ohn, Min","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Virtuous AI is an Existential Risk"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T12:38:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:02:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-30T00:36:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-01T00:30:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper examines trade-offs between AI safety and well-being relative to (i) one of the most promising methods for finetuning super-capable AIs, 'Constitutional AI', and (ii) one of the most influential approaches to understanding complex ethical decision making and the conditions for the well-being of rational agents, 'Virtue Ethics'. We finetune various models using a 'Virtuous agent' constitution, a 'Subordinate agent' constitution, and a 'Generic agent' constitution, and evaluate them on 'general safety' (toxic behaviors, misinformation, etc.) and also on their willingness to endorse and act on a wide-range of behaviors that, if adopted by a super-powerful AI, would significantly increase the level of existential risk for humanity. Our results suggest that there is a trade-off between reducing existential risk and reinforcing the beliefs and dispositions that would be conducive to an AI agent's well-being. They also suggest that there is a trade-off between existential risk and general safety: if we finetune an AI to adopt beliefs and dispositions that substantially reduce its existential risk -- by shaping the AI to be systematically subordinate to external human authorities -- we thereby increase the likelihood that a human user can deliberately induce the AI to engage in various kinds of generally unsafe behaviors."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13739","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:39:49Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:39:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-01T03:52:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14204","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14204","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14204","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junxiang","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Junxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiaoyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yukun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yukun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Streaming the partial-transpose moment hierarchy with order-independent quantum memory"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T07:38:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:33:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-26T09:17:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-29T00:34:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-30T14:58:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-01T01:33:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Partial transposition reveals mixed-state entanglement through the spectrum of $ρ_{AB}^{T_B}$, but this operator is not generally a physical state. Its power moments are accessible through collective permutations, whose coherent width grows with moment order. Here we introduce a streaming protocol that processes fresh copies sequentially while retaining one copy and a reusable ancilla. By realizing opposite cyclic permutations on the two subsystems, each measurement record yields unbiased estimators of all moments $p_2,\\ldots,p_K$ through successive outcome products. For an $n$-qubit state, the protocol uses $2n+1$ active qubits independent of $K$ and $O(K\\log K/ε_{\\rm mom}^2)$ copies, matching $Ω(K/ε_{\\rm mom}^2)$ lower bounds up to a logarithmic factor, including a PT-specific isospectral construction. Higher-order moments strengthen thermal-state entanglement certification toward the exact PPT threshold. Simulations cover pure and mixed states, and a cloud quantum processor implements a three-qubit instance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Main text: 16 pages, 5 figures, 1 table; supplementary materials: 16 pages, 2 figures, 1 table; references: 3 pages. Total: 35 pages, 7 figures, 2 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14204","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:49:42Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:49:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-01T03:52:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14207","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14207","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14207","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Auguste","familyName":"Tetenoire","name":"Tetenoire, Auguste","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vijaya Raghavan","familyName":"Kannan","name":"Kannan, Vijaya Raghavan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mikaël","familyName":"Kepenekian","name":"Kepenekian, Mikaël","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arnaud","familyName":"Fihey","name":"Fihey, Arnaud","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Fast and accurate simulation of Raman spectra of gold-organic systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Chemical Physics (physics.chem-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T07:40:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-30T01:03:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1039/d6cp02208a","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Resolving the spectral Raman signature of molecules grafted on a metallic support is often a difficult task, in which quantum chemistry methods allow for precious additional rationalization and signal attributions, especially to probe the formation of a bond with the support. In the specific case of gold-organic architectures based on a Au-C bond, only a limited amount of experimental and theoretical reference data are available in the literature, and Raman simulations based on quantum mechanics quickly become unaffordable with the size of the system. In this work, we evaluate the precision of a cost-efficient DFTB method to simulate Raman spectra of gold-organic systems at different scales, from gold complexes to functionalized gold surfaces. After a validation of the method through a careful comparison of DFTB Raman spectra of organometallic gold(I) and (III) complexes to DFT and experimental reference data, we discuss the case of molecules grafted on gold aggregates. For these simulations, the choice of the model (cluster or periodic surface) appears to be critical, and significant differences arise (positions and intensities of the peaks) when considering a full metallic slab, as allowed by the low computational cost of the method."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14207","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-15T01:49:46Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:49:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-30T04:08:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14693","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14693","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14693","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengxin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Pengxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lihao","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Lihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siyang","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Siyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingdi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jingdi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning Coordinated Preference for Multi-Objective Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T17:55:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T01:02:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-27T22:08:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-29T01:56:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Cooperative multi-objective multi-agent reinforcement learning (MOMARL) models team decision making under multiple, potentially conflicting objectives. In this setting, conflicts arise not only across objectives but also across agents with different observations, roles, and contributions. We propose Preference Coordinated Multi-agent Policy Optimization (PCMA), which learns coordinated agent-specific preferences to enable complementary trade-offs among agents. Theoretically, we formulate cooperative MOMARL as a team-optimal equilibrium problem, and show that, under suitable conditions, preference diversity yields a first-order improvement in the team objective. Experiments on multiple cooperative MOMA environments and a practical traffic-control scenario show that PCMA improves both performance and trade-off coordination."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14693","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T02:00:03Z","registered":"2026-06-15T02:00:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-29T07:11:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14629","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14629","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14629","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianzhe","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Jianzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"When Good Verifiers Go Bad: Silent Negative Transfer in Verifier-Guided VLM Training"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T16:55:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:59:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-21T18:50:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-23T00:08:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-25T18:04:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-29T00:05:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Verifier reliability is not portable across tasks. A verifier-guided self-DPO pipeline with genuine held-out gains on MathVista (+9.6 points on self-training data, +8.0 held out) can be harmful on MMMU. The failure is invisible from the target-task self-training signal: over six learner-verifier configurations, MMMU self-training performance still improves by +3.52 points while held-out performance drops by 1.42 points. We call this silent negative transfer: a verifier validated as useful before can still show signatures of successful self-training after its induced update stops transferring to unseen data. Downstream failure alone cannot diagnose this: poor generalization may mean the verifier induces a wrong learning direction, or that the correct direction itself does not generalize beyond the self-training distribution. We separate these effects via gradient fidelity F, the alignment between verifier-induced and correct training directions, and gradient transferability T, the alignment between correct and held-out directions, giving a conservative Safe-Transfer Margin: positive first-order held-out alignment is certified when arccos(F) + arccos(T) \u0026lt; pi/2. We then introduce Asymmetric Acceptance Gating (AAG), a precision-first filter selecting preference pairs by the verifier's absolute confidence in the response receiving the positive update. On MMMU, AAG raises fidelity from 0.29 to 0.42. In a same-cell analysis, the correct direction stays positively aligned with the held-out direction (T = 0.31), yet raw verification rotates the induced update to negative held-out alignment (-0.13); AAG restores positive alignment (+0.22). Across all six MMMU configurations, AAG beats raw verifier-guided training. Verifier reliability thus means not that it worked before, but that it induces the right update on the task at hand and that this update stays useful beyond the data that generated it."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14629","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:58:42Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:58:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-29T07:11:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14586","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14586","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14586","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shilong","familyName":"Xiang","name":"Xiang, Shilong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zirui","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zirui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengzhi","familyName":"Mao","name":"Mao, Chengzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"S$^2$COPE: Self-Supervised Concept Discovery via Preference Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T16:02:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:57:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-27T00:30:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-29T01:10:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Self-supervised learning can learn useful representations from unlabeled images. Yet these representations remain largely opaque, making it difficult to understand what visual concepts they encode. Can self-supervision instead produce useful visual concepts that are directly understandable to people? We introduce S$^2$COPE, a framework that represents images as sets of concepts expressed in natural language. A vision-language model proposes candidate concepts, and a self-supervised objective evaluates how well each concept describes the source image while distinguishing it from other images. This feedback is used to construct preferences and train the model to propose better concepts. Across eight diverse visual datasets, our self-supervised concept representations transfer without adaptation and improve downstream recognition accuracy over prior interpretable approaches. In medical imaging, our discovered concepts reveal spurious visual cues and enable a more robust classifier. These results show that self-supervision can discover useful, human-readable visual representations from unlabeled images."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14586","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:57:48Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:57:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-29T07:11:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14402","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14402","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14402","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ajay K.","familyName":"Poonia","name":"Poonia, Ajay K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anton","familyName":"Kirch","name":"Kirch, Anton","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joan","familyName":"Ràfols-Ribé","name":"Ràfols-Ribé, Joan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lucrezia","familyName":"Catanzaro","name":"Catanzaro, Lucrezia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anish","familyName":"Rao","name":"Rao, Anish","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karol","familyName":"Kołątaj","name":"Kołątaj, Karol","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vittorio","familyName":"Scardaci","name":"Scardaci, Vittorio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giuseppe","familyName":"Compagnini","name":"Compagnini, Giuseppe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guillermo P.","familyName":"Acuna","name":"Acuna, Guillermo P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ludvig","familyName":"Edman","name":"Edman, Ludvig","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicolò","familyName":"Maccaferri","name":"Maccaferri, Nicolò","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Modifying Electrochemical Doping in Light-Emitting Electrochemical Cells with Gold Nanoparticles"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Applied Physics (physics.app-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T12:39:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-29T00:39:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1002/advs.78120","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Electrochemical doping offers dynamic control of the electronic properties of organic semiconductors, and it is the enabling feature of a range of technologies, including electrochemical transistors, energy-storage devices, light-emitting electrochemical cells (LECs), and bioelectronics. Electrochemical doping is commonly controlled by the selection of the constituents in the active material of the device or the applied voltage bias, but herein we report that the incorporation of Au nanoparticles (Au-NPs) at an electrode interface can constitute an alternative control parameter. The LEC features balanced p- and n-type electrochemical doping that forms a p-n junction doping structure in its active material, and we find that it is possible to reshape this doping profile by incorporating Au-NPs at an electrode interface. Specifically, we establish that the inclusion of neat non-capped Au-NPs at the anodic interface shifts the p-n junction (i.e., the emission zone) away from the anode. In contrast, the inclusion of Au-NPs capped with sodium citrate is found to reverse this behavior, so that the emission zone is instead moved towards the anode. We utilize this control parameter to shift the emission zone towards a position of constructive (destructive) interference, as manifested in a strong increase (decrease) of the LEC emission efficiency. Our findings establish an interfacial strategy for modulating the spatial profile of electrochemical doping and tuning device performance without altering the chemistry of the active material, relying instead on the surface modification of one electrode. This approach is important because it provides a versatile and minimally invasive route to optimize electrochemical devices while preserving the intrinsic properties and formulation of the active material."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14402","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-15T01:53:55Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:53:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-29T07:11:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14397","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14397","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14397","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mykola","familyName":"Vysotskyi","name":"Vysotskyi, Mykola","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runqi","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Runqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Grzegorz","familyName":"Biziel","name":"Biziel, Grzegorz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michal","familyName":"Zakrzewski","name":"Zakrzewski, Michal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sebastian","familyName":"Montagna","name":"Montagna, Sebastian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Damian","familyName":"Rynczak","name":"Rynczak, Damian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shreyansh","familyName":"Padarha","name":"Padarha, Shreyansh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kumail","familyName":"Alhamoud","name":"Alhamoud, Kumail","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihao","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Zihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"William","familyName":"Lugoloobi","name":"Lugoloobi, William","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Rawal","name":"Rawal, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanna","familyName":"Yershova","name":"Yershova, Hanna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taras","familyName":"Rumezhak","name":"Rumezhak, Taras","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guohao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Guohao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fazl","familyName":"Barez","name":"Barez, Fazl","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baoyuan","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Baoyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arkadiusz","familyName":"Drohomirecki","name":"Drohomirecki, Arkadiusz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chris","familyName":"Russell","name":"Russell, Chris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christopher","familyName":"Summerfield","name":"Summerfield, Christopher","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adam","familyName":"Mahdi","name":"Mahdi, Adam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Volodymyr","familyName":"Karpiv","name":"Karpiv, Volodymyr","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philip","familyName":"Torr","name":"Torr, Philip","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adel","familyName":"Bibi","name":"Bibi, Adel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Running the Gauntlet: Hard Agentic Tasks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T12:32:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:46:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-25T10:49:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-26T00:41:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-09T16:27:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-10T01:01:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-13T12:59:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-09-15T01:08:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-09-28T11:24:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-09-29T02:45:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As agentic systems continue to evolve and are widely deployed in real-world scenarios, there is a growing demand to faithfully evaluate their capabilities. However, current benchmarks are typically built on popular applications with relatively simple tasks and focus on a narrow set of capabilities while overlooking broader dimensions, resulting in saturated performance on modern agents and failing to probe their limitations. To this end, we introduce GauntletBench, a web-based benchmark for evaluating agent generalisation in challenging scenarios, focusing on three underexplored capabilities (temporal perception, graphical understanding, and 3D reasoning), across five less-covered professional applications (Video Editor, Workflow Builder, 3D Modeller, Flight Analyser, and Circuit Designer), each with 27 vision-intensive tasks (135 in total). Our benchmark provides a modular pipeline that comprises an environment compatible with both open- and closed-source agent frameworks, a controlled web-based application, a well-structured task suite, and an automated evaluation engine with diverse metrics. Contrary to widespread expectations, our empirical results reveal that frontier agentic systems remain far from achieving human-level performance. Even the state-of-the-art agent achieves only a 28.2% success rate on our GauntletBench, highlighting the limitations in these overlooked capabilities and generalisation. By comparison, non-expert human annotators achieve over 80% success on our challenging yet feasible tasks, revealing the substantial gap between current agent capabilities and those required for complex real-world scenarios."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14397","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:53:49Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:53:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-29T07:11:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13853","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13853","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13853","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chandan","familyName":"Chaudhary","name":"Chaudhary, Chandan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alaaeldein","familyName":"Abdelkader","name":"Abdelkader, Alaaeldein","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yansong","familyName":"Pei","name":"Pei, Yansong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammed","familyName":"Benidris","name":"Benidris, Mohammed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joydeep","familyName":"Mitra","name":"Mitra, Joydeep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Spatial Load Correlation in AI Data-Center-Dominated Power Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T19:32:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-29T03:02:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/pesgm58988.2026.11693422","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The proliferation of large-scale data centers introduces spatially correlated demand profiles that challenge the long-standing assumption of statistical independence of loads in power system analysis. This paper examines the emergence of such load correlations and evaluates their impact on data-center-dominated grids. Analytical derivations reveal that correlated load fluctuations amplify aggregate stochastic disturbances, reduce voltage stability margins through weakened reactive power stiffness, and degrade frequency stability margin by erosion of natural load diversity effects. Real-time digital simulation studies confirm that moderate spatial correlation in distributed data centers produces simultaneous frequency deviations and voltage fluctuations across multiple buses. The findings offer transmission system operators a physics-based perspective to interpret emerging oscillatory phenomena and establish stability planning criteria grounded in measurable load-correlation structures rather than traditional diversity assumptions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"To appear in proceedings of 2026 IEEE Power \u0026amp; Energy Society General Meeting, 19-23 July 2026, Montr\\'eal, Canada"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13853","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-15T01:42:16Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:42:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-29T07:11:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13877","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13877","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13877","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pokuang","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Pokuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaidi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Kaidi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adeesh","familyName":"Desai","name":"Desai, Adeesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Temitope","familyName":"Amosa","name":"Amosa, Temitope","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Davood","familyName":"Soleymanzadeh","name":"Soleymanzadeh, Davood","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiuzhou","familyName":"Lei","name":"Lei, Jiuzhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhao","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yuhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minghui","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Minghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"She","name":"She, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ContactWorld: What Representations Matter for Vision-Tactile Latent World Models in Contact-Rich Manipulation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T20:01:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:10:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-08T02:58:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-09T02:14:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-24T17:42:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-25T01:17:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Contact-rich manipulation poses a fundamental challenge for world models: visual and tactile observations capture different aspects of physical interaction, and their utility depends critically on how this information is represented. We introduce ContactWorld, a controlled benchmark and empirical study of vision-tactile representations across 12 contact-rich manipulation tasks. Using a fixed world-model architecture, training procedure, and planning framework, we examine representation effects through three complementary properties: spatial fidelity, motion coherence, and predictive stability. Point clouds preserve task-relevant geometry and track physical motion more reliably than image observations, helping explain their higher average planning success of 32.1%, compared with 20.7% and 22.0% for wrist- and front-view RGB, respectively. Tactile observations generally reduce long-horizon prediction-error accumulation, but these gains do not translate uniformly into task success. Structured tactile force fields provide the most consistent downstream improvements, with PointCloud+TacFF achieving the highest overall simulated success rate of 36.1%. We further validate these trends through 900 physical trials spanning six tasks, two robot platforms, and three tactile sensing systems. Together, ContactWorld identifies sensory representation as a central design factor in vision--tactile world models and provides empirical guidance for predictive planning in contact-rich manipulation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project website: https://contact-world.github.io"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13877","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:42:49Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:42:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-25T02:56:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14646","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14646","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14646","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yao","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonathan J.","familyName":"Fortney","name":"Fortney, Jonathan J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Laura K.","familyName":"Schaefer","name":"Schaefer, Laura K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Hydrogen and Helium Dissolution, Outgassing, and Loss in Evolving Sub-Neptune Magma Oceans: Examining Demographic Features and Radius Evolution"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Earth and Planetary Astrophysics (astro-ph.EP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T17:08:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T01:00:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-22T19:33:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-24T00:07:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Sub-Neptunes' molten interiors are expected to accommodate large quantities of volatiles, potentially altering their radius evolution. Previous studies have examined this effect in isolation with simplified evolution modeling, often assuming idealized interior and atmospheric conditions. To address this limitation, we introduce SEAMIST, a unified evolution model for sub-Neptunes and super-Earths that self-consistently combines, for the first time, interior structure, cooling, rock/iron solidification, boil-off, photoevaporation, H/He dissolution, and atmospheric composition. SEAMIST considers both a partially soluble case, in which hydrogen partitions into the magma ocean with a fraction set by mantle-envelope boundary conditions, and a fully miscible case, in which hydrogen may fully dissolve into the magma ocean at high temperatures. We identify a novel catastrophic boil-off mechanism, triggered by a positive feedback between hydrogen outgassing and mass loss that can operate billions of years after disk dispersal. In partially soluble models, the impact of H/He dissolution on radius evolution is modest. This contrasts with previous expectations, as we find that increasing hydrogen abundance from outgassing enhances mass-loss efficiency, counterbalancing volatile replenishment from the rock/iron interior. Fully miscible hydrogen, in contrast, significantly enhances envelope survival especially around low-mass stars. Overall, at intermediate to low masses, mass-radius curves from partially soluble models match observed distributions. The fully miscible case predicts a pronounced radius peak and excess planets around low stellar masses that appear inconsistent with current observations, although it reproduces the observed radius ``cliff\" near 4$R_\\oplus$ at higher masses. Our results suggest that high metallicity may explain the cliff, although alternatives cannot be entirely ruled out."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24 pages, 10 figures. in press, ApJ"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14646","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:59:04Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:59:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-24T02:53:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13786","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13786","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13786","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Center of Gravity, Niels Bohr Institute, Copenhagen, Denmark","Max Planck Institute for Gravitational Physics","Institute for Mathematics, Astrophysics and Particle Physics, Radboud University, Nijmegen, The Netherlands"],"givenName":"Ariadna Ribes","familyName":"Metidieri","name":"Metidieri, Ariadna Ribes","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Institute for Mathematics, Astrophysics and Particle Physics, Radboud University, Nijmegen, The Netherlands"],"givenName":"Béatrice","familyName":"Bonga","name":"Bonga, Béatrice","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Institute for Mathematics, Astrophysics and Particle Physics, Radboud University, Nijmegen, The Netherlands","Max Planck Institute for Gravitational Physics","Leibniz University Hannover, Hannover, Germany"],"givenName":"Badri","familyName":"Krishnan","name":"Krishnan, Badri","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Institut de Mathématiques de Bourgogne UMR 5584, Université Bourgogne Europe, CNRS, Dijon, France"],"givenName":"José Luis","familyName":"Jaramillo","name":"Jaramillo, José Luis","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Universality in the Transition from Inspiral to Plunge for Extreme-Mass-Ratio-Inspirals: High-Accuracy Analytic Solutions and Catastrophe Theory"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Astrophysical Phenomena (astro-ph.HE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Mathematical Physics (math-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T18:00:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:04:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-23T14:41:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-24T01:08:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We revisit the transition from inspiral to plunge for extreme mass-ratio inspirals on quasi-circular, inclined orbits in Kerr spacetime from the perspective of catastrophe theory. Our goal is to uncover the mathematical structures underlying the universality of the transition dynamics, which remains governed by the same Painlevé I differential equation as for equatorial inspirals despite the additional complexity. We first analyze the solution of the Painlevé I equation selected by the physical boundary conditions of slowly evolving quasi-circular inspiral at early times. We argue that these conditions uniquely select the tritronquée solution of Painlevé I. We then compare existing high-accuracy analytic approximations of the tritronquée solution with direct numerical integrations of the Painlevé I equation, finding comparable accuracy and improved stability under differentiation and integration for the analytic solution. In the second part of this work, we show that the equilibrium structure of the Kerr radial effective potential admits a natural interpretation in terms of catastrophe theory. Equatorial orbits are associated with the fold catastrophe, while inclined orbits are described by the cusp catastrophe. In both cases, the transition to plunge corresponds to slow evolution across fold lines of the catastrophe manifold, providing a geometric explanation for the universal appearance of the Painlevé I equation in the transition dynamics."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24 pages, 9 figures. Changes in v2: modified title, simplified proof in Appendix C, and corrected some minor typographical errors"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13786","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:40:50Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:40:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-24T02:53:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14489","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14489","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14489","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hugo","familyName":"Catalá","name":"Catalá, Hugo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ezequiel","familyName":"Valero","name":"Valero, Ezequiel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Germán","familyName":"Rodrigo","name":"Rodrigo, Germán","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Physics-Informed Variational Quantum Classifier for Phase Detection in Strongly Correlated Matter"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T14:25:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:51:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-22T15:28:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-23T01:07:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The characterisation of quantum phases in strongly correlated systems is a crucial milestone for the deployment of quantum sensors. In this work, we present a Physics-Informed Variational Quantum Classifier (VQC) designed to detect the topological phase transition between the Fermi polaron quasiparticle and the molecular bound state. Unlike conventional Machine Learning approaches, our quantum architecture is constructed via the Trotterised time-evolution of an effective Hamiltonian, ensuring that the learnable parameters correspond to interpretable physical quantities. We show that the VQC efficiently discovers the optimal interferometric protocol, specifically the evolution time and effective bath interactions required to maximise the visibility of Ramsey fringes, thereby clearly distinguishing the Bose-Einstein Condensate (BEC) and Bardeen-Cooper-Schrieffer (BCS) regimes. Furthermore, we report the validation of this classifier on the QRed superconducting quantum processor (BSC-CNS). Despite the intrinsic hardware noise and decoherence, the VQC preserves the relative ordering of the topological phases. We demonstrate that the physics-informed architecture achieves a linear gate complexity $\\mathcal{O}(N)$, bypassing the exponential memory wall of classical simulation and ensuring scalability to many-body regimes."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14489","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:55:47Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:55:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-23T02:51:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14208","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14208","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14208","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tatsuhiro","familyName":"Misumi","name":"Misumi, Tatsuhiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Real-time pseudo entropy and modular-Hamiltonian correlations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Theory (hep-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Mesoscale and Nanoscale Physics (cond-mat.mes-hall)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T07:44:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:33:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-22T14:13:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-23T00:43:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1103/mmyj-pp8f","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Pseudo entropy is a complex-valued generalization of entanglement entropy defined from a reduced transition matrix. We study the pseudo entropy associated with a real-time transition matrix between an initial pure state and its unitary time evolution. For a subsystem $A$, we show that the short-time behavior of real-time pseudo entropy is governed by the correlation between the physical Hamiltonian $H$ and the modular Hamiltonian $K_A=-\\logρ_A$ of the initial reduced state, $ S_A(t,0)=S_A(0)-it \\langle K_A(H-\\langle H\\rangle)\\rangle + \\mathcal{O}(t^2)$. For Hermitian dynamics, the initial imaginary response is controlled by the symmetrized covariance of $H$ and $K_A$ with an overall minus sign, while the initial real response is governed by their commutator. Thus the imaginary part of real-time pseudo entropy is not merely a branch artifact: it is a time-oriented modular response generated by the correlation between microscopic time evolution and subsystem coarse graining. We clarify the relation of this result to the known first law of pseudo entropy, derive an all-order expression in a Schmidt-diagonal model, recover thermal pseudo entropy as a special case, illustrate the covariance/commutator decomposition in a two-qubit model, and confirm the covariance response in transverse-field Ising-chain quenches, including a finite-size study of a modular susceptibility near the Ising critical region. We discuss how this amplitude-level oriented response can be related to ordinary entropy production, and also give a concrete $\\mathcal{PT}$-symmetric toy-model illustration of the non-Hermitian extension."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 3 figures. v2:minor corrections and references added"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14208","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-15T01:49:47Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:49:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-23T02:51:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14157","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14157","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14157","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paula Joy B.","familyName":"Martinez","name":"Martinez, Paula Joy B.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sebastian Felipe R.","familyName":"Bundoc","name":"Bundoc, Sebastian Felipe R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning Urban Access Costs from Origin-Destination Flows via Inverse Optimal Transport"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T06:29:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:29:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-22T09:02:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-23T00:41:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Cities deliver basic services through mixed public-private facility networks, including schools, clinics, transit providers, and subsidized service points. In these systems, planners often observe where households go, but not the latent cost function through which they trade off factors such as distance, price, and institutional access. We study this urban problem through school choice in the Philippines, where the country's largest national education subsidy is intended to redirect learners from congested public schools to participating private schools. Treating school-to-school enrollment flows as an entropic optimal transport plan, we recover latent choice costs using two complementary inverse optimal transport models: an interpretable distance-banded model with a subsidy term, and a neural cost model trained through a differentiable Sinkhorn forward pass. Applied to 283{,}016 learner trips across 23{,}820 observed flows in the most populated region, the framework estimates a subsidy-equivalent distance, $λ^{(k)}$, interpreted as the kilometers of perceived travel cost offset by the subsidy. The case demonstrates how administrative origin-destination data can be transformed into interpretable planning metrics for accessibility-aware subsidy design, facility siting, and urban service allocation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Oral Presentation. 2026 International Conference on Urban AI"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14157","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:48:42Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:48:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-23T02:51:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14071","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14071","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14071","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ene","familyName":"Meco","name":"Meco, Ene","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingyi","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Yingyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emadeldeen","familyName":"Hamdan","name":"Hamdan, Emadeldeen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adam","familyName":"Watts","name":"Watts, Adam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmet Enis","familyName":"Cetin","name":"Cetin, Ahmet Enis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ShearFuse-UNet: Hadamard, DCT, and Shearlet Transform Fusion for Next-Day Wildfire Spread Prediction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T03:38:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:23:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-21T03:23:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-22T01:32:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose ShearFuse-UNet, a lightweight and computationally efficient deep learning model for next-day wildfire spread prediction from multi-modal satellite data. The model integrates three complementary transform-domain branches inside each encoder block of a U-Net backbone: a 2D Fast Walsh-Hadamard Transform (WHT) branch, a 2D Discrete Cosine Transform (DCT) branch, and a cone-adapted digital Shearlet residual branch. The WHT and DCT branches establish orthogonal latent spaces with learnable spectral scaling and fixed soft-thresholding, while the Shearlet branch provides anisotropic, multi-directional feature decomposition that explicitly encodes the elongated edge structures characteristic of fire fronts. A learned SpectralFusion gate adaptively combines the WHT and DCT responses, and the Shearlet reconstruction is added as a residual. This three-branch design bears a loose structural analogy to transformer self-attention: the WHT and DCT branches provide complementary spectral representations that are adaptively fused, while the Shearlet branch contributes directional content through a residual pathway. Unlike self-attention, the proposed design relies on fixed mathematical transforms rather than learned projection operators, reducing parameter count and computational cost. Evaluated on the WildfireSpreadTS dataset, ShearFuse-UNet achieves an F1 score of 0.596 with only 267k parameters, outperforming a ResNet18-based U-Net (14M parameters, F1 = 0.589) and demonstrating a highly favorable accuracy-efficiency trade-off. Results on the Google Next-Day Wildfire Spread dataset further validate these findings across a different benchmark."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14071","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:46:54Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:46:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-22T04:52:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14053","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14053","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14053","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shijie","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Shijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiangfeng","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Jiangfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengfei","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Pengfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Hybrid Uncertainty Sensitivity Analysis Based on the HSIC for High-Dimensional Responses with Aleatory--Epistemic Separation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"62P30, 62H20, 65C20","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T02:58:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:22:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-20T00:53:50Z","dateType":"Withdrawn","dateInformation":"v2; The authors have identified several issues in the current version of the manuscript that require further examination and correction. The paper is therefore withdrawn to allow the authors to address these issues before submitting a revised version"},{"date":"2026-09-22T00:53:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Quantifying the influence of hybrid aleatory and epistemic uncertainties on high-dimensional system responses remains a major challenge in global sensitivity analysis (GSA). Existing Hilbert--Schmidt Independence Criterion (HSIC)-based approaches are primarily restricted to single-output settings and lack a rigorous decomposition of heterogeneous uncertainty sources and their interactions. To address this limitation, a novel double-space tensor-product RKHS framework is proposed for sensitivity analysis under hybrid uncertainty. By constructing factorized kernels over both the latent input space and the multidimensional output space, a concurrent double Möbius inversion is derived to orthogonally decompose the global dependence measure into pure aleatory effects, pure epistemic effects, and their interaction contributions. The resulting dimension-wise sensitivity indices preserve the uncertainty attribution structure across all output dimensions. To satisfy the independence assumptions required by the decomposition, an auxiliary-variable representation based on the inverse probability integral transform is introduced, enabling the treatment of hierarchical uncertainties and Copula-induced correlations within a unified latent space. A fully vectorized single-loop implementation is further developed to avoid the computational burden of nested Monte Carlo simulation. Statistical significance and estimation uncertainty are quantified through permutation testing and Bootstrap confidence intervals. Numerical studies on a modified multi-output Ishigami function and an aerodynamic pressure-field problem demonstrate the accuracy, scalability, and practical applicability of the proposed framework."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"The authors have identified several issues in the current version of the manuscript that require further examination and correction. The paper is therefore withdrawn to allow the authors to address these issues before submitting a revised version"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14053","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:46:32Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:46:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-22T04:52:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13726","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13726","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13726","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Slava G.","familyName":"Turyshev","name":"Turyshev, Slava G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"High-Precision Relativistic Time Scales for Mars Surface and Orbital Clocks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Earth and Planetary Astrophysics (astro-ph.EP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Instrumentation and Methods for Astrophysics (astro-ph.IM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T09:29:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:02:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-20T03:24:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-22T00:57:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We develop a Mars-centered post-Newtonian framework for relating barycentric coordinate time, Mars-centered coordinate time, a conventional Mars surface time scale, and the proper times of landed and orbiting clocks. The construction follows the International Astronomical Union BCRS/TCB formalism, introduces Areocentric Coordinate Time (TCA), and writes each clock transformation as a secular rate plus zero-mean periodic terms. Terms are retained when their fractional-frequency amplitude exceeds 5e-18 or their one-way accumulated timing amplitude exceeds 0.1 ps. The numerical realization uses the GMM-3 Mars gravity field through degree and order 120, point-mass tides from the Sun, Phobos, and Deimos with origin and dipole terms removed, and bounds on omitted local c^-4 and external-perturber terms. Representative low-Mars-orbit, areostationary, Phobos-/Deimos-distance, and highly elliptical relay regimes are evaluated. Relative to the adopted Mars surface scale, a 300 km near-polar clock is slower by 4.56 microseconds per day, while areostationary and Deimos-distance clocks are faster by 9.13 and 9.52 microseconds per day. The leading Mars-J2 timing line is about 87 ps at 300 km altitude and remains several ps near areostationary radius for inclined or librating areosynchronous cases; perihelion-scaled solar tides become retained sub-ps terms in high relay orbits. The result is a reference-system and model-retention framework, not a final operational Mars Time Ephemeris. A realized sub-ps system still requires a selected planetary ephemeris, Mars orientation and seasonal-gravity model, spacecraft orbit determination, calibrated link delays, and covariance analysis. Time-variable low-degree gravity from seasonal CO2 exchange is a leading surface-realization term and must be modeled, monitored, or empirically bounded before sub-ps Mars surface-scale claims are made."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"33 pages, 8 figures, 11 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13726","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:39:33Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:39:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-22T04:52:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13812","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13812","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13812","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John R.","familyName":"Graham","name":"Graham, John R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Campbell R.","familyName":"Harvey","name":"Harvey, Campbell R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manish","familyName":"Jha","name":"Jha, Manish","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CFOs Meet LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computational Finance (q-fin.CP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"General Finance (q-fin.GN)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T18:32:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:06:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-21T14:49:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-22T02:07:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Business sentiment is a closely watched economic signal, but measuring it is slow and costly: surveys typically reach only a few hundred firms, arrive periodically, and take time to compile. We show that large language models hold the potential to address these shortcomings. We prompt an LLM to role-play as the CFO of a specific company on a specific date, for every public-company CFO who responded to the Duke--Federal Reserve CFO Survey between 2002 and 2025, and answer a question about economy-wide optimism. The LLM-generated optimism score predicts the individual CFO's actual answer, even in specifications that include firm and year-quarter fixed effects as well as a control variable measuring the human CFO's lagged response. Accuracy increases with the information provided to the LLM, and the relation persists under quarterly aggregation. We find the same patterns hold for two other questions measuring CFO expectations: the respondent's optimism about their own firm and their expectation of own-firm revenues. With appropriate conditioning, LLMs may in the future be able to serve as digital twins of executives, offering scalable, high-frequency expectations data for financial research and policy."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"21 pages, 4 tables, 1 figure"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13812","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:41:23Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:41:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-22T04:52:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13986","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13986","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13986","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elchanan","familyName":"Nafcha","name":"Nafcha, Elchanan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Nodal degeneration of chiral algebras II: Local structure and chiral Zhu algebras"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Algebra (math.QA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Mathematical Physics (math-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Algebraic Geometry (math.AG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Representation Theory (math.RT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T00:04:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:16:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-18T10:29:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-21T00:43:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Given a universal factorization algebra $\\mathcal{A}$, we constructed in our previous paper a derived associative algebra $\\mathfrak{Z}_{\\mathcal{A}}^0$, together with chiral modules $\\\\mathfrak{Z}_{\\mathcal{A}}^+$ and $\\mathfrak{Z}_{\\mathcal{A}}^-$ associated to a puncture, and a chiral bimodule $\\mathfrak{Z}_{\\mathcal{A}}$ associated to a node. Furthermore, these constructions assemble to a factorization $\\mathcal{A}$-module over any family of nodal punctured curves. In this paper, we show that in the case where $\\mathcal{A}$ is constructed from a quasi-conformal vertex algebra $V$, the zeroth homology algebra $H^0\\mathfrak{Z}_{\\mathcal{A}}$ is naturally isomorphic to Zhu's associative algebra $A(V)$, and we identify $H^0\\mathfrak{Z}_{\\mathcal{A}}$ with the bimodule underlying the mode-transition algebra of Damiolini-Gibney-Krashen. We also give an explicit description of the smoothing module $H^0\\tilde{\\mathfrak{Z}}_{\\mathcal{C}}$ which describes the deformation of $H^0\\mathfrak{Z}_{\\mathcal{A}}$ which we attach to a smoothing family of a nodal curve. We therefore get a geometric interpretation of the Zhu algebra and the mode-transition algebra, as the integration of a factorization algebra over a certain compactification of configuration spaces of punctured nodal curves."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Fixed typos, updated abstract"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13986","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:45:06Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:45:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-21T02:28:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14167","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14167","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14167","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ignacio","familyName":"Barros","name":"Barros, Ignacio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michaël","familyName":"Cadilhac","name":"Cadilhac, Michaël","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guillermo A.","familyName":"Pérez","name":"Pérez, Guillermo A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"NEXP-Completeness of Existential Presburger Arithmetic with Divisibility"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Logic in Computer Science (cs.LO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Complexity (cs.CC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T06:49:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:31:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-28T19:32:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-30T01:14:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-19T10:03:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-22T00:01:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-17T16:50:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-09-18T01:13:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We prove that satisfiability for existential Presburger arithmetic with divisibility (EPAD) is NEXP-hard. Together with the known NEXP upper bound, this establishes NEXP-completeness. The lower bound is obtained by encoding succinct Boolean formulas whose satisfying assignments may have exponential length. A central difficulty is to represent, within a polynomial-size EPAD formula, the exponentially long integers arising in this encoding.\n To address this difficulty, we introduce fixed-width arithmetic logic circuits (ALCs), whose gates perform addition, multiplication, and bit shifts. We show that polynomial-size uniform ALC families compute exactly the functions in FPSPACE, while their succinct exponential-size counterpart computes exactly the functions in FEXP. In both cases, the computed functions admit polynomial-size functional definitions in EPAD. This provides the compressed arithmetic needed for the lower-bound reduction.\n The same construction yields NEXP-hardness for nonerasing word equations with Presburger length constraints over a fixed two-letter alphabet. We also study the elimination of divisibility constraints by enumerating their possible quotients. Earlier work identified an NP fragment in which the variables can be ordered so that divisibility dependencies always move forward through the order. We introduce a different fragment, called merge-absorptive, in which bounded-quotient divisibilities can successively connect all variable components. This fragment is polynomial-time recognizable and admits complete finite-quotient elimination, yet its satisfiability problem remains NEXP-complete. Finally, we show that the elimination process may necessarily produce equations with exponentially large coefficients."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14167","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:48:55Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:48:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-18T02:49:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13866","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13866","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13866","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Efe","familyName":"İzbudak","name":"İzbudak, Efe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kadri İlker","familyName":"Berktav","name":"Berktav, Kadri İlker","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AKSZ Construction for Shifted Contact Structures"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Algebraic Geometry (math.AG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Symplectic Geometry (math.SG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"14A20, 14A30, 14F08","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T19:46:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:09:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-17T16:12:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-18T01:10:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper establishes the AKSZ theorem for shifted contact structures and its applications. In brief, to resolve certain obstructions, we first define the quotient mapping stack as the quotient of the symplectified mapping space by the constant multiplicative group action. We then prove that if $X$ is an $n$-shifted contact derived Artin stack and $Y$ is an $\\mathcal{O}$-compact, $d$-oriented derived stack, the quotient mapping stack $$[\\mathrm{Map}(Y, \\widetilde{X})/\\mathbb{G}_m]$$ admits an $(n-d)$-shifted contact structure.\n In addition, by formalizing the derived analogue of the graded contact AKSZ formalism, we also introduce the notion of weak shifted contact structures in derived algebraic geometry and prove that under global trivialization of the contact line bundle, the unmodified mapping stack inherits a weak contact structure.\n Extending this setup to spaces with boundary, we demonstrate that derived fillings naturally induce Legendrian morphisms between quotient mapping stacks, and that topological gluing of cobordisms evaluates to derived Legendrian intersections. Furthermore, we trace the transgression of the canonical shifted $1$-form to prove that our quotient mapping stacks satisfy the derived Classical Master Equation (CME).\n As applications of our quotient mapping stack formalism, we define the derived analogues of specific topological field theories, including the Jacobi, Courant-Jacobi, and Loop Space Sigma Models. Next, we establish the contact analogue of the PTVV theorem for moduli spaces of perfect complexes. Finally, by composing this geometric construction with the perverse linearization in a companion paper, we elevate these moduli spaces to generate Cohomological Contact Extended Topological Field Theories."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"v2: Contact versions of PTVV moduli theorems added as applications, minor edits to the ETFT result. 20 pages, all comments welcome! :)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13866","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:42:35Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:42:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-18T02:49:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14065","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14065","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14065","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arani","familyName":"Maiti","name":"Maiti, Arani","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sauvik","familyName":"Roy","name":"Roy, Sauvik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nirmalya","familyName":"Ghosh","name":"Ghosh, Nirmalya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ayan","familyName":"Banerjee","name":"Banerjee, Ayan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Subhasish Dutta","familyName":"Gupta","name":"Gupta, Subhasish Dutta","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Probing the Broken Spatial Symmetry of a Stratified Medium with Structured Light"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optics (physics.optics)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T03:21:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:23:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-16T01:54:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-17T00:21:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study theoretically near-symmetric resonant stratified media to show how a tiny broken spatial symmetry can effectively be probed by structured light with or without orbital angular momentum. This is achieved by examining both the in-plane and out of plane Goos-Hanchen and Imbert Fedorov shifts, respectively, in the reflected light, magnified by resonant enhancement and when needed with weak value amplification. We show that non-reciprocity in reflection for illumination from opposite ends can result in different shifts, even to the extent of shifts with opposite signs for tiny imbalance resulting from the broken symmetry. Our methodology is applicable to any general stratified medium with homogeneous and isotropic constituent layers being responsive to any entity that may cause such symmetry breaking - including temperature, refractive index, displacement etc. in the near symmetric case. This may have possible use for sensing applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 6 figures, Comments are appreciated"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14065","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:46:47Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:46:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-17T02:46:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13901","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13901","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13901","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jafar","familyName":"Bakhshaliyev","name":"Bakhshaliyev, Jafar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Niels","familyName":"Landwehr","name":"Landwehr, Niels","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SpikF-GO: Spiking Fourier Graph Operators for Multivariate Time Series Forecasting"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Neural and Evolutionary Computing (cs.NE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T20:48:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-17T01:11:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1007/978-3-032-37664-0_3","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Spiking Neural Networks (SNNs) have emerged as an energy-efficient alternative to conventional neural networks, demonstrating strong performance in computer vision and robotics. More recently, SNNs have been applied to time series forecasting (TSF), with methods exploring spiking temporal backbones, spike-compatible positional encodings, Fourier-domain processing, and redesigned neuron dynamics. However, existing SNN forecasting approaches process variables independently, lacking explicit mechanisms for modeling inter-variable dependencies. This is a critical limitation in multivariate settings, where cross-variable correlations carry substantial predictive information. We propose Spiking Fourier Graph Operators (SpikF-GO), which addresses this gap by combining a hypervariate graph formulation in which every scalar observation becomes a graph node with spike-driven spectral processing. SpikF-GO introduces a Hard Concrete frequency gate for learnable sparse frequency selection and a Complex LIF gate that applies independent spiking neurons to real and imaginary Fourier components, preserving binary, event-driven computation throughout the spectral domain. We further present a variant incorporating Central Pattern Generator-based positional encodings for stronger long-range temporal modeling. Evaluated on eight benchmarks under a unified experimental protocol, SpikF-GO achieves the best average rank among all SNN methods and outperforms its ANN counterpart, FourierGNN, at reduced energy cost. SpikF-GO maintains competitive accuracy even at substantially smaller embedding dimensions, thereby achieving significant energy reductions. To our knowledge, this is among the first works to bring graph-based multivariate modeling into the spiking domain for TSF and the first to provide a unified comparison across SNN forecasting architectures under a common experimental protocol."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"23 pages, 2 figures, 11 tables. Accepted for presentation at ECML PKDD 2026. Code: https://github.com/jafarbakhshaliyev/SpikF-GO"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13901","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-15T01:43:20Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:43:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-17T02:46:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14582","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14582","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14582","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pollob Chandra","familyName":"Ray","name":"Ray, Pollob Chandra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sabah Binte","familyName":"Noor","name":"Noor, Sabah Binte","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fazlul Hasan","familyName":"Siddiqui","name":"Siddiqui, Fazlul Hasan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Temporal Planning Framework for Disruption Aware Dynamic Route Optimization in Heterogeneous Railway Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T16:00:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:57:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-14T20:51:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-16T00:42:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1108/rs-06-2026-0047","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Efficient route optimization play a vital role in ensuring both safety and punctuality in railway operations. It is very crucial particularly in heterogeneous multi-gauge railway networks with varying train speed, stopping pattern, infrastructure compatibility constraints increase coordination complexity. In single-track systems these challenges are further intensify due to all trains to share the same track and requires frequent track switching.Stochastic disruptions events including blocked tracks, blocked trains, engine failure and speed slowdowns introduces additional unpredictability in operations and deviate the timetable. However, existing studies predominantly focuses on high-level timetabling, omitting operational details such as track switching coordination. As a result leaving decision to human operators, increasing safety risks into railway operations. This study proposes a framework based on temporal planning for dynamic route optimization and disruption management in heterogeneous railway systems. The framework formulates railway operations as a temporal planning problem using PDDL 2.1 with explicitly modeling gauge compatibility constraints and diverse disruption scenarios. It generates conflict-free timestamped operational plans specifying both optimized schedules and executable action sequences. To evaluate the proposed framework, we developed a benchmark problem set with 200 instances using up to 1,000 track points and 120 trains. Two state-of-the-art temporal planners and a plan validator were employed to assessed the framework. The experimental results demonstrate that the framework effectively generates temporal operational plans for heterogeneous railway systems and handles multi-gauge constraints, disruptions, and reduces dependence on manual decision making."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14582","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-15T01:57:43Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:57:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-16T02:45:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14624","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14624","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14624","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jia-Xin","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Jia-Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiuwen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiuwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alex","familyName":"Zunger","name":"Zunger, Alex","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Spin disorder competing with positional symmetry breaking governs the metal-insulator behavior in oxide paramagnets"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computational Physics (physics.comp-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T16:47:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:59:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-14T05:11:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-15T01:35:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Numerous transition-metal oxides have low-temperature, long-range-ordered antiferromagnetic (AFM) states that are generally insulating, and high-temperature, disordered paramagnetic (PM) phases. The latter can be either insulating (predicted here for NaFeO3), or metallic (predicted here and previously observed in NaOsO3). Similar distinctions have been traditionally affected in strongly correlated models by the value used for Coulomb repulsion U. Here we show an alternative, strong-correlation-free (U=0) view suggesting that the distinction between insulating and metallic PM phases is governed by the competition between local magnetic moment disorder and the polymorphous distribution of off-center atomic displacements. Such parameter-free, energy-lowering symmetry breaking density functional calculations provide a framework for understanding metal-insulator behaviors across different quantum materials in terms of measurable local structural and magnetic parameters."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 4 figures, 1 table"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14624","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:58:36Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:58:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-15T04:50:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14584","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14584","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14584","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanlu","familyName":"Lian","name":"Lian, Yanlu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Xue","name":"Xue, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Minimal Covering Bodies and a Minkowski-Type Criterion for Lattice Coverings"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Metric Geometry (math.MG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"11H31, 52C17, 52B20, 52C22","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T16:01:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:57:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-05T13:37:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-07T01:39:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-13T08:49:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-15T01:02:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study convex bodies whose translates by a fixed lattice cover space and for which every proper convex subbody loses this property. In three dimensions, the convex hull of independent translates of the six Kuhn tetrahedra always gives a lattice covering. We give a geometric proof using auxiliary tetrahedra and the parity of the covering multiplicity. We also prove polytopality and boundary restrictions for minimal covering bodies, and show that every three-dimensional parallelohedron admits a Kuhn representation with respect to its face-to-face tiling lattice. Constructions from Reeve tetrahedra give non-symmetric minimal covering bodies with eight vertices in dimension three and centrally symmetric ones with sixteen vertices in dimension four, with unbounded volumes for the integer covering lattice. They have pairwise distinct arithmetic contact types, which record lattice contacts and the faces containing them. The covering theorem also gives a finite intersection criterion for a prescribed lattice basis, with three intersection tests in the centrally symmetric case."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"29 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14584","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:57:46Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:57:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-15T04:50:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13989","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13989","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13989","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alef Iury Siqueira","familyName":"Ferreira","name":"Ferreira, Alef Iury Siqueira","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lucas Rafael Stefanel","familyName":"Gris","name":"Gris, Lucas Rafael Stefanel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luiz Fernando de Araújo","familyName":"Vidal","name":"Vidal, Luiz Fernando de Araújo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Frederico Santos","familyName":"de Oliveira","name":"de Oliveira, Frederico Santos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christopher Dane","familyName":"Shulby","name":"Shulby, Christopher Dane","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anderson da Silva","familyName":"Soares","name":"Soares, Anderson da Silva","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arlindo Rodrigues Galvão","familyName":"Filho","name":"Filho, Arlindo Rodrigues Galvão","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mask, Sample, Revise: A Revisable CTMC Inference Stack for Guided Discrete Flow Matching Text-to-Speech"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T00:13:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:16:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-13T21:53:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-15T01:22:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent alignment-free non-autoregressive (NAR) text-to-speech (TTS) models formulate synthesis as a conditional infilling task, bypassing explicit duration predictors and external aligners. When speech is represented with neural codec tokens, the infilling problem becomes discrete, making Discrete Flow Matching (DFM), a Continuous-Time Markov Chain (CTMC) framework for discrete generation, a natural fit. However, inference-time control for stable low-step conditional infilling remains underexplored. We propose Mask, Sample, Revise, an inference-time CTMC stack for alignment-free DFM-TTS. The stack combines predictor-free guidance to strengthen text conditioning, prompt-matched conditional coupling to align the probability path with the acoustic prompt, and SC-ReMask, a schedule-constrained remasking mechanism that introduces token-to-mask transitions so de-masking decisions can be revised. These components require no post-hoc fine-tuning and operate in a single tau-leaping sampler. Controlled ablations show that this stack improves intelligibility and robustness in the low-NFE prompted setting, outperforming unguided and guidance-only samplers with substantially more steps."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13989","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:45:10Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:45:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-15T04:50:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14520","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14520","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14520","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexey S.","familyName":"Mironov","name":"Mironov, Alexey S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Wind vector retrieval from miniaturized wave-enabled sea-surface drifters"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Atmospheric and Oceanic Physics (physics.ao-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T14:51:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:53:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-11T09:09:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-14T00:34:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Open-ocean in situ winds are sparse, limiting the calibration and validation of satellite wind products. We evaluate wind-vector retrieval from MELODI (Miniaturized Electronics Lagrangian Oceanographic DrIfter), a compact freely drifting GNSS/IMU wave buoy. Wind speed is inferred from the shape of the measured wave acceleration spectrum. A regularized regression with a gated nonlinear correction (Wind Inversion using Tikhonov Regularization, WITR), trained on scatterometer winds and evaluated by leave-one-buoy-out cross-validation against ASCAT MetOp-B/C and HY-2B/C, gives a root-mean-square difference (RMSD) of 0.91 m/s. Symbolic regression distills the trained model into compact spectrum-only and motion-enhanced equations suitable for onboard use; the reduced drag law gives 0.93-0.94 m/s. Wind direction is estimated, without any fitted reference-wind regression, from IMU-derived directional wave moments in the 0.60-0.90 Hz wind-sea band, with a circular mean absolute angular difference (MAAD) of 9.4 degrees against ASCAT. These differences are comparable to published scatterometer-moored-buoy comparisons and improve substantially on the traditional single-band Toba inversion. Validation is strongest from 4 to 14 m/s and sparse above approximately 16 m/s; stronger winds are assigned low confidence. The results show that small wave-sensing drifters can provide useful wind-vector observations for satellite calibration and validation, although stronger-wind sampling and independent anemometer validation remain priorities."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"54 pages, 9 figures, 10 tables. Submitted to the Journal of Atmospheric and Oceanic Technology"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14520","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:56:26Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:56:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-14T02:22:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13992","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13992","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13992","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Santiago","familyName":"Garcia","name":"Garcia, Santiago","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Group Quantization and Mellin Representations of the Heston Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Mathematical Finance (q-fin.MF)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Pricing of Securities (q-fin.PR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T00:24:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:16:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-09T05:48:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-11T00:01:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We develop an Affine Holonomy Group Quantization framework for the Heston stochastic volatility model. The Heston affine pricing symbol is decomposed into a finite symplectic quadratic sector and a complementary holonomy sector, leading to a Poincare-Cartan form whose characteristic flow yields the affine Riccati dynamics. Momentum polarization gives a Mellin pricing representation, while the Riccati equation admits a projective linearization. The resulting option pricing formula is validated numerically against the standard Heston solution, and the Black-Scholes model is recovered as a limiting case."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Substantially revised version. The geometric construction has been reorganized and clarified, with a sharper separation between the finite symplectic and holonomy sectors. The Cartan and characteristic structures, momentum polarization, and the projective Riccati formulation, numerical validation, and Black-Scholes reduction have been expanded"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13992","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:45:14Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:45:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-11T02:41:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14411","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14411","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14411","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Piotr","familyName":"Mirowski","name":"Mirowski, Piotr","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ben","familyName":"Wedin","name":"Wedin, Ben","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Reinald Kim","familyName":"Amplayo","name":"Amplayo, Reinald Kim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rich","familyName":"Galt","name":"Galt, Rich","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Duncan","familyName":"Williams","name":"Williams, Duncan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rida","familyName":"Qadri","name":"Qadri, Rida","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jaume","familyName":"Sanchez-Elias","name":"Sanchez-Elias, Jaume","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Erin","familyName":"Drake-Kajioka","name":"Drake-Kajioka, Erin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sian","familyName":"Gooding","name":"Gooding, Sian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lucia","familyName":"Lopez-Rivilla","name":"Lopez-Rivilla, Lucia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joao G. M.","familyName":"Araujo","name":"Araujo, Joao G. M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lion","familyName":"Schulz","name":"Schulz, Lion","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Satinder","familyName":"Baveja","name":"Baveja, Satinder","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shakir","familyName":"Mohamed","name":"Mohamed, Shakir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Edward","familyName":"Grefenstette","name":"Grefenstette, Edward","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Laura","familyName":"Rimell","name":"Rimell, Laura","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Richard","familyName":"Evans","name":"Evans, Richard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Fabula: Building a Narrative Storytelling Sidekick with the Writers' Community"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T12:46:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:47:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-10T12:38:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-11T00:49:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We design and evaluate Fabula, an interactive app for fiction writers. Fabula uses detailed narrative plans informed by general narratological theory. Stories are structured hierarchically into scenes and beats that can be (re)generated and revised at script and story plan level. Using participatory AI, we critically evaluate and improve Fabula with casual and published writers, via design interviews and writing sessions with 42 experts, and large-scale internal and external testing. We interrogate our design choices: (1) whether a language model-based auto-evaluator, optimized on human experts' preferences, can improve story quality, (2) whether users want UI that exposes the detailed narrative plan alongside the story script, (3) to what extent our narratology assumptions fit localised storytelling traditions and serve screenwriters or playwrights, and (4) whether convergent iteration over the story plan supports writers' creativity. Building on critical feedback and concerns, we use Fabula as a cultural probe in adversarial design, and identify potentials for writing feedback and for interactive storytelling."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"41 pages, 10 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14411","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:54:07Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:54:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-11T02:41:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13923","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13923","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13923","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Montero","name":"Montero, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"César","familyName":"Aybar","name":"Aybar, César","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miguel D.","familyName":"Mahecha","name":"Mahecha, Miguel D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luis","familyName":"Gómez-Chova","name":"Gómez-Chova, Luis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards an open registry of Earth observation instruments"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Databases (cs.DB)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Digital Libraries (cs.DL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T21:21:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-11T00:29:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.5194/isprs-archives-l-4-w1-2026-167-2026","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Earth observation (EO) is essential to understanding the Earth system, enabling the transformation of planetary properties into measurable variables that can be analysed, compared, and modelled. In recent decades, EO capabilities have grown rapidly, accompanied by an even faster expansion in the number and variety of available EO instruments. Today, EO includes instruments deployed on satellites, airborne platforms, and terrestrial or in-situ systems. However, despite this proliferation of instruments, users often lack a single, reliable source describing their existence and key characteristics. Although existing data catalogues have substantially improved dataset discovery, they primarily describe data products rather than providing persistent, curated metadata about the instruments that produced them. Here we present Awesome Earth Observation Instruments, an open, standardized, and community-oriented registry providing machine-readable metadata for EO instruments. The catalogue is hosted on GitHub and allows contributors to submit instrument metadata following a common schema. The schema combines a lightweight core with modular extensions covering spectral, geometric, and data access-related metadata, enabling both standardization and flexibility across diverse EO systems. All submissions undergo automated schema validation and human review. Because the schema is open, versioned, and extensible, the catalogue can continuously evolve as new instruments and metadata requirements emerge. This facilitates the discovery, interpretation, and analysis of EO data in light of instrument characteristics. To support programmatic access and interoperability, we further envisage an API for integration within common EO analysis environments. The catalogue is openly available at https://github.com/awesome-spectral-indices/awesome-earth-observation-instruments."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13923","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-15T01:43:47Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:43:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-11T02:41:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13720","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13720","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13720","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elisabetta","familyName":"Rocchetti","name":"Rocchetti, Elisabetta","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alfio","familyName":"Ferrara","name":"Ferrara, Alfio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Refusal Beyond a Single Direction: A Preliminary Comparison of Diff-in-Means and INLP"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T06:58:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:01:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-10T11:37:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-11T00:45:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Arditi et al. (2024) has shown that refusal in safety fine-tuned chat models is mediated by a single linear direction in the residual stream, recoverable by a difference-in-means (DiM) of harmful and harmless activations. We compare DiM-based interventions (activation addition and directional ablation) with two interventions derived from Iterative Nullspace Projection (INLP)-nullspace projection and counterfactual flipping-on five open-weight chat models, asking whether INLP can match DiM at steering refusal and whether its richer parameterisation yields more tweakable interventions. INLP counterfactual flipping is competitive with DiM directional ablation on refusal suppression, while nullspace projection is weaker on most models. Applying each method at the layer selected by the other shows that flipping's competitiveness is robust to this change while directional ablation's is not, suggesting that DiM's apparent edge is partly a property of its own selection procedure. Restricting INLP to the leading directions of the extracted subspace preserves most of the suppression effect at near-baseline perplexity, giving a tunable capability. Geometrically, the two INLP interventions land in qualitatively different regions of activation space: nullspace projection collapses transformed activations between the harmful and harmless clusters, while counterfactual flipping moves them into the opposite cluster, suggesting that the model encodes the absence of a concept differently from its opposit--an intriguing distinction that warrants further investigation in future work."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13720","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:39:26Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:39:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-11T02:41:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14529","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14529","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14529","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bidushi","familyName":"Sharma","name":"Sharma, Bidushi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dhiren Kumar","familyName":"Basnet","name":"Basnet, Dhiren Kumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On some Non-Permutations of Quadratic Extension of Finite Field"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Number Theory (math.NT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"11T06, 11T23, 12E10","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T15:02:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:53:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-09T10:37:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-10T00:40:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this article, we study polynomials over $\\mathbb{F}_{q^2}$ that do not permute $\\mathbb{F}_{q^2}$. More precisely, we characterize polynomials of the forms $x^q + b x^2 + c x + d$ and $x^{q+1} + b x^q + c x + d$ over $\\mathbb{F}_{q^2}$ according to whether they are permutation or non-permutation polynomials. To this end, we determine the exact number of zeros of these polynomials using existing results on certain special Weil sums."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14529","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:56:37Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:56:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-10T02:33:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13945","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13945","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13945","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziqing","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ziqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lili","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Lili","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaize","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Kaize","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LatentDx: Latent Multi-Agent Communication for Cross-Hospital Rare-Disease Diagnosis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T22:11:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:13:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-09T01:23:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-10T00:16:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Rare diseases affect over $300$ million patients across more than $7{,}000$ conditions, yet no single hospital encounters enough cases of any one condition for reliable diagnosis. Cross-hospital collaboration could help by allowing a diagnosing institution to use distributed, case-specific diagnostic evidence, but privacy regulations restrict the transmission of identifiable clinical text across institutional boundaries. This setting raises two challenges: existing medical agent systems often rely on textual evidence exchange, while raw latent states such as hidden states and KV caches may still reveal prompt-derived clinical content. We introduce LatentDx, a latent multi-agent communication framework in which hospital agents keep private clinical records and retrieved cases local, and send compact latent KV blocks to a host agent for rare-disease diagnosis. LatentDx supports two deployment settings: same-backbone hospital agents use latent KV distillation, while hospitals with different LLM backbones use cross-family latent alignment. On CrossRare-Bench, a self-built large-scale rare-disease benchmark with hospital-level partitions, LatentDx improves cross-hospital diagnostic performance while reducing reconstructable clinical content relative to raw-latent communication baselines."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13945","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:44:15Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:44:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-10T02:33:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14644","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14644","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14644","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiu-Lei","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Xiu-Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hai-Peng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hai-Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei-Hong","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Wei-Hong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chu-Wen","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Chu-Wen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eulogio","familyName":"Oset","name":"Oset, Eulogio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"In search for signals of the $D\\bar{D}$ bound state $X(3700)$ from study of the $B^+ \\to D^+ D^- K^+$, $B^0 \\to D^+ D^- K^0$ and $Λ_b \\to D^+ D^- Λ$ reactions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Phenomenology (hep-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T17:07:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T01:00:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-05T08:19:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-09T00:27:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1103/6w2t-tpmv","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We perform a theoretical study of the $B^+ \\to D^+ D^- K^+$, $B^0 \\to D^+ D^- K^0$ and $Λ_b \\to D^+ D^- Λ$ reactions by looking at the production mechanisms, with special emphasis in the final state interaction of the charmed mesons, which gives rise to the $X(3930)$, coupling strongly to $D_s\\bar{D}_s$, and another state that we call $X(3700)$, coupling strongly to $D \\bar D$ that qualifies as a $D \\bar D$ bound state. The combined study of all these reactions shows that the final state interaction responsible for the production of these resonances is more important in the $B^+ \\to D^+ D^- K^+$ reaction than in the $Λ_b \\to D^+ D^- Λ$ one. We have taken this into account and shown that normalizing the $D^+ D^-$ mass distributions to the same value at the peak of the $ψ(3770)$ production, the two mass distributions are quite different in a range of 10 MeV above the $D^+ D^-$ threshold, with a value about an order of magnitude bigger for the $B^+ \\to D^+ D^- K^+$ reaction than for the $Λ_b \\to D^+ D^- Λ$ one. The $D^+D^-$ mass distribution by itself also shows an enhancement of about one order of magnitude in the presence of the $X(3700)$ with respect to a calculation where this resonance is absent. We make a call to measure these magnitudes in the coming LHCb upgrades, which would bring great support to the existence of the bound $D^+ D^-$ state."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 23 figures, 1 table"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14644","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-15T01:59:01Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:59:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-09T06:06:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14704","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14704","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14704","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rixin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Rixin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eugene","familyName":"Chiang","name":"Chiang, Eugene","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Edges of Planetary Systems: Falling Off the Kuiper Cliff in a Dissipating Gas Disk"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Earth and Planetary Astrophysics (astro-ph.EP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T17:59:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T01:02:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-27T07:02:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-28T01:16:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-06T03:42:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-09T00:51:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Probably the last planetesimals to have formed from dust in the solar nebula are Cold Classical Kuiper belt objects (CCKBOs). To the extent that they are isolated and unchanged since birth, CCKBOs offer direct insights into nebular processes. Their population density drops abruptly beyond a heliocentric radius of $\\sim$47 au, a feature known as the \"Kuiper Cliff\". We show with global, 1D (radial), time-dependent models how gaseous protoplanetary disks that disperse from magnetic and photoevaporative winds leave behind planetesimal disks with Cliff-like outer edges. The gas disperses from the inside out, creating transitional disks whose inner cavities expand from $\\lesssim$ 1 au to $\\gtrsim$ 100 au. Gas at the cavity boundary presents a pressure maximum toward which dust particles drift, triggering the streaming instability which clumps dust into planetesimals massive enough to decouple from gas. The receding cavity wall thus paves a disk of planetesimals which truncates when dust and gas are spent. With no fine-tuning, we show how a generic gas disk clearing from the inside out reproduces the Kuiper Cliff and the CCKB surface density. Connecting these global 1D results with published local 3D simulations of dust and gas, we see how many properties of the CCKB -- its radial extent, total mass, individual object sizes, and binary statistics -- follow from the streaming instability at work in a late-stage transition disk."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ApJ. Simulation videos are available at https://www.rixinli.com/CCKB"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14704","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T02:00:18Z","registered":"2026-06-15T02:00:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-09T06:06:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14518","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14518","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14518","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liou","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Liou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Joshi","name":"Joshi, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ashish","familyName":"Kundu","name":"Kundu, Ashish","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Characterizing Privacy-Audit Alignment in Behavioral Audit of Machine Unlearning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T14:49:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:53:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-04T19:50:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-09T00:08:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The removal of learned data from Machine Learning models through Machine Unlearning (MU) has been widely studied; however, there is no agreed-upon scheme for auditing MU. Existing work shows that a dishonest model owner can falsify evidence to avoid executing MU, while curious auditors (and adversaries) can infer privacy-sensitive properties of the model and its training data even with limited access. Yet auditing of MU under mutual distrust between the model owner and the auditor remains unexplored. In this paper, we characterize how much a generic audit scheme that relies solely on querying the model for behavioral signals inevitably results in privacy leakage related to the retained set by providing a geometric transfer theorem that establishes a lower bound on retained set membership distinguishability based on the audit accuracy. In addition, we study how the unlearned set, target sample, and query protocol jointly determine the privacy-audit transfer coefficient through local and global model-parameter-space geometry. Our empirical experiments on both convex and non-convex models strongly support these results. Our results call for more careful consideration of the privacy-audit tension under a realistic auditor model and serve as a foundation for greater scrutiny of privacy-preserving audit scheme designs for the MU pipeline."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14518","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:56:23Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:56:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-09T06:06:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14199","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14199","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14199","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuhui","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Xuhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiwei","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Weiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weihua","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Weihua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiarui","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiarui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haojia","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Haojia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qianou","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Qianou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tongshuang","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Tongshuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiming","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maarten","familyName":"Sap","name":"Sap, Maarten","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OdysSim: Building Foundation Models for Human Behavior Simulation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T07:31:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:33:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-07T20:06:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-09T02:03:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models are increasingly deployed as human simulators for interactive evaluation and social simulation. Yet helpfulness-driven post-training pulls them toward a homogeneous, overly agreeable assistant register, creating a behavioral Sim2Real gap. We present OdysSim, the largest open systematic investigation of behavioral foundation models, i.e., models trained to simulate human behavior at scale. We propose SOUL, a taxonomy of five capability axes (CONV, SS, COG, ROLE, EVAL) that unifies 62 datasets and 23 benchmark tasks under one framework. Specifically, we curate the OdysSim corpus (21.4M interactions, 10B tokens, retrofitted with back-generated social contexts), construct the SOUL-Index benchmark, and develop an end-to-end training recipe combining midtraining, task-specific RL, and expert distillation. The resulting open 8B OSim model ranks first or tied-first on 8 of 23 tasks, outperforming any individual frontier model by this count, with the strongest gains on conversational and social tasks. Its outputs are also more human-like in length, formatting, and word choice, and it transfers zero-shot to out-of-distribution user simulation on $τ$-bench, nearly matching real users on reaction alignment (93.2 vs. 93.5). We further show that LLM-as-judge RL induces reward-hacking patterns, and that our detectors can mitigate them during post-training. Together, our findings suggest that behavioral foundation models require rethinking the LLM training paradigm. We release all artifacts to support future research."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"34 pages. Code: https://github.com/sunnweiwei/OdysSim ; Models and data: https://huggingface.co/collections/cmu-lti/odyssim ; v2: Corrected two bibliography entries"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14199","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:49:36Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:49:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-09T06:06:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14182","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14182","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14182","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chris","familyName":"Angstmann","name":"Angstmann, Chris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tim","familyName":"Gebbie","name":"Gebbie, Tim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Correlation emergence and the Epps effect in two coupled limit order books"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Trading and Market Microstructure (q-fin.TR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Analysis of PDEs (math.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Statistical Finance (q-fin.ST)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"91G15, 60K50, 91B80","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T07:02:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:31:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-08T14:40:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-11T00:37:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-05T12:47:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-09T00:34:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We give a unified analytic account of correlation emergence and the Epps effect in two coupled limit order books. The Epps effect is the empirical reduction in measured cross-asset log-return correlation at short aggregation scales, or equivalently the recovery of measured correlation as the aggregation interval increases. The model starts from a fixed-grid discrete random walk for order flow in operational time, with creation, cancellation and diffusion. A pair-trader coupling between the books is introduced at the level of order creation, and calendar time is imposed through separate observation clocks. We clarify how the operational-time model reduces to coupled reaction--diffusion equations with a moving reaction boundary defining the model log-mid-price. Using a regularised local-response representation of the coupling, we derive approximate closed-form expressions for realised correlations as a function of aggregation time. Here the Epps effect is shown to arise from two distinct mechanisms: asynchronous observation clocks (subordination), finite coupling response times, and their combination."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages, 1 figure, 7 appendices. Revised numerical implementation to conform to the fixed operational-time lattice and explicit calendar-time subordination, corrected numerical source and coupling treatment; the analytical results and main conclusions are unchanged. Reproducibility code: https://github.com/timgebbie/correlation-emergence-reproducibility"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14182","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:49:14Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:49:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-09T06:06:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14138","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14138","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14138","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zijing","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Zijing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baole","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Baole","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Optimal heat transport at the edge of energy stability"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Fluid Dynamics (physics.flu-dyn)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T05:52:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:28:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-03T00:50:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-04T01:22:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-04T01:43:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:17:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-08T01:20:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-09-09T02:11:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"High heat transfer in Rayleigh--Bénard convection is commonly associated with vigorous turbulent motion, but turbulence intensity alone does not explain how a limiting transport state is selected. We propose that such limiting states are organized by marginal energy stability. Starting from the exact perturbation-energy balance, we determine, for a prescribed mean temperature profile, the smallest neutral Rayleigh number over the balance parameter and all admissible disturbances. The corresponding marginal modes are then coupled to the exact mean-temperature equation, producing a self-consistent profile and heat flux. At large $Ra$, the selected branch gives $Nu\\simeq0.0245Ra^{1/2}$ and develops conductive inner layers, logarithmic-like intermediate regions and a weakly stably stratified core. An equivalent background-field formulation yields the same governing equations and establishes uniqueness of the selected mean profile. Three-dimensional simulations at $10^6\\le Ra\\le10^8$ show that distributed thermal forcing based on this profile suppresses convective motion while retaining a large wall heat flux."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14138","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:48:19Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:48:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-09T06:06:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13848","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13848","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13848","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zacharias","familyName":"Veiksaar","name":"Veiksaar, Zacharias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maxime","familyName":"Bouton","name":"Bouton, Maxime","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Temporally Consistent Graph Q-Networks for Intelligent Network Control"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T19:25:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-09T01:11:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/infocom59046.2026.11571413","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Mobile networks continue to grow in complexity and next generation networks are expected to support both increasing traffic loads and more diverse services. As network complexity rises, optimizing antenna parameters under dynamic or changing objectives becomes increasingly challenging. We propose a novel multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL) algorithm for high-level control and orchestration of mobile networks.\n The Temporally Consistent Graph Q-Network (TC-GQN) algorithm learns a self-predicting representation of the whole network that is task-independent and aggregates information from all base-stations. A graph neural network is trained using a global reward function to assign coordinated local actions based on the learned encoding of the global network state. We evaluate the algorithm in a simulated environment to orchestrate an energy-saving feature across multiple sectors and multiple carriers under different quality of service (QoS) constraints. The proposed algorithm outperforms state-of-the-art graph-based baselines and a competitive rule-based controller by improving hardware sleep time while maintaining QoS. Moreover, the learned representation enables rapid adaptation to changing intents."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages, 5 figures. Accepted to the 6G AI-RAN Workshop at IEEE INFOCOM 2026. The final published version will be available via IEEE Xplore"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13848","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-15T01:42:09Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:42:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-09T06:06:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13774","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13774","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13774","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jon","familyName":"Goker","name":"Goker, Jon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luca V.","familyName":"Iliesiu","name":"Iliesiu, Luca V.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elisa","familyName":"Tabor","name":"Tabor, Elisa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A note on the quantization of angular momentum for black holes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Theory (hep-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T18:00:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:04:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-03T19:14:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-07T00:05:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We argue that the gravitational path integral for rotating black holes is periodic in the angular velocity, implying the quantization of angular momentum in arbitrary dimensions for either asymptotically flat or AdS boundary conditions. In AdS$_3$, this periodicity is a consequence of the boundary mapping class group. In higher dimensions, the periodicity arises from an infinite family of saddles labeled by integer shifts of the angular velocity unrelated to the boundary mapping class group; summing over these saddles enforces quantization independently of any large boundary diffeomorphism. We construct these saddles explicitly for Kerr-Newman black holes in both asymptotically flat space and AdS$_4$, and observe that even the path integral for the 4D Schwarzschild black hole, typically the simplest case, receives contributions from an infinite set of rotating saddles."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"19 Pages + 3 Appendices, 3 figures; v2: typos corrected, reference added"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13774","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:40:35Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:40:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-07T02:20:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14660","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14660","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14660","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Per","familyName":"Bäck","name":"Bäck, Per","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Patrik","familyName":"Lundström","name":"Lundström, Patrik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Johan","familyName":"Öinert","name":"Öinert, Johan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Johan","familyName":"Richter","name":"Richter, Johan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Hilbert's basis theorem for Poisson Ore extensions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Rings and Algebras (math.RA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Commutative Algebra (math.AC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantum Algebra (math.QA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"17B63 (Primary) 13E05, 16P40, 16S36 (Secondary)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T17:29:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T01:01:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-19T18:54:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-21T00:05:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-02T12:36:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-04T00:48:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1002/mana.70248","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We prove an analogue of Hilbert's basis theorem for Poisson Ore extensions and Poisson Laurent Ore extensions. We also obtain corresponding results for iterated Poisson Ore extensions and iterated Poisson Laurent Ore extensions associated to commuting Poisson-pairs. Finally, we give examples of Poisson Ore extensions that are Poisson-Noetherian without being Noetherian as ordinary algebras."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages; added Remark 2 and Lemma 3 on localization, simplifying the Laurent case of Proposition 5 and the Laurent cases of Theorems 1 and 6, with some additional minor corrections and clarifications; corrected some typos"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14660","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-15T01:59:21Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:59:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-04T02:44:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14503","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14503","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14503","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anouar El","familyName":"Harrak","name":"Harrak, Anouar El","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hatim","familyName":"Tayeq","name":"Tayeq, Hatim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benaissa","familyName":"Zerroudi","name":"Zerroudi, Benaissa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Efficient and Fast Tensor-Product Multinode Shepard Collocation for Elliptic PDEs on Cartesian Grids"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Numerical Analysis (math.NA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"65N35, 65D05, 65D15, 41A05, 41A20","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T14:34:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:52:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-02T19:18:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-04T00:07:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce a Grid-Based Multinode Shepard Collocation Method (GBMSC) for two-dimensional elliptic boundary value problems on rectangular domains. The method combines Shepard-type partition functions with tensor-product Lagrange interpolation on overlapping local Cartesian subgrids. The Cartesian structure avoids the local unisolvency search required by multinode Shepard constructions on scattered data, while the local character of the approximation yields sparse collocation matrices and moderate conditioning. We establish the main structural properties of the method, including the cardinal property, tensor-product polynomial reproduction, and local interpolation estimates. Numerical experiments for Poisson problems with Dirichlet and mixed boundary conditions confirm the polynomial reproduction property up to round-off accuracy and show regular convergence for smooth non-polynomial solutions. Comparisons with Multinode Shepard Collocation Method (MSC) and Kansa's collocation methods indicate that the proposed discretization preserves high accuracy with significantly better conditioning than the RBF-based schemes considered."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"19 pages, 5 figures, 6 tables; submitted to journal"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14503","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:56:04Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:56:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-04T02:44:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14202","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14202","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14202","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zishang","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Zishang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xinan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rong","familyName":"Qu","name":"Qu, Rong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruibin","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Ruibin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MeEvo: Metacognitive Evolution Combined with Natural Evolution for Automatic Heuristic Design"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Neural and Evolutionary Computing (cs.NE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T07:36:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:33:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T02:42:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-16T01:16:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-17T13:32:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-18T00:52:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-28T07:03:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-06-30T00:53:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-09-03T03:46:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-09-04T00:25:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have advanced Automatic Heuristic Design (AHD) by enabling heuristic generation through reasoning and code synthesis. In LLM-based AHD, the LLM reasons about algorithm design and generates executable heuristic code. Existing architectures adopt two main paradigms: Natural Evolution applies crossover and mutation to this code to explore diverse strategies, but discards the reasoning traces behind the design decisions, weakening knowledge retention; Metacognitive Evolution retains these reasoning traces and refines them through reflection, but lacks population-level recombination, limiting exploration. These limitations reduce search efficiency, stability, and solution quality on complex problems. To address this gap, we propose MeEvo, an AHD framework that cyclically couples Natural Evolution and Metacognitive Evolution with operator balance that shifts from exploration to exploitation. Natural Evolution explores heuristic code while recording LLM-generated reasoning traces, fitness values, errors and best heuristic into a shared history; Metacognitive Evolution then reflects on this history to generate improved heuristics that feed into the next Natural Evolution cycle. This design enables population-driven exploration and reflection-driven refinement to reinforce each other. Experiments on five optimization problems show that MeEvo achieves stronger performance and lower variance than tested LLM-based AHD architectures, especially on complex constrained tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14202","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:49:39Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:49:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-04T02:44:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14080","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14080","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14080","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ivan","familyName":"Frolov","name":"Frolov, Ivan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Denis","familyName":"Gorodkov","name":"Gorodkov, Denis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dmitrii","familyName":"Korshunov","name":"Korshunov, Dmitrii","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Nabutovsky","name":"Nabutovsky, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Geodesic nets on the Euclidean plane and closed geodesic nets on Euclidean surfaces"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Differential Geometry (math.DG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T03:56:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:24:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-02T15:05:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-03T01:04:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We prove that if $M$ is a closed Riemannian surface of diameter $d$ and area $v$ with sectional curvature in the $[-1,1]$ interval, then a closed geodesic net of length $l$ has at most $f(l,d,v)$ branch points, where $f(l,d,v)=(400\\bar{l})^{(180\\bar{l})^4}$ for $\\bar{l}= \\max\\{l, \\frac{\\exp(d)}{\\min\\{1, \\frac{v}{4}\\}}\\}$\n This answers a question posed by S. Becker-Kahn. We also prove that for each geodesic net in the Euclidean plane with at most n unbalanced (boundary) vertices such that all its unbalanced vertices have degree 1, the number of balanced vertices of degree $\\ge 3$ (=branch points) does not exceed $(25n)^{2n^2}$. This answers a question posed in [GM] and [NP]."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14080","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:47:05Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:47:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-03T02:41:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14019","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14019","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14019","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roberto","familyName":"Vila","name":"Vila, Roberto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eduardo","familyName":"Nakano","name":"Nakano, Eduardo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cecilia","familyName":"Castro","name":"Castro, Cecilia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Tail representations for absolute, signed-power, inverse, and logarithmic moments"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Probability (math.PR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Statistics Theory (math.ST)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T01:40:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:18:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-02T02:31:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-03T00:21:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Let $X$ be a real-valued random variable and let $p\u0026gt;0$. When $X$ can take negative values, $X^p$ is not generally real-valued for non-integer $p$; formulas for moments of arbitrary positive order must therefore distinguish the absolute power $|X|^p$ from the signed power $\\operatorname{sgn}(X)|X|^p$. Using the layer-cake identity, we give a self-contained treatment of tail representations for the positive and negative parts of $X$, including absolute and signed-power moments, centered forms, and exact formulas on ordered discrete supports and integer lattices. The basic identities require neither a density nor continuity and apply to continuous, discrete, singular, and mixed distributions. For strictly positive random variables, we also give representations and finiteness criteria for inverse moments, together with distribution-function and Frullani--Laplace formulas for logarithmic moments. Examples illustrate two-sided support, atoms, lattice tails, and behavior near zero. The presentation places these classical identities in a common notation and states the domain and integrability conditions required in each case."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 1 figure"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14019","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:45:49Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:45:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-03T02:41:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13791","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13791","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13791","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zahra","familyName":"Sharbaf","name":"Sharbaf, Zahra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ignacio","familyName":"Ferreras","name":"Ferreras, Ignacio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anna R.","familyName":"Gallazzi","name":"Gallazzi, Anna R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefano","familyName":"Zibetti","name":"Zibetti, Stefano","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniele","familyName":"Mattolini","name":"Mattolini, Daniele","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Laura","familyName":"Scholz-Díaz","name":"Scholz-Díaz, Laura","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A novel data-driven approach to extract stellar population properties from galaxy spectra using absorption indices"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Astrophysics of Galaxies (astro-ph.GA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T18:01:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:05:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-17T10:52:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-02T13:34:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1093/mnras/stag1528","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In an era of highly complex machine learning methods that often are informative but not straightforward to interpret, Principal Component Analysis (PCA) offers a simple, easily interpretable approach. With no fitting parameters, it extracts the most salient statistical trends in data without the need for training sets. In this paper, we explore a large range of composite stellar population models defined for detailed analyses of galaxy spectra from surveys. Six of the most prominent spectral indices are targeted to visualize a PCA-based latent space created by the model data. The age-metallicity degeneracy is broken in the 3-dimensional space spanned by the first three eigenvectors, but we emphasize that non-trivial combinations of all six absorption indices are needed for this. Moreover, the last eigenvector suggests an intriguing tug of war between two Balmer indices: H$γ_A$ and $Hδ_A$, that can help discern the presence of recent bursting behaviour, as it exploits the different behaviour of the two indices over timescales $\\sim$0.5-1 Gyr. Comparisons can be made between SDSS and LEGA-C galaxy spectra based on the latent space created by the models. This method, based on pure data, produces excellent results in agreement with standard SPS model fitting techniques, allowing for the study of stellar populations in a variety of surveys or observational/synthetic databases on solid ground."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"20 pages, including 10 figures and 1 table. Accepted for publication in MNRAS"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13791","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-15T01:40:56Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:40:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-03T02:41:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14617","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14617","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14617","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongyan","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Yongyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Contact-Consistent Interaction Dynamics Normalization for Predictive Physical Human--Robot Interaction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T16:41:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:59:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-11T23:21:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-14T00:39:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-01T03:20:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-02T00:29:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Safe physical human--robot interaction on floating-base robots requires interaction regulation under changing contact constraints. We develop a contact-consistent normalization in which the residual end-effector channel is represented as a linear double integrator in acceleration coordinates. Both discrete prediction matrices are independent of configuration and support mode; posture and contact enter only through task-inertia force recovery and constraints. The controller combines a constant-Hessian receding-horizon QP, an acceleration-disturbance observer, and a priority-consistent realization. Classical operational-space impedance is shown to be the unconstrained infinite-horizon limit. MuJoCo experiments on a 17-DOF biped and a Menagerie-derived Unitree G1 model evaluate sustained forces, transmitted shocks, and scheduled contact-model changes. The observer sustains near-offset-free tracking across a genuine contact-set switch and a scheduled support-mode transition, while disturbance estimation---not contact consistency alone---is the dominant source of fixed-stance accuracy; covariance inflation gives only scenario-dependent transient benefit."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14617","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:58:27Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:58:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-02T03:08:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14307","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14307","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14307","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Victor","familyName":"Barberteguy","name":"Barberteguy, Victor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmet","familyName":"Iscen","name":"Iscen, Ahmet","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mathilde","familyName":"Caron","name":"Caron, Mathilde","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alireza","familyName":"Fathi","name":"Fathi, Alireza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gül","familyName":"Varol","name":"Varol, Gül","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cordelia","familyName":"Schmid","name":"Schmid, Cordelia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Pano3D: Unified 3D Reconstruction and Panoptic Segmentation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T09:43:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:39:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-30T10:03:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-02T01:11:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in 3D feedforward reconstruction neural networks have achieved remarkable success in dense reconstruction from images without any camera parameters. Yet, equipping these models with robust semantic understanding remains an open problem. Here we introduce an approach that performs 3D reconstruction and 3D panoptic segmentation in a unified framework. We build on existing 3D reconstruction models and augment them with a set-based mask decoder. The approach is jointly trained with a geometric and semantic loss, which are shown to be mutually beneficial. More precisely, the features are initialized from the geometric information and then finetuned to capture jointly geometry and semantics. We demonstrate the generality of our approach by successfully applying our framework both to online and all-to-all attention reconstruction backbones. Our method achieves state-of-the-art performance in 3D panoptic segmentation across ScanNet, ScanNet200, and ScanNet++ datasets. Ablation studies show that such joint training of a unified model equips 3D feedforward reconstruction neural networks with panoptic segmentation and yields mutually beneficial improvements."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ECCV 2026. Project page: https://victorbbt.github.io/Pano3D/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14307","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:51:54Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:51:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-02T03:08:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14636","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14636","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14636","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zongyuan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zongyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hong","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Hong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Defu","familyName":"Lian","name":"Lian, Defu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Enhong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Enhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning Generated Controls under Fractured Geometry: Projective Residualization and Variation-Allocation Frontiers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T17:02:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T01:00:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-29T07:23:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-30T00:28:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-29T07:59:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-01T00:28:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Many two-stage estimators assess the first-stage learner by prediction error, even when the next stage uses its residual. In control-function instrumental variables, that residual must preserve the latent control direction without removing the treatment variation that identifies the structural response. A scalar prediction score does not reveal how the learner allocates this variation. Under piecewise-smooth graph geometry, interpolation can suppress the control, whereas isotropic smoothing can leak systematic variation across boundaries. We formulate this as a variation-allocation problem and introduce Adaptive Anisotropic Instrumental Heat Flow (A-IHF). The method uses pilot treatment contrasts to adapt edge conductance, takes the complement of a sparse graph resolvent as the generated control, and selects candidates without consulting outcomes. For a linear control-function regression, the generated control is identified only by its span. Working in that projective geometry, we derive an exact finite-sample fidelity--relevance frontier, spectral identities for remaining treatment variation and coefficient distortion, and a lower bound for monotone fixed-graph residual filters. A connected construction proves that adapting conductance can remove the corresponding fixed-graph obstruction. In a 54-cell benchmark, the A-IHF family wins 32 cells; its guarded observational variant lowers mean nonlinear response error by 8.3%, with the largest gains in fractured designs. Controlled rewiring explains when the graph should be used, replaced by a fallback, or rejected. The resulting lesson is task-specific: a first stage for generated controls should be judged by control fidelity, downstream relevance, and graph compatibility together."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"86 pages, 9 figures, including supplementary material. Revised version; submitted to Artificial Intelligence"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14636","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:58:51Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:58:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-01T05:15:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14581","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14581","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14581","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guanyu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Guanyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiyi","familyName":"Kong","name":"Kong, Weiyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zeyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boer","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Boer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baiqing","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Baiqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peiyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Peiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyu","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Tianyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CARE: Context-Aware Ranking Evolution with Executable Scoring Programs for Budgeted Reaction Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T15:58:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:57:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-24T06:46:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-25T00:32:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-29T16:12:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-30T02:09:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-01T12:11:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:32:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-08-31T06:08:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-09-01T01:38:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"High-throughput experimentation can evaluate many reaction conditions, yet combinatorial condition spaces still exceed the available experiment budget. This makes experiment selection a sequential decision problem: each new condition must be chosen from limited observations before its outcome is known. LLMs can express task-specific selection logic. A direct recommendation, however, is neither a persistent executable object that can be validated and revised nor an independently auditable decision rule. We introduce CARE, a reference-conditioned controller that separates program synthesis from experiment selection. An LLM writes an executable scoring program that ranks the remaining conditions, while a non-LLM reference policy supplies a numerical candidate and support summary. CARE forms an optional alternative from the program, applies a reference-conditioned intervention gate to compare it with the reference, and records the decision before the selected outcome is revealed. Each new outcome updates controller state and can trigger retention, revision, or regeneration of the active program. This outcome-guided program evolution changes the scoring logic without updating the LLM parameters. In matched offline replay with 30 seeds on eight reaction-optimization tasks, CARE attains the lowest normalized regret, the highest normalized best-so-far AUC, and the highest Top-1% Success@15 among the evaluated methods. These results support using a scoring program written by an LLM as one component of a reference-conditioned optimizer rather than as a standalone experiment selector."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"27 pages, 5 figures. Code: https://github.com/SHITIANYU-hue/care"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14581","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:57:42Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:57:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-01T05:15:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14551","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14551","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14551","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ranpeng","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Ranpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yincong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yincong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guoqiang","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Guoqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiming","familyName":"Zhi","name":"Zhi, Weiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TRACE: Trajectory-Routed Causal Memory for Delayed-Evidence Visuomotor Imitation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T15:30:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:55:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-16T07:41:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:36:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-31T07:07:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-01T01:41:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Robots under autonomous operation may require decisions based on evidence that is no longer visible. We study delayed-evidence tasks, where an early cue disappears before a later decision point, so visually similar observations can require different actions. In these settings, the current observation is not a sufficient state for control. We introduce TRAjectory-routed Causal Evidence (TRACE), a memory framework for visuomotor imitation policies. TRACE stores task-relevant visual and robot-state evidence, such as object identity, target choice, or route-dependent state, in a fixed-size latent memory that remains bounded over long episodes. Instead of indexing memory by raw time or manually provided task labels, TRACE uses path signatures: compact, order-sensitive features of the executed robot-state trajectory. These signatures do not store the visual cue itself; rather, they provide trajectory-conditioned keys for writing and retrieving the evidence stored when the cue was visible. When the robot later reaches an ambiguous observation, the policy conditions on TRACE memory to recover the missing context and choose the correct branch. TRACE attaches through lightweight adapters to policies, without changing the policy backbone, action head, or imitation objective. Across real-world long-horizon manipulation tasks with visually ambiguous branch points, TRACE improves branch selection and task success over alternative baselines, including short-history and recurrent memory. Project page: https://jeong-zju.github.io/trace"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14551","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:57:05Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:57:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-01T05:15:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14304","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14304","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14304","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arkadiy I.","familyName":"Syamtomov","name":"Syamtomov, Arkadiy I.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Light-front transverse profiles of scalar and spin-two chromoelectric EMT responses in near-threshold charmonium probes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Phenomenology (hep-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T09:38:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:39:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T09:46:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-16T01:35:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-10T13:12:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-01T00:58:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1016/j.physletb.2026.140881","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"I construct light-front transverse profile functions for the scalar and spin-two chromoelectric energy-momentum-tensor responses selected by compact-charmonium probes of the proton. The scalar branch is the anomaly and sigma amplitude, while the spin-two branch contains the gravitational combination $3B_i(t)-D_i(t)=6J_i(t)-3A_i(t)-D_i(t)$ rather than $A_i(t)$ alone. In the Drell-Yan frame, these two amplitudes define normalized transverse response profiles whose integrated ratio reproduces the forward light-front spin-two/scalar strength, while the finite transverse-distance behavior is sensitive to the relative scalar and gravitational slopes. The construction gives a spatial representation of the chromoelectric EMT projection without interpreting a measured near-threshold slope as a model-independent static three-dimensional mass radius."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14304","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-15T01:51:50Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:51:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-01T05:15:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13885","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13885","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13885","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenyue","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chenyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shangyuan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Shangyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hoi-To","familyName":"Wai","name":"Wai, Hoi-To","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anthony Man-Cho","familyName":"So","name":"So, Anthony Man-Cho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning Graph Topology with Functional Priors via Bilevel Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T20:21:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-01T00:04:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/tsp.2026.3727897","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Learning graph topology of complex networks is challenging due to limited data availability and imprecise data models. Different from prior works that focus on structural priors with explicit control on macroscopic properties such as sparsity, this paper proposes a novel functional prior approach for graph topology learning. We postulate that complex networks are inherently optimized to perform a certain task (e.g., social networks specialize at optimizing a welfare function, biological networks are resilient towards node/edge deletion), which can be incorporated as a regularizer to assist in graph learning. Mathematically, we formulate a bilevel optimization problem where the lower-level problem solves the associated task on a candidate graph topology and the upper-level problem trades off between data fitting and task performance. We design a two-timescale gradient descent (TTGD) algorithm and show that under verifiable conditions, it finds a stationary point to the bilevel graph learning problem with a sublinear convergence rate. We provide theoretical insights on the graph topology learned from the functional priors and show that the resulting regularizers subsume a broad class of graph filter regularizers, including polynomial graph regularizers as special cases. We show via extensive experiments on synthetic and real datasets that the proposed formulation gives rise to reliable estimates of graph topology, even with insufficient data."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 8 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13885","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-15T01:42:59Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:43:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-01T05:15:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13787","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13787","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13787","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alberto Manuel","familyName":"Martínez-García","name":"Martínez-García, Alberto Manuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrés","familyName":"del Pino","name":"del Pino, Andrés","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roeland P.","familyName":"van der Marel","name":"van der Marel, Roeland P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giuseppina","familyName":"Battaglia","name":"Battaglia, Giuseppina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ewa L.","familyName":"Łokas","name":"Łokas, Ewa L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eduardo","familyName":"Vitral","name":"Vitral, Eduardo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin A.","familyName":"McKinnon","name":"McKinnon, Kevin A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Laura L.","familyName":"Watkins","name":"Watkins, Laura L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nitya","familyName":"Kallivayalil","name":"Kallivayalil, Nitya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sangmo Tony","familyName":"Sohn","name":"Sohn, Sangmo Tony","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guillaume F.","familyName":"Thomas","name":"Thomas, Guillaume F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Salvador","familyName":"Cardona-Barrero","name":"Cardona-Barrero, Salvador","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Borja","familyName":"Anguiano","name":"Anguiano, Borja","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jairo A.","familyName":"Alzate-Trujillo","name":"Alzate-Trujillo, Jairo A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francisco","familyName":"Nogueras-Lara","name":"Nogueras-Lara, Francisco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paul","familyName":"Bennet","name":"Bennet, Paul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adrián","familyName":"Hidalgo-Pinilla","name":"Hidalgo-Pinilla, Adrián","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reconstructing the orbits of Milky Way dwarf galaxies: A Large Magellanic Cloud perspective"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Astrophysics of Galaxies (astro-ph.GA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T18:00:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:04:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-31T14:43:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-01T02:13:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The orbital histories of the dwarf satellites of the Milky Way (MW) are key to understanding their evolution and placing their present-day properties in a dynamical context. We present the results of the orbit integration of 72 dwarfs in the vicinity of the MW, based on accurate 6D phase-space coordinates from the literature and a suite of six realistic, time-evolving gravitational potentials that account for the mutual interaction of the MW and the Large Magellanic Cloud (LMC). We provide the largest catalogue of orbital parameters for MW dwarfs to date, in terms of both galaxy sample size and range of potentials explored. We also assess the binding status of the dwarfs and estimate their infall times, finding that the majority of them have spent the last 5 Gyr within the MW virial radius. From the reconstructed orbits, we identify ten likely LMC satellites, several of which have experienced very close passages within the LMC stellar disc. For the Small Magellanic Cloud (SMC), we find that its most recent pericentre about the LMC ($\\sim$8 kpc, $\\sim$170 Myr ago) is consistent with predictions from the direct collision scenario proposed to explain the LMC's offset and tilted bar. We also note a broad temporal coincidence between previous SMC pericentres and star formation rate peaks reported in both Magellanic Clouds, suggesting a causal connection. Finally, we identify Grus II and Tucana IV as possible MW satellites recently captured by the LMC, based on their pronounced orbital deflections and velocities relative to the LMC."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"27 pages, 9 figures, 5 tables, 5 appendices, accepted for publication in A\u0026amp;A"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13787","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:40:51Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:40:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-01T05:15:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14477","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14477","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14477","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David J.","familyName":"Rosario","name":"Rosario, David J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Houda","familyName":"Haidar","name":"Haidar, Houda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Steph","familyName":"Campbell","name":"Campbell, Steph","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John","familyName":"Schneider","name":"Schneider, John","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chris","familyName":"Packham","name":"Packham, Chris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nancy A.","familyName":"Levenson","name":"Levenson, Nancy A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Almudena","familyName":"Alonso-Herrero","name":"Alonso-Herrero, Almudena","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Richard I.","familyName":"Davies","name":"Davies, Richard I.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dan","familyName":"Delaney","name":"Delaney, Dan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Santiago","familyName":"García-Burillo","name":"García-Burillo, Santiago","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Erin K. S.","familyName":"Hicks","name":"Hicks, Erin K. S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sebastian F.","familyName":"Hönig","name":"Hönig, Sebastian F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mason","familyName":"Leist","name":"Leist, Mason","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Enrique","familyName":"Lopez-Rodriguez","name":"Lopez-Rodriguez, Enrique","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miguel","familyName":"Pereira-Santaella","name":"Pereira-Santaella, Miguel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anelise","familyName":"Audibert","name":"Audibert, Anelise","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Enrica","familyName":"Bellocchi","name":"Bellocchi, Enrica","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Françoise","familyName":"Combes","name":"Combes, Françoise","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ismael","familyName":"García-Bernete","name":"García-Bernete, Ismael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Boorman","name":"Boorman, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew J.","familyName":"Bunker","name":"Bunker, Andrew J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luis","familyName":"Colina","name":"Colina, Luis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tanio Diaz","familyName":"Santos","name":"Santos, Tanio Diaz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fergus R.","familyName":"Donnan","name":"Donnan, Fergus R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Poshak","familyName":"Gandhi","name":"Gandhi, Poshak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Omaira","familyName":"González-Martín","name":"González-Martín, Omaira","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Laura Hermosa","familyName":"Muñoz","name":"Muñoz, Laura Hermosa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alvaro","familyName":"Labiano","name":"Labiano, Alvaro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cristina Ramos","familyName":"Almeida","name":"Almeida, Cristina Ramos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Claudio","familyName":"Ricci","name":"Ricci, Claudio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rogemar A.","familyName":"Riffel","name":"Riffel, Rogemar A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dimitra","familyName":"Rigopoulou","name":"Rigopoulou, Dimitra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Rouan","name":"Rouan, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marko","familyName":"Stalevski","name":"Stalevski, Marko","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lulu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Lulu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GATOS XV: A JWST/MIRI survey of extended circumnuclear dust emission in nearby Seyfert galaxies"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Astrophysics of Galaxies (astro-ph.GA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Astrophysical Phenomena (astro-ph.HE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T14:15:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:51:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-28T11:20:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-31T00:43:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The subarcsecond angular resolution and stable background of JWST has given us the first high-fidelity images of the arcsecond-scale environment around Active Galactic Nuclei (AGNs) in the nearby Universe. With mid-infrared (MIR) surface brightness sensitivities that are much deeper than the best ground-based instruments, the Mid-InfraRed Instrument imager (MIRIM) now allows us to understand the structure and thermal properties of dust using information over wavelengths of $5$-$25$ $μ$m, almost all of the MIR range. We present a Cycle 1 JWST MIRIM survey of Seyfert galaxies with the express aim of characterising AGN-heated dust in the central few 100 pcs, and searching for signatures of dust-laden nuclear outflows. This paper outlines the motivation behind the programme, the data reduction and analysis techniques used to isolate the nuclear and extended emission, and a comparison of the observed MIR structures with those seen in other phases (stars, ionised and molecular gas, absorbing dust). In concert with earlier studies that used these data, we conclude that resolved AGN-heated dust is widespread in the Seyfert population, extending out to a few hundred pcs from the nucleus and often displaying a higher surface-brightness compared to the more widespread star-forming dusty circumnuclear disk. Even after accounting for contamination from emission lines in the MIRI filters, we find strong spatial correlations between MIR dust emission and the AGN-ionised gas in the narrow-line region (NLR)."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"31 pages, 18 figures, MNRAS in press"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14477","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:55:30Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:55:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-31T02:25:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14580","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14580","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14580","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liancheng","familyName":"Gong","name":"Gong, Liancheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiwei","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Julia","familyName":"Mendelsohn","name":"Mendelsohn, Julia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Persuasion Index: A Theory-Guided Framework for Persuasion Analysis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T15:58:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:57:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-27T21:11:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-31T00:11:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Identifying persuasive rhetorical cues is critical across domains, from detecting information manipulation and improving AI safety to advancing public health communication. We propose the Persuasion Index (PI), a taxonomy of 15 dimensions grounded in persuasion theories from psychology and communication, and one transparent implementation using 55 sub-features built from lexicons and rule-based detectors. The taxonomy is modular: individual detectors can be replaced while preserving the theoretical structure. We evaluate PI on four public datasets for English argumentative text that vary in domain, style, and outcome measures, and show that PI provides a shared feature space for interpreting rhetorical patterns associated with persuasion-related outcomes. Linear models show that PI features carry meaningful predictive signal while remaining computationally lightweight. Dimension-level analyses reveal recurring associations between PI dimensions and persuasion outcomes across datasets, while also highlighting topic- and stance-specific variation. We release PI as an open-source package and web interface for principled and auditable analysis of human and AI-mediated communication."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP 2026 Main Conference"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14580","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:57:41Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:57:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-31T02:25:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14240","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14240","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14240","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meige","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Meige","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zitong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zitong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jay","familyName":"Pujara","name":"Pujara, Jay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AFFORDANCE20Q: Evaluating Affordance Reasoning from Physical Properties"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T08:20:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:35:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-28T08:25:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-31T00:34:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Affordance reasoning, the inference of an object's action possibilities from its physical properties (e.g., shape and material), is fundamental to human physical understanding and increasingly critical for Large Language Models (LLMs). However, existing affordance benchmarks largely expose explicit object identities in the evaluation setup, allowing models to rely on memorized object-affordance mappings rather than reasoning over physical properties. To address this gap, we introduce Affordance20Q, a novel affordance reasoning benchmark formulated as a 20-Questions game without exposing the object's identity. In each game, the model identifies a hidden object's affordance from a candidate set by asking yes/no questions about its physical properties. Affordance20Q comprises 1,009 games over 454 objects and 59 affordances, all manually filtered, refined, and annotated. We conduct comprehensive experiments with 15 state-of-the-art LLMs and find a substantial gap (~20 points) compared to human performance. A KL-based information-gain (IG) analysis further shows that models fail to ask discriminating questions as the game progresses. To close the gap, we develop KB-Anchored Rule Induction (KARI), a pipeline based on LLMs that generates affordance rules grounded in evidence from knowledge bases (KBs). KARI improves open-source LLMs by up to 15.2 points, while the limited coverage of KBs hinders further gains. We release all our code and data at https://github.com/1171-jpg/Affordance20Q.git."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP Findings 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14240","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:50:29Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:50:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-31T02:25:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13952","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13952","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13952","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eric","familyName":"Yocam","name":"Yocam, Eric","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Varghese","familyName":"Vaidyan","name":"Vaidyan, Varghese","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Micah","familyName":"Flack","name":"Flack, Micah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gurcan","familyName":"Comert","name":"Comert, Gurcan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Judith L.","familyName":"Mwakalonge","name":"Mwakalonge, Judith L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Side-Channel Attacks Survive Noise Cancellation in 3D Printers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Emerging Technologies (cs.ET)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"C.3; K.6.5","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68M25","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T22:32:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:14:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-31T00:36:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-28T00:20:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/access.2026.3727190","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Active Motor Noise Cancellation (AMNC) is a noise-reduction feature shipped in commercial fused deposition modeling (FDM) 3D printers. Because it suppresses the acoustic emissions that side-channel attacks exploit, it has security-relevant side effects, though we find no evidence it was designed as a security control. We present a duration-controlled evaluation of side-channel leakage on AMNC-equipped hardware, using a public dataset of 144 synchronized acoustic and vibration recordings from two Bambu Lab printers across 12 object classes. Spectral analysis confirms suppression is measurably active: the motor-resonance band rises only 4.92 dB above its background-relative baseline during printing. Leakage nonetheless survives it. Acoustic classification is at chance for 30-second windows (11.11%, permutation p = 0.188) but reaches 27.08% on a duration-clean 60-second window and 40.28% under distributed sampling, against an 8.33% baseline: the channel is suppressed within short windows, not eliminated. The dominant discriminator, however, is print duration itself, which classifies at 63.89% (95% CI [55.78, 71.28]) from recording length alone and is validated against sliced G-code print time at r = 0.907. Vibration carries genuine geometry information independent of duration: with the observation window equalized by truncation, classification reaches 45.83% against a 25% baseline (permutation p = 0.023). It nonetheless adds no measurable information beyond duration in paired comparison. The leak is architecture-specific, present on the core-XY device (36.11%) and indistinguishable from chance on the bed-slinger (13.89%), and a consumer handset recovers as much as a mounted accelerometer (25.00% vs 26.39%). Noise cancellation raises the observation time an acoustic attacker requires without eliminating the channel, and leaves print duration entirely untouched."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"v2: Substantially revised and retitled. Adds duration-controlled analysis, spectral verification of suppression, per-capture-configuration results, and G-code ground-truth validation. Conclusions revised: acoustic leakage is observation-time dependent rather than eliminated, and print duration is identified as the dominant discriminator. 14 pages, 5 figures, 3 tables. Under review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13952","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-15T01:44:23Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:44:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-28T02:45:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14571","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14571","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14571","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guanming","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Guanming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqi","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Yuqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hansu","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Hansu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weihang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Weihang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiahao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ning","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Ning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tun","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Tun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"StreamMemBench: Streaming Evaluation of Agent Memory for Future-Oriented Assistance"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T15:48:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:56:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-26T13:12:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-27T00:49:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A central role of personal-agent memory is to turn stored information and prior interactions into future-oriented assistance. In daily use, useful cues come from what the agent observes and how the user interacts with the agent, and the agent must carry them forward from the current request to similar future tasks. Existing memory benchmarks usually test dialogue recall or task improvement in isolation, leaving the trajectory from streaming observations to later assistance largely untested. We introduce StreamMemBench, a streaming benchmark that constructs a two-step task sequence around each evidence anchor from EgoLife egocentric streams. The initial task tests evidence use, while the follow-up task tests whether feedback and interaction experience are reused. Four metrics diagnose evidence recall, initial evidence use, feedback incorporation, and follow-up reuse. Experiments with eight memory systems across two backbones show that current systems often fail to use observed evidence or turn feedback into reliable follow-up behavior, even when evidence is stored or feedback is incorporated locally. StreamMemBench is publicly available at https://github.com/landian60/StreamMemBench."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to Findings of EMNLP 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14571","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:57:30Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:57:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-27T02:28:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14005","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14005","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14005","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyungseop","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Hyungseop","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiho","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jiho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Woochul","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Woochul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Context-Guided Semantic Alignment for Feature Fusion Networks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T00:54:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:17:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-26T06:06:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-27T00:26:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Feature fusion networks are fundamental components in modern object detectors, aggregating multi-scale features to detect objects of varying sizes. However, directly fusing features from different pyramid levels often introduces semantic inconsistency, causing information conflicts that distort the fused representation and degrade detection accuracy. In this paper, we propose Feature Interaction NEtwork (FINE), a lightweight semantic alignment module that refines low-level features via high-level contextual guidance using cross-level attention prior to fusion. To bridge the structural gap and ensure computational efficiency, we introduce an Alignment-Aware Token Sampling that aligns corresponding spatial regions across scales, reducing the attention complexity by an order of magnitude. % The resulting attention weights generate a spatial-channel modulation map that is upsampled and applied to the low-level features via residual element-wise modulation. The resulting attention weights generate a spatial-channel modulation map that is applied to the low-level features via residual modulation. This mechanism ensures that the network selectively enhances semantically relevant pixels while preserving the sub-pixel localization accuracy necessary for dense prediction tasks. FINE is generally applicable to various detectors and consistently improves detection accuracy with minimal computational overhead. Our code is publicly available at https://github.com/HyungseopLee/FINE."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"BMVC 2026 (Oral)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14005","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:45:31Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:45:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-27T02:28:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14572","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14572","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14572","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maciej","familyName":"Dunajski","name":"Dunajski, Maciej","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Timothy","familyName":"Moy","name":"Moy, Timothy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Heavenly equations in de Sitter space"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Theory (hep-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Differential Geometry (math.DG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Exactly Solvable and Integrable Systems (nlin.SI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T15:50:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-18T00:33:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-24T22:34:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-26T00:13:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We demonstrate that all anti-self-dual Einstein metrics with non--zero cosmological constant $Λ$ locally arise from solutions of a single second order PDE introduced by Lipstein and Nagy. We show how this equation fits into the heavenly formalism of Plebański, and establish a Lax pair. Finally we show how Plebański's second heavenly equation arises in the limit as $Λ\\rightarrow 0$."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"In memory of Jerzy Lukierski. A connection with the Chudecki-Przanowski equation and some other gauge choices clarified. Equation (3.5) corrected. Final version, to appear inn JHEP"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14572","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:57:31Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:57:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-26T02:42:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14494","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14494","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14494","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxi","familyName":"Zha","name":"Zha, Jiaxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Logarithmic topological Hochschild homology of the truncated Brown--Peterson spectrum"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Algebraic Topology (math.AT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"55P43, 55P42","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T14:29:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:51:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-25T04:05:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-26T00:26:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study logarithmic topological Hochschild homology (log THH) of the truncated Brown--Peterson spectra $\\mathrm{BP}\\langle n\\rangle$. We first construct compatible prelog structures on $\\mathrm{BP}\\langle n\\rangle$ generated by the classes $v_i$. Then we compute the $V(n)$-homotopy groups of log THH of $\\mathrm{BP}\\langle n\\rangle$ for the prelog structures generated by $v_n$ and $(v_1,\\cdots,v_n)$, under the assumption that the Smith--Toda complex $V(n)$ exists as a ring spectrum. At height $2$ and for $p\\geq7$, we also compute the $V(2)$-homotopy groups of log THH of $\\mathrm{BP}\\langle 2\\rangle/v_1$ relative to $v_2$."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"23 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14494","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:55:53Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:55:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-26T02:42:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14342","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14342","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14342","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Isabella","familyName":"Fumarola","name":"Fumarola, Isabella","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandra I.","familyName":"Liosi","name":"Liosi, Alexandra I.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Parv","familyName":"Khurana","name":"Khurana, Parv","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adam","familyName":"Meziane","name":"Meziane, Adam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Isaac","familyName":"Balbolia","name":"Balbolia, Isaac","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sherwin J.","familyName":"Spencer","name":"Spencer, Sherwin J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonathan F.","familyName":"Morrison","name":"Morrison, Jonathan F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Flow behind the Imperial Front Wing: comparison of results from volumetric PTV experiment and Nektar++ simulations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Fluid Dynamics (physics.flu-dyn)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T10:52:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:42:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-24T22:07:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-26T00:12:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"High-fidelity simulations are increasingly adopted, due to advances in computational power and methods such as Direct Numerical Simulation (DNS) and hybrid Large-Eddy Simulation (LES). These approaches are particularly valuable for unsteady flows around complex geometries at high Reynolds numbers; however they still require careful experimental validation. Planar and stereo Particle Image Velocimetry (PIV) are widely used for measurements but limited by measurement-plane selection and their ability to capture vortices shapes and trajectories. This motivates the growing interest in volumetric techniques, historically difficult to implement in industrial settings. Recent advances in Particle Tracking Velocimetry (PTV) for measuring flows over large volumes make this approach suitable for validating numerical simulations of complex flows.This study compares volumetric PTV measurements against high-fidelity LES to assess the capabilities and limitations for industrial flows. The aim is to establish a benchmark PTV dataset for motorsport aerodynamics using the Shake-The-Box algorithm. The experiment was carried out in the 10x5 wind tunnel at Imperial College London equipped with a rolling road for ground effect simulation and capable of testing up to 50% scale F1 model. Volumetric PTV measurements were performed downstream of the open-source Imperial Front Wing (IFW) at Re=74896. Results are compared with planar PIV studies and implicit LES simulation using spectral h/p elements in Nektar++. This work addresses open questions in the literature concerning the wake of the IFW. Good quantitative agreement is observed in the wake topology. A previously unreported vortex is identified which has the key role of preventing the merging of other dominant structures. These results demonstrate the suitability of PTV and STB for industrial applications while providing a benchmark dataset for the IFW."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submitted to Measurement Science and Technology"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14342","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:52:40Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:52:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-26T02:42:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14128","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14128","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14128","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Johnson","familyName":"Phosavanh","name":"Phosavanh, Johnson","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dmytro","familyName":"Matsypura","name":"Matsypura, Dmytro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Designing Efficient and Reachable Routes: The $k$-Step-Central Shortest Path Problem"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Data Structures and Algorithms (cs.DS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Combinatorics (math.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T05:24:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:28:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-25T03:19:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-26T00:24:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Designing rapid transportation routes requires balancing efficiency and reachability. Shortest-path models ensure direct, cost-efficient routes but ignore coverage, while centrality-based approaches maximize accessibility but do not enforce operational constraints. We study the problem of selecting a shortest path that maximizes reachability, measured as the number of nodes within a fixed distance of the path. To do this, we introduce the $k$-Step-Central Shortest Path problem and analyse its structural properties. We show that optimal solutions on unweighted graphs can be found in polynomial time and propose an algorithm with a novel pruning rule. We also prove that the problem becomes NP-hard when edge weights are introduced. Additionally, we show that our algorithm can be used to solve the NP-hard problem of finding the closeness-central shortest path in a graph. We demonstrate the efficiency and scalability of our algorithm on synthetic and real-world networks with up to 2,000 nodes. Our results show that improving reachability can substitute for route expansion: increasing the reach of transit lines drastically increases their coverage with shorter routes. This suggests that investments in active transport infrastructure that improve reachability can be more effective than extending primary routes, providing a data-driven basis for allocating resources in network design."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14128","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:48:07Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:48:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-26T02:42:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13856","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13856","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13856","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeffrin","familyName":"Sam","name":"Sam, Jeffrin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dzmitry","familyName":"Tsetserukou","name":"Tsetserukou, Dzmitry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Output-Level Regularization Eliminates the Seed Lottery in Single-GPU VLA Fine-Tuning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.9; I.2.6; I.5.1","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T19:33:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:09:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-25T06:54:20Z","dateType":"Withdrawn","dateInformation":"v2; Some errors in the reference, and Im still developing to make it more novel"},{"date":"2026-08-26T00:34:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Fine-tuning a vision-language-action model (VLA-JEPA) on a single GPU should be simple: load a pretrained checkpoint, run training, deploy. There is a hidden danger. Run the same fine-tuning code thirteen times -- same data, same architecture, different random seed -- and twelve runs produce a robot succeeding 91--94% of the time, while one run silently degrades to 65.2%: a 29 pp gap with no error message, no warning, and no way to predict which seed will fail. We call this the seed lottery. We trace the cause to output collapse: the action predictor quietly learns to produce nearly identical outputs regardless of what the robot sees. Existing weight-level methods (L2, EWC) are structurally blind to this collapse -- they penalize weight changes, but collapse occurs in directions weights can move freely without affecting outputs, a gap we formalize via the Jacobian null-space. Across 7 methods x up to 13 seeds x 3 LIBERO benchmarks, three output-level regularizers -- VICReg (n=12 seeds), Dropout (n=4), and a halved learning rate (n=5) -- each eliminate every catastrophic seed (0/21 combined collapses vs. 1/13 Baseline; F(12,11)=28.7, p\u0026lt;0.001), while weight-level methods (L2, EWC) preserve the lottery. The simplest fix is changing one number in your optimizer config."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Some errors in the reference, and Im still developing to make it more novel"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13856","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:42:20Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:42:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-26T02:42:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13705","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13705","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13705","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aristotelis","familyName":"Lazaridis","name":"Lazaridis, Aristotelis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aman","familyName":"Sharma","name":"Sharma, Aman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dylan","familyName":"Bates","name":"Bates, Dylan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brian","familyName":"King","name":"King, Brian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vincent","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Vincent","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jack","familyName":"FitzGerald","name":"FitzGerald, Jack","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"When Can One Neuron Fix Repetition Loops in LLMs?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-09T21:20:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:00:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-24T23:42:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-26T00:15:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The Gemma 4 instruction-tuned models share a reproducible failure: on long factual enumeration prompts, such as TV episodes, the 88 IAU constellations, or the 151 original Pokemon, they collapse into repetition, either a tight verbatim loop or a list whose entries decay onto one answer. These loops reach 87.5% (7/8 generations) and survive prompt rewording and most sampling adjustments. In this paper, we explore whether edits to a few internal model components can directly reduce this failure, without relying on repetition penalties, which can distort valid repetition and degrade task performance. To locate such targets, we combine per-layer ablation with per-neuron or routed-expert attribution, then evaluate weight edits over complete generations. We find that these edits substantially reduce detected loops on the prompts and seeds used to select them; in Gemma 4 E2B, for example, one sign-inverted neuron suffices. Across all four Gemma models, detected loops fall from 46/384 to 12/384 on frozen held-out prompts and seeds, driven mainly by E4B and 31B, while general-purpose benchmarks show no statistically detectable regressions. Our attribution methodology identifies useful candidates, but rankings vary across examples. At longer generation budgets, edits remain effective for E2B and E4B, whereas remaining failures in 26B and 31B shift toward doom looping: non-convergent self-correction over facts the model cannot recall. In exploratory experiments on Qwen3.5 and LFM2.5, sparse edits also reduce repetition, providing preliminary cross-family evidence, although effect strength and selectivity vary. Overall, our results show the promise and limits of targeted, small-scale weight editing: it can suppress specific repetition failures and provide a training-free causal intervention, but does not reveal a universal loop circuit, guarantee clean termination, or supply missing knowledge."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13705","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:39:07Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:39:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-26T02:42:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14229","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14229","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14229","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christian","familyName":"Adamcewicz","name":"Adamcewicz, Christian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hugh","familyName":"McDougall","name":"McDougall, Hugh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paul D.","familyName":"Lasky","name":"Lasky, Paul D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eric","familyName":"Thrane","name":"Thrane, Eric","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Fortifying gravitational-wave population inference with normalizing flows"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Astrophysical Phenomena (astro-ph.HE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T08:11:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:34:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-24T07:15:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-25T00:14:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1103/qq4j-1nx6","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As the LIGO-Virgo-KAGRA collaboration's (LVK's) gravitational-wave transient catalog grows, we are learning a wealth of information from the population properties of binary black hole mergers. Events in the catalog are represented with posterior samples describing the astrophysical parameters for each event. Population studies combine these samples to measure the distribution of astrophysical parameters such as black hole masses and spins. However, the posterior-sample representation of each event is only approximate. We construct a mock population with masses drawn from an astrophysically-motivated distribution with sharp features. Using this, we demonstrate that when $\\gtrsim 300$ events are combined, even with each event's posterior represented by $1 \\times 10^4 {-} 2 \\times 10^4$ samples, the numerical error can become large enough that the resulting population inference is unreliable. We consider two solutions. In the short term, we show that nested samples (already produced by LVK analyses) can be used to more accurately describe each event in population studies. But this will only grant a temporary reprieve until the nested-sample representation becomes inadequate. In the longer term, we propose to represent each event with a normalizing flow. In order to represent each event with sufficient accuracy, each normalizing flow can be used to generate an arbitrarily large number of new posterior samples with a significantly reduced computational cost relative to traditional sampling methods. When compared to nested sampling, our normalizing flows produce posterior draws with a median of $\\approx 80\\%$ fewer likelihood evaluations per sample, while also providing greater opportunity for parallelization. We believe refinement of normalizing flow architectures and training techniques in future works could further reduce this per-sample cost significantly."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"23 pages, 13 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14229","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-15T01:50:14Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:50:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-25T03:59:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14197","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14197","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14197","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nir","familyName":"Guttman","name":"Guttman, Nir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A. Makai","familyName":"Baker","name":"Baker, A. Makai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paul D.","familyName":"Lasky","name":"Lasky, Paul D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eric","familyName":"Thrane","name":"Thrane, Eric","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Licence to Bin: Accurate and Scalable Inference for Binary Neutron Stars in Next-Generation Gravitational-Wave Detectors"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Astrophysical Phenomena (astro-ph.HE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T07:30:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:32:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-24T00:43:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-25T01:10:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Next-generation gravitational-wave observatories will observe binary neutron-star mergers with much higher signal-to-noise ratios, over much longer durations and across broader frequency bands than current detectors. These long-duration signals present a major computational challenge for Bayesian parameter estimation. Reduced-order quadrature is a promising approach for accelerating inference, but in this regime its standard construction encounters severe memory and accuracy limitations. We present a practical reduced-order quadrature construction for long binary neutron-star signals with time-dependent detector response and full effects of the observatories' free-spectral range. Our approach combines improved adaptive frequency sampling, disk-backed streaming, and subbanded reduced-order quadrature construction, enabling efficient and accurate reduced-order models for signals that were previously intractable. We demonstrate for the first time reduced-order Bayesian inference on an approximately 2 h binary neutron-star signal extending down to 5 Hz and with signal-to-noise ratio 2090. We show the resulting reduced-order quadrature remains sufficiently accurate for practical inference. The full analysis is carried out in about 48 h using 128 CPU cores. We also find that, when time-dependent detector-response effects are included, a single Cosmic Explorer detector can localize such a signal to a 90% credible sky area of approximately $41~\\mathrm{deg}^2$, with important implications for multimessenger astronomy and cosmology. These results demonstrate that reduced-order methods can make next-generation binary neutron-star inference computationally feasible."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 7 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14197","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:49:33Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:49:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-25T03:59:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13701","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13701","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13701","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boyuan","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Boyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haiyang","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Haiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongjie","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yongjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangji","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Guangji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guanghao","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Guanghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaorong","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Xiaorong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Synergistic Blood Pressure Estimation via Contactless mmWave Radar and Imaging Photoplethysmography: A Feasibility Study"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-07T03:55:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-25T00:15:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/jsen.2026.3706110","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Continuous, non-contact blood pressure (NCBP) monitoring holds significant promise for pervasive cardiovascular care, yet single-modality approaches -- such as imaging photoplethysmography (iPPG) -- remain constrained by environmental artifacts, skin-tone sensitivity, and the absence of proximal cardiac mechanical information. This study investigates the feasibility of a dual-modality sensing paradigm that synergistically integrates facial iPPG with posterior-facing frequency-modulated continuous wave (FMCW) millimeter-wave radar to capture complementary hemodynamic cues: distal optical volumetric fluctuations and proximal cardiac micro-motions (radar motion signals, RMS). To bridge the morphological disparity between these heterogeneous streams, we develop an end-to-end deep learning architecture, BiLSTM-MS-DiCNN, which leverages multi-scale dilated convolutions for spatial feature extraction and bidirectional long short-term memory for temporal dependency modeling. In a controlled feasibility study involving 15 healthy participants across distinct hemodynamic states (resting, deep breathing, and post-exercise), the proposed framework achieved a Mean Absolute Difference (MAD) of 4.71 mmHg for systolic BP (SBP) and 4.60 mmHg for diastolic BP (DBP) under resting conditions, with consistent performance during physiological perturbations. These preliminary findings demonstrate the viability of mmWave-iPPG fusion as a promising pathway toward robust, unobtrusive NCBP monitoring."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24 pages, 8 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13701","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-15T01:39:02Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:39:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-25T03:59:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13971","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13971","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13971","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaomeng","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Xiaomeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanyu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yanyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gordon Guocheng","familyName":"Qian","name":"Qian, Gordon Guocheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ivan","familyName":"Skorokhodov","name":"Skorokhodov, Ivan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Viacheslav","familyName":"Ivanov","name":"Ivanov, Viacheslav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Avalon","familyName":"Vinella","name":"Vinella, Avalon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanzhi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yanzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sergey","familyName":"Tulyakov","name":"Tulyakov, Sergey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anil","familyName":"Kag","name":"Kag, Anil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Prompt2Effect: Training-Free Image-to-Video Model Specialization via LoRA Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T23:26:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:15:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-01T01:02:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-02T00:13:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-21T03:38:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-24T00:18:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While personalizing Image-to-Video (I2V) diffusion models with specific visual effects is increasingly demanded for high-end generation, current practice requires training a separate Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) module for each effect, incurring substantial data curation and iterative optimization costs that hinder interactive control. We present Prompt2Effect, a weight-driven hypernetwork that amortizes per-effect training by directly synthesizing effect-specific LoRA weights in a single forward pass. Unlike prior hypernetworks that regress adapter weights purely from semantics, Prompt2Effect is explicitly conditioned on the frozen base model weights, grounding prediction in the structural geometry of each layer. Furthermore, instead of predicting raw LoRA matrices, we introduce an SVD-canonicalized parameterization that resolves factorization ambiguity and stabilizes large-scale synthesis. Extensive experiments demonstrate that Prompt2Effect achieves on-par or superior video quality and effect alignment compared to conventional LoRA fine-tuning, while reducing the computational cost from 56 GPU training hours to 3.3 seconds of hypernetwork inference. When used as initialization for subsequent fine-tuning, our predicted weights further improve final performance and accelerate optimization by approximately 10x."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ECCV 2026, project page: https://xiaomeng-yang.github.io/Prompt2Effect"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13971","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:44:48Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:44:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-24T02:28:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13892","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13892","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13892","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sarah","familyName":"Allen","name":"Allen, Sarah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pranay","familyName":"Anchuri","name":"Anchuri, Pranay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Austgen","name":"Austgen, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maryam","familyName":"Bahrani","name":"Bahrani, Maryam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samuel","familyName":"Breckenridge","name":"Breckenridge, Samuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aaron","familyName":"Buchwald","name":"Buchwald, Aaron","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christian","familyName":"Cachin","name":"Cachin, Christian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrés","familyName":"Fábrega","name":"Fábrega, Andrés","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jared","familyName":"Fernandez","name":"Fernandez, Jared","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James Hsin-yu","familyName":"Chiang","name":"Chiang, James Hsin-yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marwa","familyName":"Mouallem","name":"Mouallem, Marwa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roi","familyName":"Bar-Zur","name":"Bar-Zur, Roi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Neil","familyName":"DeSilva","name":"DeSilva, Neil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ittay","familyName":"Eyal","name":"Eyal, Ittay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giulia","familyName":"Fanti","name":"Fanti, Giulia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ari","familyName":"Juels","name":"Juels, Ari","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Miller","name":"Miller, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christian","familyName":"Sillaber","name":"Sillaber, Christian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dani","familyName":"Vilardell","name":"Vilardell, Dani","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pramod","familyName":"Viswanath","name":"Viswanath, Pramod","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenhao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wenhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matt","familyName":"Weinberg","name":"Weinberg, Matt","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sen","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Sen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianzhu","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Jianzhu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Fan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Crypto x AI, AI x Crypto: A Survey"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T20:32:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:11:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-21T15:44:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-24T00:50:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The intersection of crypto x AI is spawning papers, products, online posts, and companies. All the surrounding buzz, though, obscures what exactly has been done, what the opportunities and challenges are, and what open questions deserve attention. This survey paper asks what AI can do for blockchain-based technologies (broadly construed as \"crypto\") (crypto x AI), and vice versa (AI x crypto). We systematize existing work, summarize key takeaways, highlight open research questions, and offer a perspective on pervasive industry misconceptions, concluding that AI and crypto are still in the very early stages of meaningful integration."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13892","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:43:08Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:43:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-24T02:28:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14370","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14370","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14370","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefanie M.","familyName":"Kampa","name":"Kampa, Stefanie M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthias","familyName":"Schmidt","name":"Schmidt, Matthias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Florian","familyName":"Sammüller","name":"Sammüller, Florian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Spherical metadensity functional learning for inhomogeneous classical fluids"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Soft Condensed Matter (cond-mat.soft)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Statistical Mechanics (cond-mat.stat-mech)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T11:59:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:45:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-20T11:06:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-21T00:40:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We develop classical density functional learning to address fluids with truncated pairwise interparticle interactions in three-dimensional spherical geometry. Simulation data for systems with randomized repulsive pair potentials provide the basis for supervised training of a neural metadensity functional, thereby making efficient use of results for radial distribution functions in the bulk fluid via the test particle route. Specifically, we develop spherical local learning in order to represent the one-body direct correlation functional in terms of a neural network, which captures spatial curvature effects as well as the metadensity functional dependence on the thermally scaled pair potential. The framework yields efficient access to inhomogeneous structuring and related physical phenomena that occur in fluids and general solvents when adsorbed against curved solutes and confined inside of spherical and planar cavities. Test particle setups facilitate accurate prediction of the bulk fluid pair structure and verification of thermodynamic test particle sum rules via functional line integration. Applying the metadensity functional for Henderson inversion allows one to infer accurately the pair potential from the bulk radial distribution function. We address implications of the geometrical setup for two-body quantities and obtain the two-body direct correlation functional from automatic differentiation. For the hard sphere fluid, we confirm metadensity functional predictions against results from a standard neural density functional with fixed pair potential as well as to an analytic functional as given by fundamental measure theory. Simulation results provide further reference and corroborate reliable results of the spherical neural metadensity functional across a broad range of applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"19 pages, 11 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14370","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:53:15Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:53:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-21T02:30:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14336","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14336","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14336","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shubham","familyName":"Yadav","name":"Yadav, Shubham","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hemangi Madhusudan","familyName":"Shah","name":"Shah, Hemangi Madhusudan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Ricci solitons from the perspectives of energy function"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Differential Geometry (math.DG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"53B20, 53B21, 53C25","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T10:46:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:42:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-20T13:36:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-21T00:47:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This article explores to what extent the geometry of gradient Ricci solitons extends to non-gradient Ricci solitons. The primary tool is the energy function $E$ of the soliton. We study consequences of various bounds on $E$. Under mild assumptions on the scalar curvature, we prove a weighted $L^1$-Liouville type theorem for both the usual Laplacian and the drifted Laplacian $Δ_V$ associated to soliton vector field $V$, the former of which implies that Ricci solitons with bounded energy function have at most one nonparabolic end. Finally, we show that the measure $e^{-E} \\mathrm{d} \\mathrm{Vol}_{g}$ is finite for complete shrinking Ricci solitons, partially generalizing a result of Aaron Naber. As a consequence, non-gradient shrinking Ricci solitons also have finite fundamental groups, as in the gradient case."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"21 pages; The manuscript is modified"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14336","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:52:32Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:52:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-21T02:29:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13888","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13888","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13888","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pier Giuseppe","familyName":"Ledda","name":"Ledda, Pier Giuseppe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giacomo","familyName":"Ferrari","name":"Ferrari, Giacomo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giuseppe Antonio","familyName":"Zampogna","name":"Zampogna, Giuseppe Antonio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Nested homogenization of xylem-inspired porous fluidic networks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Fluid Dynamics (physics.flu-dyn)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T20:24:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:10:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-18T20:37:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-21T01:00:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Fluidic networks may contain hierarchical porous structures in which transport across macroscopic interfaces is controlled by geometrical features at multiple nested length scales. Direct resolution of all these scales rapidly becomes computationally prohibitive for network-scale studies, while reduced descriptions require a priori effective hydraulic properties. Here, we develop a nested homogenization framework for rigid porous interfaces under single-phase viscous flow. The pore-scale structure is first replaced by an effective stress-jump interface law, which is then embedded as an internal interface condition within a second characteristic problem at the intermediate scale. This successive upscaling propagates the pore-scale geometry through effective tensors to obtain a closure for flow across the whole porous structure. The approach is validated against fully resolved simulations at the mesoscopic and macroscopic scales, for two xylem-inspired channel configurations, and accurately captures pressure drops and flow redistribution while strongly reducing computational cost. The framework provides a tractable bottom-up description of a network composed of nested porous membranes and a basis for extensions to deformable structures and multiphase flows."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13888","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:43:03Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:43:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-21T02:29:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13936","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13936","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13936","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francesco","familyName":"Sinigaglia","name":"Sinigaglia, Francesco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The baryonic Tully-Fisher relation as an independent direct probe of cosmology and of the nature of dark matter"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cosmology and Nongalactic Astrophysics (astro-ph.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T21:54:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:13:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-19T08:47:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-20T00:36:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The baryonic Tully-Fisher relation (BTFR), a well-established galaxy scaling relation linking the dynamical mass of rotation-supported galaxies through their maximum circular velocity to the baryonic luminous mass, has emerged over the decades as a fundamental scaling relation and as a robust calibrated distance indicator, thereby providing a robust benchmark to test galaxy formation and evolution theories as well as an independent probe of the expansion of the Universe. In this paper, we show for the first time that the BTFR is also simultaneously directly sensitive to the cosmological parameters $Ω_m$ and $σ_8$, the astrophysical feedback from supernovae (SNe) and active galactic nuclei (AGN), and the mass of warm dark matter particles $M_{\\rm wdm}$, and can therefore be used as novel, direct probe of cosmology and fundamental physics. We perform simulation-based inference on the large DREAMS cosmological magneto-hydrodynamic simulations suite and train deep neural networks in the form of normalizing flows to estimate the posterior distributions of $Ω_m$, $σ_8$, $M_{\\rm wdm}$ and the three astrophysical free parameters, given a BTFR measurement. Our framework is able to recover unbiased values for $Ω_m$ and $σ_8$, with subpercent deviations accuracy and a $\\sim 2.6\\%$ and $\\sim 3.9\\%$ median precision, respectively, to capture the warm dark matter particle mass $M_{\\rm wdm}$ within a $\\sim 30-35\\%$ precision, as well as to constrain the SN feedback parameters (but not the one regulating AGN feedback). We conclude that, beyond its usage as a distance indicator, the BTFR constitutes a direct independent probe of cosmology and fundamental physics and opens new promising avenues, such as constraining the cosmic growth rate and testing gravity theories, to be explored with the future Square Kilometer Array."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 7 figures. Accepted for publication in A\u0026amp;A"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13936","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:44:03Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:44:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-20T02:22:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13840","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13840","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13840","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Senkang","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Senkang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengru","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Zhengru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihang","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Yihang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihan","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Zihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiqin","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Yiqin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuguang","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Yuguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi-Agent Embodied Autonomous Driving (MAEAD): From V2X Information Exchange to Shared World Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T19:10:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:08:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-12T11:55:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-13T00:41:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-16T11:22:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-18T00:53:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-19T09:51:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-08-20T00:39:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Autonomous driving is shifting from isolated vehicle intelligence toward multi-agent embodied systems that share perception, infer intent, and coordinate action under uncertainty. This survey examines this transition through the lens of Shared World Models (SWMs): predictive cross-agent representations maintained across vehicles, infrastructure, and other traffic participants. We review approximately 400 publications covering vehicle-to-everything (V2X) communication, collaborative perception, inter-agent cognition, cooperative planning, end-to-end cooperative driving, and simulation and data engines for closed-loop validation. The organizing question is how exchanged observations become aligned state, intent-aware interaction, and coordinated downstream action. Across the surveyed literature, evaluation remains concentrated in simulation, curated benchmarks, and offline protocols. Foundation-model-based coordination also lacks verifiable real-time safety guarantees in open traffic. These gaps motivate key research priorities for multi-agent embodied autonomous driving (MAEAD): verifiable shared-state maintenance, robust intent and plan alignment, and safe coordinated action under communication and computing constraints in real-world deployment. We maintain an open-source project to continuously track the latest developments at https://github.com/dl-m9/Multi-Agent-Embodied-Autonomous-Driving."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13840","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:41:58Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:41:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-20T02:22:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14438","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14438","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14438","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zikun","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Zikun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minglan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Minglan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinyou","familyName":"Zhai","name":"Zhai, Jinyou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rongjin","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Rongjin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Physics-Grounded Causal Auditing of End-to-End Driving Planners"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T13:19:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:48:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-16T09:38:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:42:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-04T01:47:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:17:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-18T01:51:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-08-19T00:21:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"End-to-end (E2E) autonomous-driving planners trained by imitation are prone to statistical shortcuts: they associate scene elements that merely co-occur with expert actions (a roadside object, a building facade) with driving decisions, rather than the variables that causally determine them. Such causal confusion silently compromises reliability in long-tail scenarios, and it is difficult to detect, because prevailing open-loop metrics (L2 displacement and collision rate) are dominated by ego status and do not indicate whether a planner depends on spurious cues. Existing remedies based on causal-intervention training require retraining large models and cannot audit a planner that is already deployed. We present CADET, a training-free framework that audits, benchmarks, and repairs spurious reliance in pretrained E2E planners without any parameter update."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8pages 4figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14438","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:54:41Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:54:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-19T02:28:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14174","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14174","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14174","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Z. C.","familyName":"Tu","name":"Tu, Z. C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Universal Construction of Generalized Lyapunov Functions for Nonlinear Dynamical Systems Using Physics-Informed Neural Networks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Chaotic Dynamics (nlin.CD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Statistical Mechanics (cond-mat.stat-mech)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T06:56:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:31:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-18T03:37:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-19T00:26:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A scalar potential landscape provides an intuitive description of the stability, transitions, and global organization of dynamical systems. For non-gradient dynamics, however, constructing global Lyapunov-type functions for nonlinear flows with recurrent structures remains a formidable challenge. Here, we introduce the generalized Lyapunov function (GLF), a scalar potential that is monotonically non-increasing along deterministic trajectories, as a unifying framework for nonequilibrium potentials. Conventional Lyapunov functions, Freidlin--Wentzell quasi-potentials, and potentials arising from Ao-type decompositions naturally emerge as special cases within this framework. Furthermore, we develop a data-free physics-informed neural network (PINN) approach in which the Lyapunov inequality and a weak divergence-scale compatibility condition are directly embedded into the loss function. The framework is demonstrated on diverse systems, including linear dynamics, the Hopf normal form, the van der Pol oscillator, a three-dimensional Hopf-link flow, and symmetric and asymmetric competitive Lotka--Volterra systems. The learned landscapes agree closely with analytical results where available and, more generally, successfully reveal invariant sets as low- or constant-potential structures. In particular, the van der Pol oscillator, Hopf-link flow, and asymmetric Lotka--Volterra system demonstrate that coherent GLFs can be constructed without relying on known closed-form expressions, establishing a versatile route to potential-landscape construction for complex nonlinear non-gradient systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14174","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:49:04Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:49:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-19T02:28:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14126","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14126","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14126","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takeshi","familyName":"Chiba","name":"Chiba, Takeshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keiju","familyName":"Murata","name":"Murata, Keiju","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daiki","familyName":"Saito","name":"Saito, Daiki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Refocusing of Wheeler--DeWitt wave functions at inner horizons"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Theory (hep-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T05:16:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:27:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-18T01:51:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-19T00:21:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study quantum gravitational effects inside hyperbolic black holes with both outer and inner horizons by solving the Wheeler--DeWitt (WDW) equation in the minisuperspace approximation. The WDW equation contains a tachyonic region where the effective potential becomes negative. We develop a numerical method that consistently evolves the wave function across this region and obtain stable solutions throughout the entire minisuperspace. For small values of the parameter $κ$, which controls the strength of quantum gravitational effects, the wave packet propagates along the classical trajectory with only mild quantum spreading. As $κ$ increases, enhanced quantum effects lead to significant spreading of the wave packet during its propagation. Nevertheless, when the initial state is localized near the outer horizon, the wave packet becomes localized again in the vicinity of the inner horizon. We refer to this recovery of localization as a refocusing phenomenon. This result suggests that, if the geometry is classical near the outer horizon, it becomes classical again near the inner horizon. Within the minisuperspace approximation, inner-horizon formation is not obstructed by quantum gravitational effects."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24 pages, 7 figures, replaced to match the accepted version in PRD"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14126","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:48:04Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:48:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-19T02:28:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14622","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14622","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14622","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Licong","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Licong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Íñigo","familyName":"Zubeldia","name":"Zubeldia, Íñigo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Alvey","name":"Alvey, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boris","familyName":"Bolliet","name":"Bolliet, Boris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anthony","familyName":"Challinor","name":"Challinor, Anthony","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Impact of non-Gaussian likelihood on cosmological constraints from the thermal Sunyaev--Zel'dovich power spectrum: a simulation-based inference analysis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cosmology and Nongalactic Astrophysics (astro-ph.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Instrumentation and Methods for Astrophysics (astro-ph.IM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T16:47:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-18T00:32:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1103/tl9p-ldwf","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The thermal Sunyaev--Zel'dovich (tSZ) power spectrum is a sensitive probe of cosmology and cluster astrophysics, but its statistics are non-Gaussian because the signal receives a significant contribution from rare, massive, low-redshift galaxy clusters. As a result, a Gaussian likelihood fails to describe the statistics of its power spectrum on large scales. We use simulation-based inference (SBI) to test the accuracy of the standard Gaussian power-spectrum likelihood for a \\textit{Planck}-like tSZ analysis. Using halo-based simulations of full-sky Compton-$y$ maps, we train neural posterior and likelihood estimators and compare the resulting constraints with those from a Gaussian likelihood assumption. Using only multipoles $\\ell \u0026lt; 1000$, we find that the Gaussian likelihood assumption gives unbiased cosmological constraints, while the SBI-based inference shows a mild broadening of the posterior distributions for the amplitudes of residual foregrounds. This suggests that the Gaussian likelihood assumption is sufficiently accurate for cosmological inference for a \\textit{Planck}-like tSZ analysis, while SBI provides a useful validation tool to model non-Gaussian likelihoods beyond analytic approximations."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"22 pages, 15 figures, submitted to Physical Review D"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14622","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-15T01:58:33Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:58:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-18T04:05:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14274","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14274","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14274","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sun Woo","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Sun Woo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Secondary terms for first moments of Selmer groups of twists of elliptic curves over global function fields"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Number Theory (math.NT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Probability (math.PR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"11G05, 11R58, 60J20","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T08:56:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:37:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-17T16:09:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-18T01:47:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Let $E$ be a non-isotrivial elliptic curve over a global function field $\\mathbb{F}_q(t)$ of characteristic coprime to $2$ and $3$. Under some explicit conditions, we determine the secondary terms for the first moments of prime Selmer groups of cyclic prime twist families of $E$ over $\\mathbb{F}_q(t)$."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Version 2: Minor corrections. 29 Pages. Comments Welcome!"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14274","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:51:12Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:51:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-18T04:05:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14225","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14225","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14225","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqing","familyName":"Kong","name":"Kong, Yuqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Finite-Sample Unbiasedly Estimable Information Monotones"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Theory (cs.IT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Science and Game Theory (cs.GT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T08:06:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:34:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-16T03:24:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-18T00:45:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Which measures of statistical dependence can be estimated unbiasedly from a fixed number of samples while satisfying the data processing inequality (DPI)? For finite alphabets, finite-sample unbiased estimability is equivalent to polynomial dependence on the distribution, and the minimum polynomial degree equals the exact sample complexity.\n Let \\(U\\inΔ_{n,m}\\) be the joint-distribution matrix, with stochastic post-processing applied to the \\(n\\)-state row variable. Our main classification concerns fixed-alphabet left-sided DPI. We first prove a result beyond polynomiality: for every \\(n,m\\ge2\\), any functional that satisfies left-sided DPI, vanishes under independence, and extends to a \\(C^1\\) function on a neighborhood of the probability simplex must vanish whenever \\(U\\) has a zero row. If DPI is strengthened to allow changes in the processed alphabet size, every such cross-dimensional family is identically zero.\n For fixed-alphabet DPI, this gives a sharp trichotomy. If \\(n\u0026gt;m\\), every such \\(C^1\\)-extendable functional is identically zero. If \\(n=m\\), every nonzero admissible polynomial is divisible on the simplex by \\((\\det U)^2\\), giving the sharp degree bound \\(2n\\). If \\(n"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14225","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:50:09Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:50:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-18T04:05:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14038","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14038","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14038","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dapeng","familyName":"Zhan","name":"Zhan, Dapeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Trivariate Hypergeometric Series Formulas for Pure Partition Functions of Multiple $3$-SLE$_κ$"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Probability (math.PR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"60J67, 33C65, 30C35, 34M03","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T02:26:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:21:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-16T02:48:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-18T00:44:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Pure partition functions of multiple SLE are characterized by null-state partial differential equations, Möbius covariance, and boundary asymptotics. After quotienting by Möbius covariance, the case of three curves is the first genuinely multivariable one: the moduli space has three independent variables, naturally represented by the three unoriented cross-ratios of the three pairs of links.\n We solve this Möbius-normalized three-variable problem for the two basic link-pattern types of multiple $3$-SLE$_κ$, namely the rainbow and neighbor patterns. Writing $β=4/κ$, we construct explicit trivariate hypergeometric-series normal forms and identify them with the corresponding pure partition functions {for all $β\u0026gt;1/2$ in both cases, equivalently for all $κ\\in(0,8)$}.\n The proof is analytic. The null-state PDEs and Möbius covariance yield recursion relations for the trivariate coefficient arrays. In the rainbow case, coefficient estimates give convergence and boundary regularity on the closed cube. In the neighbor case, Pfaff systems continue the local power series to a neighborhood of $[0,1)^3$, while {boundary analysis and corner propagation} give continuity on $[0,1]^3$ {throughout the full range $β\u0026gt;1/2$}. The two-dimensional boundary degenerations are classical Appell $F_1$ and Horn $G_2$ functions.\n The probabilistic identification uses SLE martingale arguments and Itô calculus, together with positivity and boundary regularity. We also discuss boundary degenerations, including heuristic connections with boundary Green's functions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"103 pages; revised version extends the neighbor-case result from $\\kappa\\in(0,6]$ to the full range $\\kappa\\in(0,8)$; new Section 6.4 and related revisions"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14038","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:46:13Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:46:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-18T04:05:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13979","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13979","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13979","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiguang","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Shiguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Cohn-Vossen theory for locally conformally flat manifolds"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Differential Geometry (math.DG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T23:54:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:16:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-17T09:28:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-18T01:25:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We establish a refined singularity estimate for nonnegative n-superharmonic functions. For complete noncompact locally conformally flat manifolds with nonnegative Ricci curvature, we analytically characterize the volume growth, verify Yau's conjecture on the Cohn-Vossen inequality, and prove a sharp gap theorem that removes all auxiliary assumptions from earlier works."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"48 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13979","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:44:58Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:44:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-18T04:05:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14369","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14369","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14369","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Erik","familyName":"Pfister","name":"Pfister, Erik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matti","familyName":"Schulze","name":"Schulze, Matti","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jörg","familyName":"Stiller","name":"Stiller, Jörg","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Local high order space-time adaptive MLSDC"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Numerical Analysis (math.NA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T11:57:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:44:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T12:46:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-16T01:44:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-13T13:51:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-14T00:51:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Building upon the semi-implicit multilevel spectral deferred correction (SI-MLSDC) method introduced by Pfister and Stiller [38], this work presents a 1D space-time adaptive high-order method combining discontinuous Galerkin spectral element discretizations with multilevel spectral deferred corrections. The proposed approach enables genuine arbitrary-order accuracy in space and time while dynamically balancing spatial and temporal discretization errors to reduce computational cost. A key contribution is the development of a novel temporal error estimator that in combination with a spectral error estimator in space provides a reliable basis for adaptive refinement decisions. The error estimator is compared with two alternative refinement criteria to assess their impact on accuracy, computational efficiency and their suitability for complex problems. The performance of the adaptive method is demonstrated for nonlinear conservation laws ranging from Burgers' equation to the Euler equations. Numerical results show substantial runtime reductions while maintaining the desired accuracy. In particular, significant computational savings are achieved for Burgers' equation, and challenging benchmark problems such as the Shu-Osher shock-fluctuation benchmark. These results demonstrate the potential of adaptive SI-MLSDC methods for efficient high-order space-time adaptive simulations of complex flow problems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14369","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:53:14Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:53:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-14T02:27:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13820","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13820","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13820","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stelios","familyName":"Stefas","name":"Stefas, Stelios","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"George","familyName":"Zoupanos","name":"Zoupanos, George","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Unifying Gravities with Internal Interactions based on $SO(10)$ GUT"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Theory (hep-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T18:51:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:07:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-13T14:28:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-14T00:53:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Building on two key ingredients, namely the well established gauge-theoretic formulation of gravity and the observation that the tangent space of a curved manifold need not have the same dimension as the manifold, we discuss how all known fundamental interactions can be accommodated within a single unified framework. The unification is realised by enlarging the tangent group of the four-dimensional spacetime manifold to $SO(2,16)$, a choice that simultaneously encompasses both the gauge group underlying the gravitational sector and the $SO(10)$ Grand Unified Theory for the internal interactions. The gravitational theories entering this construction are Conformal Gravity and Fuzzy (Noncommutative) Gravity, each formulated in gauge-theoretic terms."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, no figures, contribution to the Proceedings of the International Conference \"Nonlinearity, Nonlocality and Ultrametricity\" on the Occasion of Branko Dragovic's 80th Birthday, Belgrade, Serbia, 26-30 May 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13820","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:41:33Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:41:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-14T02:27:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13974","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13974","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13974","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kei","familyName":"Iida","name":"Iida, Kei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Etsuko","familyName":"Itou","name":"Itou, Etsuko","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kotaro","familyName":"Murakami","name":"Murakami, Kotaro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daiki","familyName":"Suenaga","name":"Suenaga, Daiki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Hadron spectra of finite-density QC$_2$D"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Lattice (hep-lat)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Phenomenology (hep-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Nuclear Experiment (nucl-ex)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Nuclear Theory (nucl-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T23:37:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:15:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-11T23:56:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-13T00:11:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We investigate the chemical-potential dependence of hadron spectra in two-color QCD using first-principles lattice simulations. We compute two-point correlation functions for all allowed hadronic operators by newly including the contributions from disconnected diagrams, and extract the corresponding effective masses. In the meson sector, the mass hierarchy in the hadronic phase (normal vacuum) is found to be $m_π\\lesssim m_η \u0026lt; m_σ\\mathrm{(noisy)} \u0026lt; m_ρ\\sim m_ω\\ll m_{a_1}$, which is similar to that in three-color QCD. In the superfluid phase, this hierarchy is modified, and with increasing density it changes to $m_σ\\mathrm{(noisy)} \u0026lt; m_{a_1} \u0026lt; m_ρ\u0026lt; m_π\\sim m_η \\mathrm{(noisy)} \\ll m_ω \\mathrm{(noisy)}$. In the diquark sector, the ordering remains as $m_{NG} \\lesssim m_{I=0, S} \u0026lt; m_{I=1, AV} \u0026lt; m_{I=0, PS} \\lesssim m_{I=0, V}$ in both phases, and the Nambu--Goldstone mode associated with spontaneous breaking of $U(1)_B$ is confirmed to be nearly massless. Furthermore, by comparing correlators for chiral partners, we find indications of chiral symmetry restoration at high density."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"42pages, 30figures; (ver2) Minor improvements; correlation-function data made publicly available on Zenodo (Ref.[66])"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13974","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:44:51Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:44:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-13T02:15:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13833","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13833","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13833","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luca","familyName":"Leoni","name":"Leoni, Luca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cesare","familyName":"Franchini","name":"Franchini, Cesare","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Machine-learned dynamics of surface polarons at reduced oxide surfaces"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Physics (physics.comp-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T19:03:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:08:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-22T19:44:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-13T00:33:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1021/acs.jpclett.6c02034","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reducible oxides exhibit a rich interplay of electronic, structural, and chemical properties that underpins applications in catalysis, photovoltaics, batteries, and energy storage. This interplay is strongly shaped by excess electrons, often introduced by oxygen vacancies, that localize as small polarons and influence charge transport and surface chemistry. At surfaces, these polarons play a central role in charge localization, mobility, and reactivity, yet their finite-temperature dynamics remain difficult to access from first principles due to the long time scales needed to adequately sample polaron's hopping. To overcome this limitation, we extend machine-learning-assisted polaron dynamics to redox-active oxide surfaces, using oxygen-deficient rutile TiO$_2$(110) as a paradigmatic case. By accessing several nanoseconds of dynamics over a range of temperatures, we show that small-polaron mobility at the reduced rutile TiO$_2$(110) surface is suppressed by several orders of magnitude relative to the corresponding bulk material, providing a microscopic interpretation of the lower electron mobilities observed in porous rutile TiO$_2$ compared with single-crystal samples. This suppressed mobility arises from the loss of favourable hopping pathways: surface polaron motion is largely confined to planar inter-row trajectories within the second topmost layers, with only rare interlayer hopping events. These results establish a transferable machine-learning strategy for investigating polaron dynamics in reducible oxides."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13833","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-15T01:41:49Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:41:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-13T02:15:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14498","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14498","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14498","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunhong","familyName":"Lou","name":"Lou, Yunhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xihang","familyName":"Yue","name":"Yue, Xihang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinran","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Xinran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianqi","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Tianqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linchao","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Linchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Fixed-Point Neural Operator for Size- and Functional-Transferable Hamiltonian Prediction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Chemical Physics (physics.chem-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T14:31:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:52:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-11T14:40:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-12T00:59:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Predicting the Kohn-Sham Hamiltonian with machine learning can accelerate density functional theory while retaining access to molecular orbitals, energy levels, and electronic-structure observables that energy-only surrogates cannot resolve. Yet element-wise agreement with the converged Hamiltonian, an implicit fixed point of the self-consistent field iteration, does not determine the occupied subspace that governs orbital energies and densities. Here we present HamEvo, a neural operator that learns the single-step self-consistent update and returns the converged Hamiltonian as its fixed point. HamEvo is pre-trained on intermediate self-consistent trajectories and calibrated at equilibrium with density-matrix supervision. Across benchmarks from MD17 to drug-like QMugs, HamEvo lowers Hamiltonian errors by 35-49% over direct-regression and deep-equilibrium baselines, and predicts QMugs HOMO and LUMO energies with mean absolute errors of 0.036 and 0.053 eV, near the 1 kcal/mol chemical-accuracy scale. Few-shot fine-tuning with only 20 reference conformations extends HamEvo to molecules of up to 122 atoms, well beyond the size range covered by pre-training. With thermal molecular-dynamics sampling, HamEvo captures temperature-dependent HOMO-LUMO gap renormalization beyond the harmonic approximation. Inference is up to 242 times faster than conventional DFT."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"30 pages, 5 figures, 2 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14498","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:55:58Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:55:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-12T02:37:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14064","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14064","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14064","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eve","familyName":"Tsybina","name":"Tsybina, Eve","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicholson","familyName":"Koukpaizan","name":"Koukpaizan, Nicholson","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joshua","familyName":"Hambrick","name":"Hambrick, Joshua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samim","familyName":"Konjicija","name":"Konjicija, Samim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Slaven","familyName":"Peles","name":"Peles, Slaven","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HPC-Enabled Generator Importance Assessment for RTO-Scale Resource Adequacy Planning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T03:19:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-12T01:11:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Modern power systems are increasingly under stress as aging assets approach retirement and load growth outpaces new generation construction. The severity of this challenge varies by region: in the EU, the transmission grid can partially compensate for local generation shortfalls, while in the US, generation tends to be more localized, making retirements harder to offset. Retirement of generation has consequences for system reserves, fuel supply chain, and public health. We present an high-performance computing (HPC) framework for rapidly assessing the grid importance of individual generating units and ranking them by primary fuel type, operating cost, or grid impact. Historically, such studies were computationally intensive and therefore conducted infrequently. This work demonstrates that such assessments can be completed in minutes, enabling planners to evaluate a much broader range of generation portfolio scenarios than was previously possible."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14064","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:46:46Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:46:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-12T02:37:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14574","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14574","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14574","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoxin","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Xiaoxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ranran Haoran","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ranran Haoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SIMMER: Benchmarking Latent Failures in LLM Executable Planning with a World Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T15:53:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:56:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-09T04:57:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-11T00:54:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed as planners for autonomous agents in household environments. While existing benchmarks evaluate whether LLM-generated plans execute successfully, they overlook a critical type of failure: latent failures. Unlike immediate failures that trigger instant feedback at execution time and enable timely correction, latent failures do not immediately halt plan execution but silently compromise goal achievement. In severe cases, they cause irreversible harm. To address this gap, we introduce SIMMER, a benchmark for evaluating latent failures in LLM planning through a human-curated symbolic world model grounded in the kitchen domain. SIMMER defines a world model comprising 77 actions, 262 unique objects, and approximately 46,800 possible interactions that are semantically realistic, derived from real-world cooking scripts. It then leverages a state machine executor that validates plans against the world model and detects immediate precondition violations, latent hazards, and irreversible failures. Experiments across six LLMs show that even frontier models achieve at most 17% error-free plans. Moreover, up to 56% of plans contain latent failures, the majority of which lead to irreversible consequences. We further demonstrate that explicit state reasoning via counterfactual foresight simulation can reduce latent failures by up to 72% and irreversible cases by up to 75%, suggesting a promising direction for more robust LLM planners."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at COLM 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14574","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:57:34Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:57:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-11T04:23:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14022","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14022","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14022","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yash Vardhan","familyName":"Tomar","name":"Tomar, Yash Vardhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aryav","familyName":"Das","name":"Das, Aryav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Placing Degree Scales After LayerNorm"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T01:43:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:18:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-23T22:20:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-27T00:09:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-08T16:55:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-11T00:41:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Graph neural networks (GNNs) are widely used to learn node-selection policies on graphs, and most stack graph attention (GAT) blocks with LayerNorm. On degree-sensitive tasks, LayerNorm tends to remove the degree signal these models need to rank nodes. Much recent work addresses this by redesigning normalizers or aggregators, which changes what these components compute but does not ask where, relative to LayerNorm, a degree scale should be applied. In this paper, we show that the answer follows from a single algebraic fact about LayerNorm. When a positive per-node scale is applied before LayerNorm, LayerNorm divides it out, and it never reaches the model's output. Applied after LayerNorm, the same scale comes through and reaches the score head as magnitude. From this placement rule we derive PostDeg, a parameter-free inverse-degree scale that we add as the single change to a fixed GAT backbone. PostDeg multiplies each node's normalized representation by an inverse function of its degree, and we compare it against controls in the same position. At every evaluation size, PostDeg improves over the LayerNorm backbone on influence maximization, network dismantling, and maximum independent set, and these controls show where the improvement comes from. The same scale before LayerNorm stays at the backbone, as the absorption identity predicts, and a constant scale after LayerNorm stays there too on all but the most heavy-tailed graphs, so the effect needs both the position after LayerNorm and a degree-dependent scale. The exact form of the scale matters much less, so we recommend PostDeg, which needs no tuning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14022","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:45:53Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:45:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-11T04:23:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14695","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14695","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14695","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinsu","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Jinsu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jihoon","familyName":"Tack","name":"Tack, Jihoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Noah","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Noah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jongheon","familyName":"Jeong","name":"Jeong, Jongheon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Persona-Pruner: Sculpting Lightweight Models for Role-Playing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T17:58:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T01:02:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-06T03:30:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-07T00:21:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Language Models (LMs) have shown remarkable potential as role-playing chatbots, delivering consistent, stylized interactions when given a specification of a character or user persona. However, applying these capabilities to real-world applications (e.g., ecosystems with numerous NPCs interacting simultaneously) exposes a critical inefficiency due to the excessive computational cost. In this paper, we question the necessity of dedicating a full, generalist model to a single persona, hypothesizing that a specific character identity relies on only a fraction of the model's total capacity. We observe that naively pruning LMs often severely degrades the role-playing performance for a specific persona; it does not distinguish between redundant knowledge and essential character traits. We propose Persona-Pruner, a framework that sculpts a lightweight role-playing model by isolating persona-specific sub-networks from a single description. Our experiments consistently show that Persona-Pruner preserves role-playing performance substantially more effectively than existing state-of-the-art LLM pruning techniques, reducing the performance drop from the dense model by up to 93.8% over the strongest baseline on RoleBench in LLM-as-a-judge score, while still maintaining general LLM capabilities. Code is available at https://github.com/jsu-kim/Persona-Pruner."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"25 pages; ICML 2026; Code is available at https://github.com/jsu-kim/Persona-Pruner"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14695","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T02:00:06Z","registered":"2026-06-15T02:00:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-07T02:19:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14591","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14591","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14591","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui","familyName":"Geng","name":"Geng, Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zijian","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Zijian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianjiao","familyName":"Wan","name":"Wan, Tianjiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qisheng","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Qisheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jiaxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hengzhu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Hengzhu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kele","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Kele","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AudioDER: A Deduplication-Enhanced Reasoning Dataset for Post-Training Large Audio-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T16:09:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:57:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T12:28:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-06T00:43:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in pretrained large audio-language models (LALMs) have demonstrated strong capabilities across speech, sound, and music. To adapt these models to downstream tasks without the cost of pretraining from scratch, post-training has become a widely adopted paradigm. However, the effectiveness of post-training depends critically on the quality of the training corpus. We observe that existing post-training corpora, often constructed by aggregating public audio datasets, suffer from substantial acoustic redundancy, as many of these datasets are sourced from overlapping media platforms. Such redundancy leads to repeated exposure to similar acoustic patterns, causing diminishing returns in performance despite increased data volume. address this issue, we propose a three-stage data construction pipeline that performs acoustic redundancy filtering, converts retained samples into a unified multiple-choice question-answering format with chain-of-thought generation, and finally applies quality verification and filtering. Using this pipeline, we construct AudioRE, a post-training dataset of approximately 286k instances spanning sound, speech, and music. Supervised fine-tuning on AudioRE consistently improves the performance of Qwen2-Audio-7B-Instruct across diverse audio understanding and reasoning benchmarks, outperforming models trained on the unfiltered raw corpus with substantially more instances. These results validate the effectiveness of our redundancy-aware data construction pipeline and the resulting AudioRE dataset, and further highlight the importance of minimizing acoustic redundancy in audio-language post-training. To facilitate future research, we will release both the AudioRE and the fine-tuned Qwen2-AudioRE checkpoint."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14591","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:57:55Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:57:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-06T02:19:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14363","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14363","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14363","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amartya","familyName":"Bose","name":"Bose, Amartya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Quantum-Classical Hierarchical Equations of Motion"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Chemical Physics (physics.chem-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T11:45:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:44:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:46:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-06T00:14:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We develop a quantum-classical hierarchical equations of motion (QC-HEOM) approach for simulating non-Markovian open quantum systems. The method combines the ensemble-averaged classical path reference of the quantum-classical path integral formalism with a hierarchy of auxiliary quantum influence functionals. By incorporating thermal fluctuations through an ensemble average over reference trajectories, the hierarchy is required to represent only the residual quantum memory associated with the imaginary part of the bath response function. Consequently, unlike conventional hierarchical equations of motion, QC-HEOM does not require Matsubara or Padé expansions of the thermal kernel and exhibits only weak temperature dependence of the hierarchy size. Furthermore, because thermal fluctuations are supplied through reference classical trajectories, the framework naturally extends beyond harmonic baths and enables the incorporation of anharmonic and molecular environments through externally generated trajectories. We derive the formalism and demonstrate its exactness for a harmonic bath. Applications to an asymmetric spin-boson model and the seven-site Fenna--Matthews--Olson complex illustrate the accuracy of QC-HEOM. It reproduces benchmark quasi-adiabatic path integral and hierarchical equations of motion results while requiring substantially fewer auxiliary objects, particularly at low temperatures. These results establish QC-HEOM as an efficient framework for treating residual quantum memory in quantum-classical descriptions of open-system dynamics. The separation of thermal fluctuations from residual quantum memory through the use of Wigner trajectories provides an approximate route toward hierarchical treatments of complex anharmonic environments that are inaccessible to conventional HEOM approaches."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"11 pages, 4 figures, 1 table"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14363","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:53:06Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:53:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-06T02:19:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13985","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13985","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13985","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Catherine","familyName":"Rodriquez","name":"Rodriquez, Catherine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Ghawaly","name":"Ghawaly, James, Jr","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Co-Evolved Spiking Neural Network Ensembles via Marginal Contribution Fitness"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Neural and Evolutionary Computing (cs.NE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T00:04:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-06T00:53:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3822454.3822482","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Evolutionary optimization of spiking neural networks (SNNs) becomes increasingly difficult as task complexity grows because they must search a combined topology--parameter space that grows super-exponentially with network size. We address this scaling challenge through a co-evolutionary ensemble framework in which a population of candidate SNNs is evolved with fitness defined by each network's marginal contribution to group performance. Grounded in cooperative game theory and difference evaluation functions from multiagent systems, this credit assignment rewards networks that consistently improve ensemble performance and penalizes redundancy, encouraging complementary specialization during evolution rather than relying on post-hoc combination of independently trained networks. We evaluate the approach on classification, regression, and control tasks under $μ$Caspian neuromorphic hardware constraints. Co-evolved ensembles achieve statistically significant improvements over both single-network evolution and post-hoc ensembles across all tasks, with the most pronounced gains in control, where standard evolution fails to discover effective policies and co-evolution enables a qualitative transition to near-optimal performance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at the 2026 ACM International Conference on Neuromorphic Systems (ACM ICONS 2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13985","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-15T01:45:05Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:45:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-06T02:19:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14643","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14643","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14643","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Patrice","familyName":"Theule","name":"Theule, Patrice","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Veronique","familyName":"Buat","name":"Buat, Veronique","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Denis","familyName":"Burgarella","name":"Burgarella, Denis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Estelle","familyName":"Pons","name":"Pons, Estelle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mederic","familyName":"Boquien","name":"Boquien, Mederic","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Nebular_AGN: A CIGALE module for fitting active galactic nucleus emission lines"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Astrophysics of Galaxies (astro-ph.GA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T17:07:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T01:00:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T19:33:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:07:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-21T15:32:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-22T00:57:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-22T01:33:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-07-23T00:12:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-08-04T08:57:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:39:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Aims. The increasing discovery of high-redshift AGNs in recent years imposes more stringent requirements on spectral analysis tools for deriving the properties of AGNs and their host galaxies from emission-line diagnostics. To address this need, we develop a new module for the popular SED-fitting tool Code Investigating GALaxy Emission (CIGALE), the [nebular_AGN] module, which enables the efficient and flexible simulation and fitting of emission lines originating from the broad-line regions (BLRs) and narrow-line regions (NLRs) of AGNs, and allows the estimation of the physical properties of these regions.\n Methods. We use the spectral synthesis code Cloudy to construct the database for the new module. Based on the X-ray and accretion disk continua implemented in CIGALE, we generate the incident radiation fields of the models. We then adopt the AGN geometry and dust settings implemented in CIGALE to define a flexible set of physical parameters for the gas clouds, thereby producing a comprehensive database for the [nebular_AGN] module.\n Results. We benchmark the [nebular_AGN] module using a quasar composite spectrum, an empirical metallicity calibration, and observational data from X-ray-selected AGNs. Our module can approximately reproduce the majority of quasar emission-line profiles, cover the key emission-line ratios observed in AGN samples, and provide an assessment of their physical properties. For specific combinations of parameters, the metallicity derived by our module is consistent with the empirical formula. We further compare our models with other photoionization models used to simulate AGN NLR emission, and perform a line-sensitivity study to identify the most effective diagnostic lines for each parameter in our module. Finally, we confirm that the dust attenuation law plays an important role in SED fitting."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14643","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:59:00Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:59:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-05T02:34:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14356","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14356","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14356","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Milos","familyName":"Gravara","name":"Gravara, Milos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cynthia","familyName":"Marcelino","name":"Marcelino, Cynthia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrija","familyName":"Stanisic","name":"Stanisic, Andrija","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefan","familyName":"Nastic","name":"Nastic, Stefan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PLAIground: SLO-Driven Runtime Model Selection for Compound AI Systems in the Edge-Cloud-Space Continuum"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T11:34:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:39:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/smartcomp-companion70724.2026.00061","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Applications in the 3D Computing Continuum, which unifies edge, cloud, and space, require combining multiple AI tasks such as object detection, time-series analytics, and natural language processing into Compound AI systems. These systems must satisfy stringent Service Level Objectives (SLOs) on accuracy, latency, and cost. A key mechanism for maintaining SLO compliance of Compound AI systems is runtime model selection, where AI models are dynamically switched for each workflow task. However, existing distributed and compound AI frameworks do not natively support runtime model selection. We present PLAIground, a framework that enables runtime model selection for Compound AI systems. PLAIground introduces Compoundable AI Model (CAIM) abstraction, which decouples task semantics from AI model implementations via Task and Data Contracts, enabling model switching without workflow changes. Additionally, PLAIground introduces Pixie, an SLO-driven runtime model selection algorithm, which dynamically selects the most suitable model for each task during execution. Our evaluation on two realistic Compound AI workflows demonstrates that Pixie achieves up to 91.3% accuracy while maintaining SLO compliance where fixed-model strategies either violate cost and latency budgets up to 21x or miss accuracy targets by 4%."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14356","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-15T01:52:57Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:52:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-05T02:34:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14606","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14606","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14606","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongyan","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Yongyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Interaction Dynamics for Dexterous Manipulation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T16:28:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:58:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-03T04:09:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-07T00:20:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-02T23:55:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-04T01:21:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Dexterous manipulation is fundamentally a problem of interaction dynamics: the hand must track precise finger trajectories, regulate the contact force exchanged with grasped objects, respect actuation and safety limits, and remain predictable when contact persists -- objectives in tension for any fixed-gain controller. A sustained contact torque $τ_{\\text{ext}}$ through a joint stiffness $K_d$ produces the structural bias $e_\\infty=τ_{\\text{ext}}/K_d$, so stiffening for accuracy sacrifices contact safety while softening yields by design. We make these interaction dynamics explicit and actuator-agnostic through a constant-$A_d$ double-integrator backbone, instantiating the offset-free architecture established for physical human-robot interaction (pHRI) and preserving its modeling assumptions on the reduced residual dynamics. An algebraic feedforward reduces the tendon transmission -- hydraulic, cable, pneumatic, twisted-string, or series-elastic -- to a constant-coefficient double integrator, so the QP cost inverse is precomputed offline and a 10-step receding-horizon QP runs at 500\\,Hz under contact-force (ISO/TS 15066), actuation, and jerk constraints. An encoder-only augmented-Kalman disturbance state drives steady-state error to zero under constant contact loads in the nominal detectable case. In simulation, a hydraulically actuated finger -- the worked example, adding pressure and cavitation constraints -- attains 0.6\\,mrad RMS, 0.1\\,mrad steady-state, and 7.3\\,mrad peak deflection under 1.5\\,Nm contact: 153$\\times$, 1500$\\times$, and 21$\\times$ better than classical impedance. The realized first-move stiffness (18$\\to$323\\,Nm/rad with update rate) is independently verified, and the architecture scales to a 16-DOF LEAP Hand MuJoCo model, recovering from 2.5\\,N grasp disturbances within 0.7\\,s."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14606","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:58:13Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:58:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T04:46:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14435","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14435","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14435","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dekui","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Dekui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Shadowing in Dynamical Systems: Zero-dimensional Extensions and Inverse Limits"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Dynamical Systems (math.DS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"General Topology (math.GN)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T13:16:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:48:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-18T08:58:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-19T00:35:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-02T02:53:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:52:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Shifts of finite type (SFTs) play a central role in the theory of shadowing. Good and Meddaugh showed that SFTs serve as basic building blocks in the structural theory of shadowing systems; in particular, every compact metric dynamical system with shadowing is a factor of the inverse limit of an inverse sequence consisting of SFTs. We first show that, for this factor representation alone, neither shadowing nor metrizability is needed: every compact Hausdorff dynamical system is a factor of the inverse limit of an inverse system consisting of SFTs.\n Thus, being a factor of an SFT inverse limit is not the structural feature genuinely forced by shadowing. What shadowing provides is stronger stability: in the metric case, every compact shadowing system is a factor of the inverse limit of an inverse sequence of SFTs with surjective bonding maps. Hence the associated zero-dimensional extension still has shadowing. We also prove that every compact Hausdorff shadowing system is conjugate to an inverse limit of metrizable shadowing systems with factor bonding maps."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14435","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:54:37Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:54:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T04:46:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14082","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14082","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14082","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dake","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Dake","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhizhang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhizhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiqi","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Shiqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Inverse Hessian Curvature Flow in Minkowski Space I: Cocompact Hypersurfaces"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Differential Geometry (math.DG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T03:58:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:24:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-03T14:57:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-04T02:04:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this paper, we investigate the cocompact inverse \\(σ_k\\) curvature flow in Minkowski space. We prove the longtime existence and convergence of this flow. As a consequence, quermassintegral inequalities are established."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14082","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:47:08Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:47:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T04:46:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14061","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14061","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14061","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongjian","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Dongjian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Silin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Silin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yufei","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yufei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuling","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Yuling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanfu","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Yanfu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaodong","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Xiaodong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLM Agents Can See Code Repositories"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T03:14:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:22:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T09:45:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-16T01:35:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-19T10:58:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-23T00:44:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-03T06:25:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-08-04T01:32:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Coding agents powered by large language models have demonstrated strong performance on software engineering tasks. Yet most agents consume repositories almost entirely as text, which differs from how human developers use visual structure such as folder hierarchies and dependency relationships to orient themselves in large codebases. With multimodal large language models (MLLMs), it is an open question whether agents can effectively benefit from visual representations of repositories. This paper presents the first systematic empirical study of visual repository representations for LLM-based agents on repository-level issue resolution. We evaluate four recent multimodal models. Our results show that a strictly vision-only setup degrades accuracy and increases token cost, because agents lack sufficient symbolic detail and compensate with repeated visual queries. In contrast, integrating visual graphs of repository structure as a supplementary modality alongside standard text interfaces helps agents understand structure more efficiently: input token consumption decreases by up to 26% while issue-resolution accuracy is maintained or improved. Visualization is most useful during fault localization and when the agent autonomously controls exploration depth. These findings point to a practical hybrid text-and-vision design for next-generation coding agents."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ASE 2026. Our code and data are available at https://github.com/cslsolow/SeeRepo"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14061","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:46:42Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:46:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T04:46:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13789","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13789","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13789","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Hotnisky","name":"Hotnisky, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rachel B.","familyName":"Fernandes","name":"Fernandes, Rachel B.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin K.","familyName":"Hardegree-Ullman","name":"Hardegree-Ullman, Kevin K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Steven","familyName":"Giacalone","name":"Giacalone, Steven","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kiersten M.","familyName":"Boley","name":"Boley, Kiersten M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kristo","familyName":"Ment","name":"Ment, Kristo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michelle","familyName":"Kunimoto","name":"Kunimoto, Michelle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Galen J.","familyName":"Bergsten","name":"Bergsten, Galen J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sakhee","familyName":"Bhure","name":"Bhure, Sakhee","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jessie L.","familyName":"Christiansen","name":"Christiansen, Jessie L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brandon","familyName":"Radzom","name":"Radzom, Brandon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suvrath","familyName":"Mahadevan","name":"Mahadevan, Suvrath","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"COUNTESS I: A Uniformly Vetted Catalog of Known and New Transiting Exoplanets in the TESS Northern Continuous Viewing Zone"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Earth and Planetary Astrophysics (astro-ph.EP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Instrumentation and Methods for Astrophysics (astro-ph.IM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Solar and Stellar Astrophysics (astro-ph.SR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T18:01:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:05:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-31T18:04:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:04:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The Transiting Exoplanet Survey Satellite (TESS) has transformed the study of nearby exoplanetary systems; however, its nominal observing strategy limits sensitivity to planets with orbital periods shorter than $\\sim$10 days for most parts of the sky. The two TESS Continuous Viewing Zones (CVZs) provide extended temporal baselines that help overcome this limitation, enabling the detection of longer-period ($\u0026gt;$10 days) transiting planets around nearby stars. Here, we present COUNTESS, a transit-search pipeline optimized for long-baseline TESS observations that combines multi-sector light curves with heterogeneous cadences, and implements fast-folding BLS period detection, vetting, and statistical validation. As a first application of the pipeline, we conducted a search on the primary and first extended mission photometry in the TESS northern CVZ. For this analysis, we used Gaia DR3 and 2MASS photometry to homogeneously derive a stellar catalog of FGKM stars for the TESS northern CVZ, resulting in a sample of 391,059 stars. We used COUNTESS to search for transiting planets around 26,114 of these stars with TESS-SPOC light curves and assessed its performance, recovering 115 out of 159 known TESS Objects of Interest (TOIs; $0.85\\ \\text{days} \u0026lt; P \u0026lt;124.72\\ \\text{days}$; $1.03\\ R_\\oplus \u0026lt; R_p \u0026lt; 16.35\\ R_\\oplus$). Additionally, we identified 10 new exoplanet candidates ($1.20\\ \\text{days} \u0026lt; P \u0026lt;34.62\\ \\text{days}$; $1.73\\ R_\\oplus \u0026lt; R_p \u0026lt; 4.19\\ R_\\oplus$) that passed vetting tests, including two new statistically validated sub-Neptunes, TIC 219893931b and TIC 237254473b. COUNTESS enables extended-baseline TESS analyses and identification of longer-period planets, establishing a foundation for future exoplanet demographic studies, including comparisons with Kepler and K2."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"26 pages, 10 figures, 6 tables; accepted to ApJS Journals"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13789","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:40:54Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:40:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T04:46:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13718","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13718","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13718","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aritra Sanyal Bikramarka S. Choudhury Farook","familyName":"Rahaman","name":"Rahaman, Aritra Sanyal Bikramarka S. Choudhury Farook","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Decoding the Gravitational Geometry and Stellar Photometric Profiles of Galaxy NGC 7331"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T04:26:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T02:15:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1093/mnras/stag668","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Galaxy rotation curves provide a powerful probe of mass distributions in spiral galaxies. We present a general relativistic analysis of NGC 7331 using its observed rotation curve and WISE W1 (3.4 $μ$m) photometry to constrain the stellar mass profile. Adopting a static, spherically symmetric spacetime with anisotropic matter (vanishing radial pressure), we fit a modified exponential azimuthal velocity law to kinematic data, reconstructing metric functions and deriving enclosed mass, energy density, and tangential pressure profiles. The stellar mass underpredicts the total gravitating mass at intermediate-to-large radii, indicating dominant dark matter. The physical viability of the resulting relativistic model is examined through energy conditions, causality constraints, and the stability of circular orbits. A comparison with the standard Navarro--Frenk--White dark matter profile has been done. Overall, this study demonstrates that combining rotation-curve data with photometric stellar mass estimates within a general relativistic framework provides a consistent and physically viable description of the mass distribution in spiral galaxies such as NGC~7331."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13718","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-15T01:39:23Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:39:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T04:46:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14142","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14142","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14142","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinhan","familyName":"He","name":"He, Yinhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liam","familyName":"Collins","name":"Collins, Liam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bhuvesh","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Bhuvesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jundong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jundong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Neil","familyName":"Shah","name":"Shah, Neil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Donald","familyName":"Loveland","name":"Loveland, Donald","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Implicit Reasoning for Large Language Model-based Generative Recommendation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T06:04:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:29:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T15:02:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-16T01:53:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-30T23:59:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-03T00:12:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly adopted as backbones for Generative Recommendation (GR), promising access to pretrained world knowledge. Yet reliably invoking this knowledge for GR remains poorly understood. A key obstacle is that LLM-based GR typically represents items with Semantic IDs (SIDs), disrupting LLMs' natural-language reasoning interface because these tokens are unseen by the LLM during pretraining. Existing approaches address this with expensive multi-stage pipelines that ground SIDs and elicit explicit rationales, but offer limited insight into when and why each stage is necessary. In this work, we systematically decompose explicit reasoning training pipelines for LLM-based GR, revealing three key limitations: weakened world-knowledge verbalization, misalignment between SID and natural-language token embedding spaces, and sensitivity to rationale quality, all of which hurt explicit reasoning performance. To circumvent these issues, we propose PauseRec, a lightweight implicit reasoning paradigm tailored for GR. PauseRec is exceptionally practical, avoiding costly reasoning trace acquisition and reasoning alignment training, leading to a multitude of benefits: (1) it outperforms standard explicit CoT methods by up to 6.22%, (2) it reduces training cost by up to 65% GPU hours, and (3) it speeds up inference by up to 71.3%. These results position PauseRec as a lightweight alternative to explicit rationale generation, enabling more effective and efficient LLM-based GR."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14142","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:48:24Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:48:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-03T02:13:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13977","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13977","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13977","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yoshinori","familyName":"Nomura","name":"Nomura, Yoshinori","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Creative Integration: A Decidable Criterion of Creativity"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7; I.2.0; F.4.1","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T23:49:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-28T00:59:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-31T02:14:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-03T00:16:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"\"Integrative\" solutions are widely praised but rarely defined: we lack an operational way to tell a genuine integration -- one that makes the world cheaper to describe -- from a tidy re-description. Building on the lineage that treats creativity and intelligence as compression, we give such a criterion for creative integration (CI): the resolution of a real conflict between A and B is CI if and only if, under a fixed description language, the description length strictly shrinks (C = L_pre/L_post \u0026gt; 1), with the reduction located in the conflict itself. We make the judgment decidable through four binary, conjunctive gates, and we fix its extension through a taxonomy of pseudo-integration that names and rejects the look-alikes. We back the criterion with a curated, multi-domain corpus and -- crucially -- validate it not by human inter-rater agreement but by four falsifiable tests it could fail: an independent computational check, discrimination against hard negatives, out-of-sample prediction, and description-language robustness; all pass with margin. The contribution is not \"creativity is compression\" but its decidability, discrimination, and corpus: on this account, what makes a move genuinely creative -- rather than merely novel -- is that it compresses a conflict, with novelty and value as downstream symptoms; whether all creativity is so constituted we state as an explicit conjecture. We claim only the sign of C-1; we judge, not generate. The result is a citable primitive for a broader program."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 1 figure"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13977","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:44:55Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:44:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-03T02:13:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13913","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13913","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13913","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeff","familyName":"Viaclovsky","name":"Viaclovsky, Jeff","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaokang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiaokang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Collapsing constant scalar curvature metrics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Differential Geometry (math.DG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T21:08:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:12:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-29T20:14:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-31T00:08:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We prove that a sequence of constant scalar curvature (CSC) metrics which is collapsing with bounded curvature to a manifold $(X,g_{\\infty})$ can be perturbed to a sequence of $\\mathcal{N}$-invariant collapsing CSC metrics, under a natural assumption involving the eigenvalues of the drift Laplacian on $(X,g_{\\infty})$. This answers a special case of a question of Cheeger-Fukaya-Gromov. We also give some natural conditions on the limiting metric-measure space under which the eigenvalue assumption is automatically satisfied."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"32 pages; v2 minor revision and added references"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13913","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:43:35Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:43:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-31T02:40:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14495","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14495","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14495","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boris Betancourt","familyName":"Kamenetskaia","name":"Kamenetskaia, Boris Betancourt","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mathias","familyName":"Garny","name":"Garny, Mathias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alejandro","familyName":"Ibarra","name":"Ibarra, Alejandro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessia","familyName":"Musumeci","name":"Musumeci, Alessia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Merlin","familyName":"Reichard","name":"Reichard, Merlin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Cosmological signals of dark matter semi-annihilation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Phenomenology (hep-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T14:29:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:51:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-30T16:06:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-31T01:03:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The growth of primordial density fluctuations in the early Universe leads to an inhomogeneous dark matter distribution at high redshift, where semi-annihilation processes of the form $χχ\\rightarrow χ^c ϕ$, with $ϕ$ being dark radiation, can occur with a sizable rate. Using a state-of-the-art model for the cosmological boost factor, we compute the resulting redshift-dependent flux of boosted dark matter particles generated by semi-annihilation, and we study the implications of the boosted component for structure formation and direct detection experiments. We find a model independent upper limit on the semi-annihilation cross-section from structure formation, which reads $\\langleσ_{2\\to1} v\\rangle\\leq4.2\\times10^{-19}~\\left(m_χ/1~\\rm GeV\\right)~\\mathrm{cm}^3/{\\rm s}$. Further, we find that the cosmological contribution to the boosted dark matter flux can be comparable to the galactic one, providing an $O(1)$ enhancement to the sensitivity of dark matter searches, thus slightly enhancing the discovery potential in direct detection experiments of semi-annihilation scenarios where the dark matter interacts with the nucleus."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14495","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:55:54Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:55:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-31T02:40:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14577","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14577","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14577","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gabriel","familyName":"Müller","name":"Müller, Gabriel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Timothé","familyName":"Estrampes","name":"Estrampes, Timothé","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Claudia Puertas","familyName":"González","name":"González, Claudia Puertas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jannik","familyName":"Ströhle","name":"Ströhle, Jannik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David B.","familyName":"Reinhardt","name":"Reinhardt, David B.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dana Codruta","familyName":"Marinica","name":"Marinica, Dana Codruta","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ethan R.","familyName":"Elliott","name":"Elliott, Ethan R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jason R.","familyName":"Williams","name":"Williams, Jason R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nathan","familyName":"Lundblad","name":"Lundblad, Nathan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eric","familyName":"Charron","name":"Charron, Eric","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ernst M.","familyName":"Rasel","name":"Rasel, Ernst M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthias","familyName":"Meister","name":"Meister, Matthias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wolfgang P.","familyName":"Schleich","name":"Schleich, Wolfgang P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Naceur","familyName":"Gaaloul","name":"Gaaloul, Naceur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicholas P.","familyName":"Bigelow","name":"Bigelow, Nicholas P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Trap-Quenched Matter-Wave Optics in Space"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Atomic Physics (physics.atom-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantum Gases (cond-mat.quant-gas)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T15:57:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:56:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-29T12:42:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-30T00:46:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Dual-species atomic sources in space promise to be the testbed for a multitude of searches in quantum gas physics such as a precise test of the Universality of Free Fall (UFF), few-body physics, cold molecules and quantum bubbles. These experiments demand exquisite control over the expansion energies of both condensed ensembles as well as over their differential center-of-mass dynamics. We propose a trap-quenched collimation technique featuring in-trap excitations of collective modes compatible with state-of-the-art atom-chip setups. Using NASA's Cold Atom Laboratory aboard the International Space Station, we demonstrate it on a single-species $^{87}$Rb condensate. By controlling the center-of-mass release dynamics, we observe free expansion times up to 700 ms and measure a two-dimensional expansion energy of $k_B \\cdot 78\\pm 9 \\;\\mathrm{pK}$ in the imaging plane. A detailed model of the magnetically-induced dynamics indicates that this corresponds to a two-dimensional expansion energy of about $k_B \\cdot 15^{+12}_{-5}\\; \\mathrm{pK}$ along two of the condensate's eigenaxes. Finally, we theoretically study this trap-quenched collimation scheme for a $^{41}$K-$^{87}$Rb mixture, predicting a simultaneous collimation that meets the expansion energy requirements for a state-of-the-art UFF test at the $10^{-15}$ accuracy level."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14577","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:57:37Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:57:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-30T02:25:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13987","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13987","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13987","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhipeng","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Zhipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Proof and More Variations of Bellman's Lost-in-a-forest Problem"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"49K30, 49Q10, 52A40","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T00:06:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:16:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-25T23:59:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-29T00:11:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-29T15:28:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-30T00:56:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this paper, based on our previous general formulation and computational solution to Bellman's Lost-in-a-forest Problem, we provide the proof of general solution and obtained more variations and results related to this problem. This paper provides generalized formalized method connecting curve covering, lost-in-the-forest problem, and traveling salesman problem with neighborhoods. We prove the equivalence and convergence. We also provide more results of searching for two lines, connection to Wetzel's unit arc covering problem, variations with closed path, variations in three dimensions, etc. The results include general calculation equations, partial analytical results, and numerical results."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"V3 adds formalized proofs of Theorems 1-7 in Lean 4 code"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13987","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:45:08Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:45:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-30T02:25:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13855","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13855","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13855","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ekaterina","familyName":"Piotrovskaya","name":"Piotrovskaya, Ekaterina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Phase Semantics to Base-extension Semantics (and back)"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Logic in Computer Science (cs.LO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Logic (math.LO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"F.4.1","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T19:32:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:09:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-29T11:05:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-30T00:41:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Linear logic admits a wide range of semantic presentations reflecting its resource-sensitive notion of consequence. One well-known example is phase semantics: an algebraic semantics in which formulas are interpreted in phase models, consisting of a phase space formed by a commutative monoid and a fixed subset, with respect to which an orthogonality relation is defined, and a valuation. A rather different and much more recent approach is given by base-extension semantics, which defines validity by inductively extending a provability relation on a base -- a set of inference rules over atomic propositions. We establish an equivalence between the two semantics by first defining bidirectional maps between bases and phase models, and then constructing an isomorphism between a phase model (resp. base) and its image under the composition of these maps. As a further contribution, we define the base-extension semantics clauses for the exponentials of linear logic."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"26 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13855","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:42:19Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:42:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-30T02:25:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14376","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14376","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14376","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicolas","familyName":"Grelier","name":"Grelier, Nicolas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stéphane","familyName":"Kaufmann","name":"Kaufmann, Stéphane","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Johannes","familyName":"Pfau","name":"Pfau, Johannes","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Games Mapper: Topological Data Analysis of Steam Genres"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Social and Information Networks (cs.SI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T12:07:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:45:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-28T10:06:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-29T00:41:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The video game industry comprises a vast, continuously evolving landscape of themes and genres. For studios and publishers that navigate this competitive market, understanding the structural dynamics and temporal evolution of specific game categories is crucial for identifying viable entry points. In this paper, we introduce Games Mapper, a novel analytical tool based on the Mapper algorithm from topological data analysis. Unlike traditional clustering techniques, Games Mapper captures the continuous topological relationships between datasets over time (or other guiding variables). We extend the standard algorithm with an automated cluster labelling method, ensuring highly interpretable and interactive visualisations of genre evolution. To demonstrate the efficacy of our approach, we present a comprehensive case study on Simulation games released on Steam between 2015 and 2025. Games Mapper autonomously segments the genre into coherent, persistent subgenres, and captures dynamic market shifts. Ultimately, we provide a scalable, generalisable tool for researchers and industrials to unravel complex market structures and track the evolution of the Steam ecosystem."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14376","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:53:23Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:53:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-29T02:32:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14244","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14244","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14244","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"F.","familyName":"Acero","name":"Acero, F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A.","familyName":"Acharyya","name":"Acharyya, A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A.","familyName":"Adelfio","name":"Ad